    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:dns-configuration:plan", title: "Map names to the network", minutes: 15, summary: "A DNS plan explains which server owns a name and how a client finds that server. Begin with the working directory build, then extend it deliberately.", objectives: ["Explain which DNS servers a domain member must use in this lab and why.", "Inventory the existing sbd.test and _msdcs zones without creating duplicates.", "Document the forwarding and recursion decision for an air-gapped network."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete the DC01 + DC02 identity build with both domain controllers healthy.\n\nUse an isolated Windows Server 2025 practice environment and an account delegated to manage DNS.\n\nContinue the existing sbd.test example: SBD-DC01 is 10.0.10.10 and SBD-DC02 is 10.0.10.11 on 10.0.10.0/24.\n\nThe network's routing and VLAN segmentation are already in place, with a documented service-path register."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The addresses and names below continue the training example. Match every change to your own address plan. This network has no internet DNS or time dependency: names resolve on the domain controllers, and time comes from the PDC emulator, SBD-DC01."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Record the domain, each subnet, both DNS addresses, and the owner of each service name. Keep host records and service aliases distinguishable."), LessonSection("Step 2", "On each domain controller, open DNS Manager and review the existing sbd.test and _msdcs zones. Promotion may already have created them; do not create duplicate zones."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Check the DNS role and static network settings. Domain members must use internal DNS servers that resolve the AD namespace; a public resolver is not an alternate for this environment."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Decide whether any separate internal namespace needs a conditional forwarder. With no recursive dependencies, document a no-recursion design; do not add internet forwarders or delete every root hint as a shortcut."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-WindowsFeature DNS\nGet-DnsClientServerAddress -AddressFamily IPv4\nGet-DnsServerZone -ComputerName SBD-DC01"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Both servers and the client point to the intended internal DNS service. Existing AD zones and the forwarding/recursion decision are recorded."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Save the address/name worksheet and the initial zone inventory before changing anything."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Install and configure DNS Server\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/dns/quickstart-install-configure-dns-server")], question: "Which DNS servers should a domain member use in this lab?", answers: ["The internal DNS servers that resolve sbd.test", "A public resolver whenever the first DC is unavailable", "The offline Root CA"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "AD clients need the internal namespace and its service records. Internet reachability would not replace that requirement.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Promotion already created the sbd.test and _msdcs zones. What should you do?", ["Delete them and start from a clean zone list", "Review and record them without creating duplicate zones", "Create a second sbd.test zone for DC02"], 1, "Promotion builds the zones Active Directory needs. Duplicating or recreating them breaks the record you are trying to verify."), QuizQuestion("Why does this design document a no-recursion decision instead of adding internet forwarders?", ["Forwarders are always a security violation", "Root hints make zones replicate faster", "The network has no recursive dependencies, so the decision is recorded deliberately"], 2, "In an air-gapped lab there is nothing to forward to. Writing the decision down keeps the design intentional rather than accidental.")], recap: ["Domain members point only at the internal DNS servers that resolve sbd.test.", "The existing AD zones are inventoried, not recreated.", "The forwarding and recursion decision is written down before anything changes."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 turns this plan into AD-integrated forward and reverse zones.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dns-configuration#plan", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dns-configuration:zones", title: "Configure forward and reverse zones", minutes: 20, summary: "Forward lookup answers where a name lives. Reverse lookup answers which name is assigned to an address. AD integration lets the directory replicate zone data between the selected DNS servers.", objectives: ["Confirm sbd.test is AD-integrated with the intended replication scope.", "Create a secure, AD-integrated reverse zone for 10.0.10.0/24.", "Explain how zone data moves between the two DNS hosts."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete the DC01 + DC02 identity build with both domain controllers healthy.\n\nUse an isolated Windows Server 2025 practice environment and an account delegated to manage DNS.\n\nContinue the existing sbd.test example: SBD-DC01 is 10.0.10.10 and SBD-DC02 is 10.0.10.11 on 10.0.10.0/24.\n\nThe network's routing and VLAN segmentation are already in place, with a documented service-path register."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The addresses and names below continue the training example. Match every change to your own address plan. This network has no internet DNS or time dependency: names resolve on the domain controllers, and time comes from the PDC emulator, SBD-DC01."), LessonSection("Step 1", "In DNS Manager, inspect sbd.test Properties. Confirm that it is AD-integrated and choose the domain or forest replication scope that matches the DNS servers intended to host it."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Use secure dynamic updates for the AD-integrated zone. Review delegation and permissions before broadening who can change records."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Under Reverse Lookup Zones, create an IPv4 primary AD-integrated zone for network ID 10.0.10 if an equivalent zone does not already exist. Select the approved replication scope and secure updates."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Review zone-transfer settings. AD replication between these DCs does not require enabling transfers to arbitrary DNS servers; restrict any separately required secondary-server transfers explicitly."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-DnsServerZone -ComputerName SBD-DC01 |\n  Select-Object ZoneName, ZoneType, IsDsIntegrated, DynamicUpdate, ReplicationScope\nGet-DnsServerZone -ComputerName SBD-DC02"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The intended forward and reverse zones appear on both DNS hosts after replication. The reverse zone for this /24 is 10.0.10.in-addr.arpa."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Capture the zone type, update policy, replication scope, and transfer policy for each zone."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Manage DNS zones\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/dns/manage-dns-zones")], question: "How does an AD-integrated zone reach the other DNS-enabled domain controller in its replication scope?", answers: ["An unrestricted zone transfer to every client", "Active Directory replication", "A manual copy of the DNS cache"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "AD-integrated zone data follows directory replication. A conventional secondary zone uses a different transfer mechanism.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What is the reverse lookup zone for the 10.0.10.0/24 network?", ["10.0.10.in-addr.arpa", "sbd.test.reverse", "arpa.10.0.10"], 0, "IPv4 reverse zones place the reversed network octets under in-addr.arpa, so this /24 becomes 10.0.10.in-addr.arpa."), QuizQuestion("Why choose secure dynamic updates for the AD-integrated zone?", ["It makes lookups return faster", "It removes the need for a reverse zone", "Only authenticated computers can register or change their records"], 2, "Secure updates tie record changes to directory authentication, which keeps the zone’s contents accountable.")], recap: ["Forward and reverse zones are AD-integrated with a deliberate replication scope.", "Dynamic updates are secure-only.", "Zone transfers stay restricted; directory replication moves the data."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 builds the record set your services will actually use.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dns-configuration#zones", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dns-configuration:records", title: "Create and maintain service records", minutes: 20, summary: "Build a small, intentional record set. A host record identifies a machine; an alias provides a stable service name without making that alias a separate server.", objectives: ["Distinguish host records (A and PTR) from service aliases (CNAME).", "Create a host record with its PTR and a service alias that match the plan.", "Maintain records with recorded ownership, lifetimes, and reasons."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete the DC01 + DC02 identity build with both domain controllers healthy.\n\nUse an isolated Windows Server 2025 practice environment and an account delegated to manage DNS.\n\nContinue the existing sbd.test example: SBD-DC01 is 10.0.10.10 and SBD-DC02 is 10.0.10.11 on 10.0.10.0/24.\n\nThe network's routing and VLAN segmentation are already in place, with a documented service-path register."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The addresses and names below continue the training example. Match every change to your own address plan. This network has no internet DNS or time dependency: names resolve on the domain controllers, and time comes from the PDC emulator, SBD-DC01."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Review the DC A and PTR records first. Correct mismatches through the owning host and DNS administration process rather than blindly adding another record with the same name."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Choose one approved service host from your address plan. In sbd.test, use New Host (A or AAAA), enter its actual name and address, and select the associated PTR option when the reverse zone is ready."), LessonSection("Step 3", "For a service alias, use New Alias (CNAME) and select the intended fully qualified host. For the later PKI course, reserve pki.sbd.test for the approved internal publication host; do not point it at the offline root."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Set the record lifetime to the operational plan, record static ownership, and remove only records confirmed obsolete. An alias cannot coexist with other record types at the same name."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-DnsServerResourceRecord -ComputerName SBD-DC01 -ZoneName sbd.test\nResolve-DnsName SBD-DC01.sbd.test -Type A -Server 10.0.10.10\nResolve-DnsName 10.0.10.10 -Type PTR -Server 10.0.10.10"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Forward and reverse responses agree with the address plan. Each alias reaches the intended host; creating a DNS name alone does not configure its service."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep a record table with owner, type, target, time to live, and reason for the change."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Manage DNS resource records\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/dns/manage-resource-records")], question: "What does creating pki.sbd.test as an alias accomplish?", answers: ["It publishes every certificate automatically", "It connects the offline root to the network", "It points a service name at the selected host"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "DNS supplies name resolution. The publication service, files, access, and certificate URLs still need configuration.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("A CNAME already exists at a name. What else can live at that same name?", ["Any A record", "No other record types", "A second CNAME"], 1, "An alias cannot coexist with other record types at the same name — one reason service aliases are planned deliberately."), QuizQuestion("Forward and reverse answers disagree for a domain controller. What is the right fix?", ["Correct it through the owning host and the DNS administration process", "Add another record with the same name", "Delete the reverse zone"], 0, "Mismatches get fixed at the owner, not papered over with duplicates that make troubleshooting harder.")], recap: ["Host records identify machines; aliases provide stable service names.", "pki.sbd.test is reserved for the internal publication host, never the offline root.", "Every record carries an owner, a lifetime, and a reason."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 proves that both resolvers and directory discovery actually work.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dns-configuration#records", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dns-configuration:verify", title: "Prove resolution and directory discovery", minutes: 20, summary: "Test each server explicitly before testing client fallback. A successful cached answer does not prove that both DNS servers hold the right records.", objectives: ["Query each DNS server explicitly instead of trusting cached answers.", "Verify the LDAP service records that let clients discover domain controllers.", "Run a controlled failover test and record the result."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete the DC01 + DC02 identity build with both domain controllers healthy.\n\nUse an isolated Windows Server 2025 practice environment and an account delegated to manage DNS.\n\nContinue the existing sbd.test example: SBD-DC01 is 10.0.10.10 and SBD-DC02 is 10.0.10.11 on 10.0.10.0/24.\n\nThe network's routing and VLAN segmentation are already in place, with a documented service-path register."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The addresses and names below continue the training example. Match every change to your own address plan. This network has no internet DNS or time dependency: names resolve on the domain controllers, and time comes from the PDC emulator, SBD-DC01."), LessonSection("Step 1", "From a domain client, query each DNS server for the same host and reverse record. Record the server queried and the returned data."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Query the AD LDAP service records below against both servers. These records let a client discover domain controllers; an ordinary host lookup does not prove that discovery works."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Run the DNS diagnostics and directory replication checks on a domain controller. Investigate failures in context, including the intentionally isolated network design."), LessonSection("Step 4", "During an approved lab test, make one DNS server unavailable to the test client and verify resolution through the other. Restore connectivity and repeat for the opposite server. Do not interrupt shared production services for this exercise."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Resolve-DnsName SBD-DC01.sbd.test -Server 10.0.10.10\nResolve-DnsName SBD-DC01.sbd.test -Server 10.0.10.11\nResolve-DnsName _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.sbd.test -Type SRV -Server 10.0.10.10\nResolve-DnsName _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.sbd.test -Type SRV -Server 10.0.10.11\ndcdiag /test:dns /v\nrepadmin /replsummary"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Both DNS servers return the expected host and AD service records, replication is healthy, and the client still resolves internal names when either resolver is unavailable."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep paired lookup results, diagnostic output, and the failover test record with start, restore, and final verification times."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Resolve-DnsName\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/dnsclient/resolve-dnsname\n\nMicrosoft · Dcdiag\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/dcdiag")], question: "Why query each DNS server directly?", answers: ["To distinguish a healthy pair from a single working resolver or cached answer", "To permanently bypass the client's DNS configuration", "To generate a certificate request"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Explicit server queries expose differences between resolvers before client caching and fallback obscure the result.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Which record type lets a client discover domain controllers?", ["PTR records in the reverse zone", "CNAME aliases such as pki.sbd.test", "SRV records such as _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.sbd.test"], 2, "Service (SRV) records advertise the directory service. An ordinary host lookup does not prove discovery works."), QuizQuestion("During the failover exercise, what belongs in the evidence record?", ["Only a screenshot of one successful lookup", "Start, restore, and final verification times with the paired lookup results", "The DNS service account password"], 1, "A dated failover record shows redundancy was proven, not assumed — and secrets never belong in evidence.")], recap: ["Both resolvers answer the host and SRV queries correctly.", "dcdiag and repadmin confirm DNS and replication health.", "The failover test is documented with times, not just a passing screenshot."], nextStep: "Lesson 5 gives the domain a trustworthy clock, the other dependency every sign-in relies on.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dns-configuration#verify", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dns-configuration:time", title: "Make SBD-DC01 the domain's time source", minutes: 30, summary: "Kerberos tolerates minutes of skew, not more, and an air-gapped network has no internet time to lean on. Make the PDC emulator, SBD-DC01, the domain's authoritative time source and let the domain hierarchy carry its time to everything else.", objectives: ["Configure SBD-DC01, the PDC emulator, as the domain's reliable time source.", "Keep every other system on the domain hierarchy, with nothing overriding it.", "Document how DC01's clock is set and checked, and the drift threshold the model calls for."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete the DC01 + DC02 identity build with both domain controllers healthy.\n\nUse an isolated Windows Server 2025 practice environment and an account delegated to manage DNS.\n\nContinue the existing sbd.test example: SBD-DC01 is 10.0.10.10 and SBD-DC02 is 10.0.10.11 on 10.0.10.0/24.\n\nThe network's routing and VLAN segmentation are already in place, with a documented service-path register."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The addresses and names below continue the training example. Match every change to your own address plan. This network has no internet DNS or time dependency: names resolve on the domain controllers, and time comes from the PDC emulator, SBD-DC01."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Confirm which domain controller holds the PDC emulator role — SBD-DC01 in this sample. The PDC emulator of the forest root domain sits at the top of the Windows Time hierarchy, so it is the one system you configure; everything else follows it."), LessonSection("Step 2", "On SBD-DC01, mark Windows Time as a reliable time source with no external peer, so it serves time from its own clock. In an air-gapped network that clock is the reference by declaration: record how it is set and checked — for example, compared on a schedule against an approved reference and corrected by an administrator, with each correction recorded — and the drift threshold you will hold it to."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Turn off the hypervisor's time synchronization for both domain controller virtual machines, so the host clock cannot silently override the PDC emulator. SBD-DC02 syncs from the PDC emulator, every other domain member keeps the default domain-hierarchy sync from the domain controllers, and the domain-joined Linux servers in later courses point chrony or ntpd at both domain controllers."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Confirm the network's service-path register permits time — every member to both domain controllers on UDP 123 — and write the time design into the build record: course 1.3's hardening Group Policy objects (GPOs) must leave it intact, and if a Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) value disagrees with it, that course's deviation record is where the decision lives."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Verify the chain end to end: SBD-DC01 serving as the reliable source, SBD-DC02 and a member server sourcing from a domain controller, and offsets within your recorded threshold."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ADDomain | Select-Object PDCEmulator   # expected: SBD-DC01.sbd.test\n# On SBD-DC01 (the PDC emulator): serve time from its own clock as the reliable source\nw32tm /config /reliable:yes /update\nRestart-Service w32time\nw32tm /query /source   # on SBD-DC01: Local CMOS Clock\n# On SBD-DC02 and a member server:\nw32tm /query /source   # expected: a domain controller\nw32tm /query /status\nw32tm /stripchart /computer:SBD-DC01.sbd.test /samples:3 /dataonly"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "SBD-DC01 reports itself as the reliable source (Local CMOS Clock), SBD-DC02 and the member server report a domain controller, host time synchronization is off for both domain controllers, and measured offsets sit inside the documented threshold."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record the time design decisions (how DC01's clock is set and checked, and the drift threshold), the source and status output from both controllers and the member, the stripchart offsets, the hypervisor setting for both domain controller VMs, and the register rows that permit time: every member to the domain controllers."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Windows Time service\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/windows-time-service-top\n\nMicrosoft · Windows Time service tools and settings\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/windows-time-service-tools-and-settings")], question: "Which system is the domain's authoritative time source in this design?", answers: ["Every domain member individually", "SBD-DC01, the domain controller holding the PDC emulator role", "The offline root CA"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The PDC emulator of the forest root domain is the top of the Windows Time hierarchy. Marking it reliable makes it the source everything else follows.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What does the air gap change about time design?", ["Nothing — pool.ntp.org still works", "Kerberos stops needing accurate time", "There is no internet source, so DC01's clock is the reference by declaration and how it is checked is a documented decision"], 2, "With no external source, accuracy is whatever the lab declares and monitors — which is why the time-stamp controls (AU-8, SC-45) land on the PDC emulator."), QuizQuestion("Why does time matter to authentication at all?", ["Kerberos rejects tickets outside its allowed clock skew, so drifting clocks become sign-in failures", "DNS records expire hourly", "Passwords change with the clock"], 0, "Kerberos builds replay protection on timestamps. Skew beyond the tolerance breaks authentication before anything looks wrong."), QuizQuestion("Why turn off the hypervisor's time synchronization for the domain controller virtual machines?", ["It saves processor time", "Windows Time will not start otherwise", "The host clock would silently override the PDC emulator and fight the domain hierarchy"], 2, "A virtual machine that takes time from its host has two masters. Turning host sync off leaves the domain hierarchy, rooted at the PDC emulator, as the only authority."), QuizQuestion("Where does a domain workstation get its time in this design?", ["From SBD-DC01 only, set by hand on each machine", "From a domain controller over UDP 123, following the domain hierarchy", "From the core router"], 1, "Windows Time follows the domain hierarchy: members sync from a domain controller, the other domain controller from the PDC emulator, and the PDC emulator from its own clock. That is why the register permits UDP 123 from every VLAN to the domain controllers.")], recap: ["SBD-DC01, the PDC emulator, is the declared source; how its clock is checked is written down.", "The hypervisor no longer overrides domain controller time; the hierarchy carries it everywhere.", "Offsets are measured and inside the threshold, not assumed."], nextStep: "The final lesson practices finding and documenting a DNS or time fault.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dns-configuration#time", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dns-configuration:troubleshoot", title: "Find the fault and record the fix", minutes: 15, summary: "Troubleshoot one layer at a time: client settings, server reachability, authoritative data, replication, and finally the application that uses the name.", objectives: ["Isolate a DNS failure one layer at a time.", "Distinguish a missing name (NXDOMAIN) from timeouts and service failures.", "Record the cause, the fix, and the rollback — not just a passing result."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete the DC01 + DC02 identity build with both domain controllers healthy.\n\nUse an isolated Windows Server 2025 practice environment and an account delegated to manage DNS.\n\nContinue the existing sbd.test example: SBD-DC01 is 10.0.10.10 and SBD-DC02 is 10.0.10.11 on 10.0.10.0/24.\n\nThe network's routing and VLAN segmentation are already in place, with a documented service-path register."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The addresses and names below continue the training example. Match every change to your own address plan. This network has no internet DNS or time dependency: names resolve on the domain controllers, and time comes from the PDC emulator, SBD-DC01."), LessonSection("Step 1", "For a failing lookup, record the exact name, record type, client, resolver, timestamp, and error. Compare the fully qualified name with any short-name search behavior."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Query both servers directly. If they disagree, inspect zone scope and replication. If both are correct but the client is not, inspect client DNS settings and cached data."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Distinguish a missing name (NXDOMAIN) from a timeout or service failure. Fix the owning configuration first, then use Clear-DnsClientCache on the test client if stale cache remains relevant."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Repeat the original test and the redundancy checks. Record what changed and the rollback method. Review aging/scavenging separately against DHCP, static records, and backup practices before enabling automatic deletion."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-DnsClientServerAddress -AddressFamily IPv4\nGet-DnsClientCache\nResolve-DnsName SBD-DC02.sbd.test -Server 10.0.10.10\nResolve-DnsName SBD-DC02.sbd.test -Server 10.0.10.11"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The original failure is reproducibly resolved, both resolvers agree, and the change record explains the cause rather than only showing a passing screenshot."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Complete the DNS checklist and retain the before/after lookup results with the configuration owner."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Manage DNS resource records\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/dns/manage-resource-records\n\nMicrosoft · Resolve-DnsName\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/dnsclient/resolve-dnsname")], question: "Both DNS servers return the correct address, but one client keeps using the old address. What should you inspect next?", answers: ["The Root CA private key", "The client resolver configuration and cache", "Every zone's transfer policy at once"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Narrow the investigation to the failing client before changing otherwise consistent authoritative data.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("A lookup returns NXDOMAIN from both servers. What does that tell you?", ["The name does not exist in the authoritative data", "The DNS service is stopped", "The client cache is corrupted"], 0, "NXDOMAIN is an authoritative answer that the name is missing — a different problem from a timeout or an unreachable service."), QuizQuestion("When is Clear-DnsClientCache the right step?", ["As the first step for every DNS problem", "After the owning configuration is fixed, when stale cached data remains relevant", "Instead of checking the authoritative servers"], 1, "Clearing the cache hides symptoms if the authoritative data is still wrong. Fix the owner first, then clear what is stale.")], recap: ["Troubleshoot client settings, reachability, authoritative data, replication, then the application.", "NXDOMAIN, timeouts, and service failures point at different owners.", "The change record explains the cause and the rollback."], nextStep: "That completes Configure DNS and time. Course 1.3, Harden the baseline, builds the organizational units and hardened security policies that every later system joins.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dns-configuration#troubleshoot", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "harden-baseline", number: "1.3", title: "Harden the baseline", subtitle: "OU design, DISA GPOs & STIG/SCAP verification", category: "Network build", description: "Design the organizational unit structure, apply the DISA STIG Group Policy package to the verified network core, and prove the result with before-and-after SCAP scans, reconciled checklists, and a documented exception record.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "6 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 6,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "harden-guide", title: "Harden the baseline · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "OU design, DISA GPOs & STIG/SCAP verification", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/harden-baseline", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "harden-baseline-pdf", title: "Harden the baseline · Workbook PDF", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "16 pages · edition 1.2", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/harden-baseline/SecureByDesign-harden-baseline-Workbook.pdf", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "harden-baseline-word", title: "Harden the baseline · Editable workbook (Word)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Make the workbook your own.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/harden-baseline/SecureByDesign-harden-baseline-Workbook.docx", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "harden-baseline-checklist-page", title: "Harden the baseline · Printable checklist", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Print the configuration and evidence checklist.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/harden-baseline/checklist", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "harden-baseline-checklist", title: "Harden the baseline · Configuration checklist", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Download a Markdown checklist for your configuration and evidence.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/harden-baseline/configuration-checklist.md", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:harden-baseline:ou-design", title: "Design and build the OU structure", minutes: 20, summary: "Organizational units exist for delegation and Group Policy targeting, not to mirror an org chart. Build the structure the hardening policies will hang on before importing any of them.", objectives: ["Design an OU structure around delegation and Group Policy targeting.", "Create protected OUs and redirect the default Computers and Users containers.", "Explain why the built-in Domain Controllers OU stays untouched."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete the identity build and DNS courses with replication and name resolution verified on both controllers.\n\nUse the isolated Windows Server 2025 environment and an account approved for organizational unit and Group Policy administration.\n\nHave an approved connected system and removable-media procedure for bringing downloaded hardening content into the lab."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course designs the organizational unit (OU) structure, applies the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy package, and verifies the result with Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) scans. Paths, versions, and session locations below are examples — adapt them to your tooling. Expect STIG settings to change behavior: that is the point, and the reason everything is scanned and recorded."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Design the structure by administration: a top-level SBD OU containing Servers, Workstations, Users, Groups, and an Admin OU for privileged accounts. The built-in Domain Controllers OU stays where it is and keeps its name."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Create the planned OUs with accidental-deletion protection enabled. Record each OU's purpose and who will administer its contents; an OU with no delegation or policy purpose is clutter."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Redirect the default Computers and Users containers to managed OUs with redircmp and redirusr. Group Policy cannot be linked to the default containers, so anything landing there would get only domain-level policy, never the OU-linked hardening."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Keep privileged accounts and systems separate: the Admin OU holds the identities that administer domain controllers. The issuing CA from course 1.4 joins the Servers OU for now; course 1.5 gives Tier 0 servers their own OU. Do not nest deeper than the delegation plan requires."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ADOrganizationalUnit -Filter * | Select-Object Name, DistinguishedName\nGet-ADDomain | Select-Object ComputersContainer, UsersContainer\n(Get-ADOrganizationalUnit -Identity 'OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' -Properties ProtectedFromAccidentalDeletion).ProtectedFromAccidentalDeletion"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The planned OUs exist with deletion protection, the default container redirects point at managed OUs, and each OU has a recorded purpose and administrator."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the OU design sheet: each OU's distinguished name, purpose, delegated administrator, and the redirect commands with their output."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · New-ADOrganizationalUnit\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/activedirectory/new-adorganizationalunit\n\nMicrosoft · Redirect the Users and Computers containers\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/redirect-users-computers-containers")], question: "What should drive the shape of the OU structure?", answers: ["Group Policy targeting and delegated administration", "The company org chart", "Alphabetical order of departments"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "OUs are an administrative tool. Policy and delegation boundaries define them; reporting lines belong in HR systems.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why redirect the default Computers container?", ["It makes domain join faster", "Group Policy cannot be linked to the default containers, so new machines would miss the OU-linked hardening", "It is required before DNS works"], 1, "Redirection sends newly joined machines into an OU where policy applies from their first start."), QuizQuestion("Where do the two domain controllers live after this lesson?", ["In the new Servers OU", "In the built-in Domain Controllers OU, unchanged", "In the Users container"], 1, "Domain controllers stay in their built-in OU — it is where the DC hardening policies link in lesson 4.")], recap: ["OUs follow administration, not the org chart.", "The default containers are redirected so nothing joins unmanaged.", "The Domain Controllers OU remains the link point for DC policies."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 brings the DISA tooling across the air gap the same controlled way course 1.4 will later carry certificate requests.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/harden-baseline#ou-design", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:harden-baseline:staging", title: "Stage the tooling across the air gap", minutes: 15, summary: "The hardening content is downloaded artifacts: the DISA Group Policy object (GPO) package, the SCAP Compliance Checker (SCC), and STIG Viewer. They cross the boundary the way everything does here — reviewed, hashed, and carried on approved removable media.", objectives: ["Identify the DISA artifacts the hardening work needs.", "Move downloaded tooling across the air gap with hash verification.", "Record versions so scans stay comparable over time."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete the identity build and DNS courses with replication and name resolution verified on both controllers.\n\nUse the isolated Windows Server 2025 environment and an account approved for organizational unit and Group Policy administration.\n\nHave an approved connected system and removable-media procedure for bringing downloaded hardening content into the lab."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course designs the organizational unit (OU) structure, applies the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy package, and verifies the result with Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) scans. Paths, versions, and session locations below are examples — adapt them to your tooling. Expect STIG settings to change behavior: that is the point, and the reason everything is scanned and recorded."), LessonSection("Step 1", "On an approved connected system, download the current DISA GPO package and the SRG/STIG tools (SCC and STIG Viewer) from DISA's public site. Note each file's version and the quarterly release it belongs to."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Record a SHA-256 hash for every file on the connected side, then transfer the set with the approved removable-media procedure. No live link to the lab is created for convenience."), LessonSection("Step 3", "In the lab, recalculate and compare every hash before opening anything. A mismatch stops the work, exactly as it would in the PKI signing ceremony."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Install SCC and STIG Viewer on an administrative host and stage the GPO package where the import lesson can reach it. Record tool versions; scan results are only comparable when the benchmark and tool versions are known."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ChildItem C:\\Staging\\DISA | Select-Object Name, Length\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Staging\\DISA\\*"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Every transferred file's hash matches the value recorded on the connected side, and tool and package versions are written down."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the transfer record: file names, versions, both hash sets, media custody, and the date."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "DISA · STIG Group Policy Objects package\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/gpo\n\nDISA · SRG/STIG tools (SCC and STIG Viewer)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/srg-stig-tools")], question: "Why hash the files on both sides of the transfer?", answers: ["To prove the same files arrived unaltered", "To compress them for the media", "Because SCC refuses unhashed input"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "An independently recorded hash is how a human-controlled transfer proves integrity across the boundary.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Which release cadence do the DISA GPO package and STIGs follow?", ["Weekly", "Quarterly", "Only at new Windows releases"], 1, "DISA refreshes STIG content on a quarterly cycle, which this course's standing rescan cadence follows."), QuizQuestion("A hash mismatch appears on the lab side. What now?", ["Open the file and inspect it manually", "Stop, set the media aside, and repeat the approved transfer", "Regenerate hashes until they match"], 1, "A mismatch means the file is not the one that was reviewed. Unverified content never gets opened inside the boundary.")], recap: ["The GPO package, SCC, and STIG Viewer come from DISA's public site.", "Hashes are recorded on both sides of the boundary.", "Tool and package versions are part of the evidence."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 measures the network before anything is hardened.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/harden-baseline#staging", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:harden-baseline:baseline", title: "Capture the pre-hardening baseline", minutes: 25, summary: "Measure before you change. A scan of the freshly built controllers shows the honest starting point, teaches what the operating system already secures by default, and becomes the before picture every later scan is compared against.", objectives: ["Run a SCAP baseline scan against both domain controllers.", "Distinguish automated results from manual-review items.", "Preserve raw results as evidence with hashes and versions."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete the identity build and DNS courses with replication and name resolution verified on both controllers.\n\nUse the isolated Windows Server 2025 environment and an account approved for organizational unit and Group Policy administration.\n\nHave an approved connected system and removable-media procedure for bringing downloaded hardening content into the lab."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course designs the organizational unit (OU) structure, applies the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy package, and verifies the result with Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) scans. Paths, versions, and session locations below are examples — adapt them to your tooling. Expect STIG settings to change behavior: that is the point, and the reason everything is scanned and recorded."), LessonSection("Step 1", "On each domain controller, run SCC with the Windows Server 2025 STIG benchmark and the directory-related content staged for this course. Save each session's results with the host name and date."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Open the results and read, don't just count. Note which checks already pass: Windows Server 2025 ships with defaults such as required signing on outbound SMB connections and LDAP sealing on new deployments, so part of the STIG is green before you touch anything."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Separate the automated results from the manual checks. SCAP answers what a scanner can read; items marked for manual review go to STIG Viewer for a human answer in lesson 5."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Store the raw result files unmodified, with recorded hashes. The baseline is evidence; a cleaned-up copy is a summary, not the record."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Adapt paths to your SCC installation and session directory.\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\baseline -Recurse -Include *.xml | Select-Object Name, LastWriteTime\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\*"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Both controllers have a dated baseline result set, the already-compliant OS defaults are noted, and the raw files are hashed and stored."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the per-host result files, their hashes, the benchmark and tool versions, and a short note of what the OS defaults already covered."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · What's new in Windows Server 2025\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/get-started/whats-new-windows-server-2025\n\nDISA · SRG/STIG tools (SCC and STIG Viewer)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/srg-stig-tools\n\nNIST · National Checklist Program (SP 800-70)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/projects/national-checklist-program")], question: "Why scan before applying any GPO?", answers: ["The scanner requires an unhardened system", "A dated before record makes every later change measurable and attributable", "Scanning after GPOs is not possible"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The baseline is the comparison point. Without it, the rescan proves only a state, not what your hardening changed.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Several checks pass before any hardening. What is the likely reason on Windows Server 2025?", ["The scanner is misconfigured", "Earlier lessons secretly applied GPOs", "The OS ships secure defaults, such as required SMB signing, that satisfy those checks"], 2, "Windows Server 2025 raised several security defaults, so a fresh build already meets part of the benchmark."), QuizQuestion("What happens with checks the scanner marks for manual review?", ["They are ignored as not applicable", "They count as failures forever", "A person answers them in STIG Viewer with recorded rationale"], 2, "SCAP automates what a scanner can read; manual checks need a reviewer's observed evidence to be defensible.")], recap: ["The baseline is measured, dated, and stored unmodified.", "Secure-by-default items are recognized, not claimed as your work.", "Manual checks are queued for human review."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 applies the DISA policies that close the gap you just measured.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/harden-baseline#baseline", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:harden-baseline:apply", title: "Import and link the DISA GPOs", minutes: 35, summary: "Group Policy is how the STIG becomes configuration. Import the package into the central store, link by organizational unit, and keep the lab's deliberate deviations written down rather than silently skipped.", objectives: ["Stage the package's templates in the Group Policy central store.", "Import DISA's policies and link them by organizational unit.", "Handle lab-specific deviations with documentation instead of silent edits."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete the identity build and DNS courses with replication and name resolution verified on both controllers.\n\nUse the isolated Windows Server 2025 environment and an account approved for organizational unit and Group Policy administration.\n\nHave an approved connected system and removable-media procedure for bringing downloaded hardening content into the lab."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course designs the organizational unit (OU) structure, applies the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy package, and verifies the result with Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) scans. Paths, versions, and session locations below are examples — adapt them to your tooling. Expect STIG settings to change behavior: that is the point, and the reason everything is scanned and recorded."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Copy the package's ADMX and ADML templates into the domain's Group Policy central store (the PolicyDefinitions folder in SYSVOL) so every editor on any domain controller shows the same settings."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Create empty GPOs named for the package's policies and import the package's settings into them with the GPMC Import Settings wizard or Import-GPO. Keep DISA's names with a local prefix so the source release stays identifiable."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Link the domain-controller policies to the Domain Controllers OU and the member-server policies to the Servers OU for future systems. Review link order and inheritance; do not use Enforced as a shortcut around understanding precedence."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Walk the settings that need a decision in this network — anything referencing infrastructure the lab does not have. Record each deviation with its rationale instead of deleting the setting, then update policy and confirm application."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ChildItem \\\\sbd.test\\SYSVOL\\sbd.test\\Policies\\PolicyDefinitions | Measure-Object\nGet-GPO -All | Select-Object DisplayName, GpoStatus, ModificationTime\nGet-GPInheritance -Target 'OU=Domain Controllers,DC=sbd,DC=test'\ngpupdate /force\ngpresult /r /scope:computer"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The central store serves the package's templates, the imported GPOs are linked to the intended OUs, gpresult shows them applying, and every deviation has a written reason."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the import record: package release, GPO names and links, inheritance output, and the deviation list with rationale and owner."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Create and manage the Group Policy Central Store\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-client/group-policy/create-and-manage-central-store\n\nMicrosoft · Import-GPO\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/grouppolicy/import-gpo\n\nMicrosoft · Gpresult\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/gpresult\n\nDISA · STIG Group Policy Objects package\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/gpo")], question: "Why does the OU work from lesson 1 matter here?", answers: ["GPOs link to OUs, so the structure decides which systems receive which hardening", "OUs make the GPO files smaller", "DISA requires an OU named DISA"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Policy targeting is the OU structure's whole job: domain-controller policies to the Domain Controllers OU, server policies to the Servers OU.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Where do the ADMX templates go so every domain controller's editor agrees?", ["Each administrator's desktop", "The Group Policy central store in SYSVOL", "The offline root CA"], 1, "The central store replicates with SYSVOL, so every Group Policy editor in the domain reads the same definitions."), QuizQuestion("A STIG setting assumes infrastructure the lab doesn't have. What is the right move?", ["Delete the setting from the GPO quietly", "Mark the whole package not applicable", "Record a deviation with rationale and keep it visible for the assessment"], 2, "Documented deviations keep the hardening honest. A silent edit hides a decision an assessor will later need to see.")], recap: ["The central store keeps policy definitions consistent across controllers.", "Links follow the OU design; precedence is understood, not forced.", "Deviations are documented decisions, not deletions."], nextStep: "Lesson 5 proves what the policies changed.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/harden-baseline#apply", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:harden-baseline:rescan", title: "Rescan, reconcile, and record", minutes: 35, summary: "The second scan is the verdict. Compare it with the baseline, let the automated results fill the checklists, and give every remaining open item a human answer.", objectives: ["Re-scan with matched benchmark and tool versions and compare against the baseline.", "Populate checklists from scan results and answer manual checks with evidence.", "Classify every remaining open item explicitly."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete the identity build and DNS courses with replication and name resolution verified on both controllers.\n\nUse the isolated Windows Server 2025 environment and an account approved for organizational unit and Group Policy administration.\n\nHave an approved connected system and removable-media procedure for bringing downloaded hardening content into the lab."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course designs the organizational unit (OU) structure, applies the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy package, and verifies the result with Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) scans. Paths, versions, and session locations below are examples — adapt them to your tooling. Expect STIG settings to change behavior: that is the point, and the reason everything is scanned and recorded."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Rerun SCC on both controllers with the same benchmark and tool versions as the baseline. Changing versions between scans makes the comparison meaningless."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Compare the two result sets per host: which checks moved from open to compliant, which stayed open, and whether anything regressed. Attribute each change to the policy that caused it."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Create the course checklists in STIG Viewer and bring in the scan results so automated passes are populated. Work the manual-review items one by one, recording the observed evidence for each answer."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Classify what remains open: a finding to fix now, a documented deviation from lesson 4, or a candidate for a Plan of Action and Milestones (POA&M) — the artifact the Body of Evidence course teaches you to write."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Compare result sets; adapt paths to your evidence layout.\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\baseline, C:\\Evidence\\hardened -Recurse -Include *.xml | Select-Object Directory, Name\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\*\ngpresult /h C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\SBD-DC01-gpresult.html"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Both hosts show the expected movement from the baseline, every change is attributable, and every open item is a finding, a deviation, or a POA&M candidate — never unexplained."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the before/after comparison, the populated checklist files, manual-check answers with their observed evidence, and the classification of remaining opens."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "DISA · SRG/STIG tools (SCC and STIG Viewer)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/srg-stig-tools\n\nNIST · National Checklist Program (SP 800-70)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/projects/national-checklist-program")], question: "Why keep the same benchmark and SCC versions for the rescan?", answers: ["Newer versions cost more", "Results are only comparable when the measuring stick didn't change", "DISA forbids upgrading mid-quarter"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "A version change alters which checks exist and how they evaluate, so the before/after comparison would mix causes.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("A check is still open and the lab intends to leave it that way, with a reason. What is that called?", ["A false positive", "A passing result", "A documented deviation — and possibly, later, a POA&M entry"], 2, "An intentional, justified open item is a deviation; one that will be fixed on a plan becomes a POA&M entry in the authorization path."), QuizQuestion("What makes a checklist entry for a manual check defensible?", ["The reviewer's observed evidence and rationale are recorded with it", "The box is checked", "It was green in the automated scan"], 0, "A manual answer without recorded evidence is an assertion. The observation is what an assessor can verify.")], recap: ["Same versions, honest comparison.", "Automated results fill the checklist; humans answer the rest.", "Open items end the lesson classified, never mysterious."], nextStep: "The final lesson turns this into a standing practice with owners and triggers.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/harden-baseline#rescan", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:harden-baseline:maintain", title: "Set the rescan cadence and exception record", minutes: 20, summary: "Hardening is a state you maintain, not a step you finish. Close the course by deciding who rescans, when, and which event forces an early look.", objectives: ["Consolidate deviations into an owned, dated exception record.", "Define the quarterly and event-driven rescan cadence.", "Export and back up the applied policy state as evidence."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete the identity build and DNS courses with replication and name resolution verified on both controllers.\n\nUse the isolated Windows Server 2025 environment and an account approved for organizational unit and Group Policy administration.\n\nHave an approved connected system and removable-media procedure for bringing downloaded hardening content into the lab."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course designs the organizational unit (OU) structure, applies the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy package, and verifies the result with Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) scans. Paths, versions, and session locations below are examples — adapt them to your tooling. Expect STIG settings to change behavior: that is the point, and the reason everything is scanned and recorded."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Consolidate the deviation records into one exception list: setting, reason, risk note, owner, and review date. This list travels into the authorization path as part of the Body of Evidence."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Set the standing cadence: review each quarterly DISA release for applicable updates, and rescan after any role addition or significant configuration change. Course 1.4's closing delta scans of the PKI servers are the first scheduled instance."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Export the Group Policy reports and back up the GPO set so the applied policy state is recoverable and reviewable without a live console."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Write the handoff note a future administrator or assessor would need: where the evidence lives, which versions produced it, and what is intentionally open."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-GPOReport -All -ReportType Html -Path C:\\Evidence\\gpo-report.html\nBackup-GPO -All -Path C:\\Evidence\\gpo-backup\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\gpo-report.html"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The exception list has owners and review dates, the GPO set is exported and backed up, and the rescan triggers are written where the next course will meet them."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Finish the hardening checklist: exception list, GPO backup location, report hashes, the cadence, and the named owner of the next review."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "DISA · STIG Group Policy Objects package\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/gpo\n\nNIST · National Checklist Program (SP 800-70)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/projects/national-checklist-program")], question: "What triggers a rescan before the next quarterly cycle?", answers: ["Only a calendar reminder", "A role addition or significant configuration change", "Nothing — quarterly is the only trigger"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Change is the other trigger. Course 1.4 ends with exactly such a delta scan, and so do the build courses after it.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why back up the GPO set as part of the evidence?", ["It makes gpupdate run faster", "DISA requires backups inside SYSVOL", "The applied policy state becomes recoverable and reviewable, independent of a live console"], 2, "A backup plus reports lets anyone — including a future you — verify or restore the applied state without trusting memory."), QuizQuestion("Where does the exception list end up in the bigger arc?", ["It is deleted after the course", "In the Body of Evidence the authorization path organizes and assesses", "Inside the offline root CA"], 1, "Deviations and POA&M candidates are authorization-package material; course 2.2 shows how artifacts like this list join the Body of Evidence.")], recap: ["Exceptions have owners and review dates.", "Rescans follow the DISA quarter and every meaningful change.", "The applied GPO state is exported, backed up, and hashed."], nextStep: "That completes hardening the baseline. Course 1.4 builds the two-tier PKI on this hardened base and runs this cadence's first delta scan.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/harden-baseline#maintain", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "two-tier-pki", number: "1.4", title: "Configure the two-tier PKI", subtitle: "Offline root, issuing CA, pki.sbd.test publication & templates", category: "Network build", description: "Build a complete two-tier public key infrastructure: an offline standalone Root CA and an enterprise issuing CA, signed through a controlled ceremony, with trust data served from pki.sbd.test at the exact embedded URLs, template administration on its own seat, and enrollment and revocation proven end to end.", icon: "risk", published: true, studyTime: "10 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 10,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "two-tier-pki-guide", title: "Configure the two-tier PKI · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Offline root, issuing CA, pki.sbd.test publication & templates", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "two-tier-pki-pdf", title: "Configure the two-tier PKI · Workbook PDF", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "24 pages · edition 2.0", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki/SecureByDesign-two-tier-pki-Workbook.pdf", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "two-tier-pki-word", title: "Configure the two-tier PKI · Editable workbook (Word)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Make the workbook your own.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki/SecureByDesign-two-tier-pki-Workbook.docx", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "two-tier-pki-checklist-page", title: "Configure the two-tier PKI · Printable checklist", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Print the configuration and evidence checklist.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki/checklist", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "two-tier-pki-checklist", title: "Configure the two-tier PKI · Configuration checklist", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Download a Markdown checklist for your configuration and evidence.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki/configuration-checklist.md", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:design", title: "Design the two-tier trust hierarchy", minutes: 25, summary: "The root establishes trust. The subordinate, also called the issuing CA in this design, handles approved end-entity certificates. Keeping these roles separate protects the root from daily network exposure.", objectives: ["Explain why the root stays offline while the issuing CA handles enrollment.", "Record the CA identities, cryptography, lifetimes, and CRL periods before installation, starting from this lab's example values.", "Plan AIA and CDP publication locations that clients can reach inside the air gap."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.3 (identity, DNS and time, and the hardened baseline); verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server 2025 hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01 in a disconnected workgroup, SBD-SUBCA01 joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 as a hardened member server at 10.0.30.30 in the Servers OU (course 1.5 moves it, with the issuing CA, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, and template administration on its own seat. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Draw the hierarchy: offline standalone root → enterprise issuing CA → approved server/client certificates. There is no network connection to the root."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Record final host and CA names, key provider, algorithm, key sizes, hashes, validity periods, renewal owners, and recovery arrangements. Start from this lab's example values in the next step and change any that your own policy requires: they are examples, not universal requirements."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Example values for this lab: the root CA uses an RSA 4096 key with SHA-256 and a 20-year certificate, and publishes a base CRL every 26 weeks with an overlap period and no delta CRL, refreshed at each offline ceremony. The issuing CA uses RSA 4096 (3072 at minimum) with SHA-256 and a 10-year certificate, a weekly base CRL, and a daily delta CRL. End-entity certificates last one to two years. NIST SP 800-57 Part 1 rates RSA 2048 at 112 bits of security, which it accepts for new signatures only through 2030, so a CA that will still be signing after 2030 uses at least 3072 bits (128 bits of security); Windows Server 2025 requires at least 2,048-bit RSA for TLS server authentication."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Know where each value is set. You choose the key, the hash, and the root's 20-year validity in the AD CS Configuration wizard. Each CA's CAPolicy.inf sets its CRL periods (CRLPeriod, CRLPeriodUnits, CRLDeltaPeriod, CRLDeltaPeriodUnits), which certutil -setreg can change later. The issuing CA's 10-year lifetime is set on the root (CA\\ValidityPeriod and CA\\ValidityPeriodUnits) before the signing ceremony, because a subordinate CA's lifetime comes from the CA that signs it."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Plan a stable internal publication name such as pki.sbd.test. The Authority Information Access (AIA) location supplies issuer certificates; the CRL Distribution Point (CDP) supplies certificate revocation lists."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Choose the publication host, paths, access rules, and refresh schedule before issuing certificates. Clients inside this air-gapped network must reach those locations without relying on the public internet."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Run on the future issuing CA, not the offline root.\nGet-CimInstance Win32_ComputerSystem | Select-Object Name, Domain, PartOfDomain\nResolve-DnsName SBD-DC01.sbd.test\nResolve-DnsName SBD-DC02.sbd.test"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The written plan separates trust, issuance, publication, and administration. It identifies who renews CRLs before they expire, and every key size, hash, lifetime, and CRL period has a recorded value and the setting that applies it."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the approved hierarchy, the cryptography and lifetime table with the setting that applies each value, and a worksheet mapping every certificate/CRL URL to its actual publisher and file."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · PKI design considerations\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-cs/pki-design-considerations\n\nMicrosoft · CAPolicy.inf syntax\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/core-network-guide/cncg/server-certs/prepare-the-capolicy-inf-file\n\nNIST · SP 800-57 Part 1 Rev. 5, Recommendation for key management\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/57/pt1/r5/final")], question: "Which role issues routine server certificates in this design?", answers: ["The offline root for every request", "The enterprise issuing CA", "The DNS server"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The issuing CA handles end-entity enrollment while the offline root signs the approved subordinate CA certificate.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What do the AIA and CDP locations supply?", ["Private keys for backup", "DNS zone transfers", "Issuer certificates and certificate revocation lists"], 2, "Authority Information Access serves issuer certificates; CRL Distribution Points serve revocation lists. Clients must be able to reach both."), QuizQuestion("Why is pki.sbd.test planned before any CA is installed?", ["Issued certificates embed these URLs, so the publication name must be stable first", "The root cannot be installed without it", "DNS requires a CNAME before zones replicate"], 0, "Certificates keep the extensions they were issued with. A stable publication name avoids reissuing everything later."), QuizQuestion("In this lab, where is the issuing CA's 10-year certificate lifetime set?", ["In the issuing CA's own CAPolicy.inf", "On the offline root, with CA\\ValidityPeriod and CA\\ValidityPeriodUnits, before the signing ceremony", "In the certificate template for web servers"], 1, "A subordinate CA's lifetime is set by the CA that signs it. The root's issued-certificate validity settings decide how long the issuing CA's certificate lasts, so they are set and verified before the ceremony.")], recap: ["Trust, issuance, publication, and administration are separated in writing.", "Cryptography, names, lifetimes, and CRL periods are approved before installation, starting from this lab's example values.", "CRL renewal has a named owner and a date."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 performs the controlled offline root installation.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#design", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:root", title: "Configure the offline Root CA", minutes: 30, summary: "Treat root installation as a controlled event. Its private key is the highest-value secret in the hierarchy, and its configuration shapes the subordinate certificate it will sign.", objectives: ["Verify the disconnected workgroup host before installing the role.", "Install a standalone root CA with the approved CAPolicy.inf and settings.", "Configure publication URLs and the CRL schedule before signing anything."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.3 (identity, DNS and time, and the hardened baseline); verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server 2025 hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01 in a disconnected workgroup, SBD-SUBCA01 joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 as a hardened member server at 10.0.30.30 in the Servers OU (course 1.5 moves it, with the issuing CA, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, and template administration on its own seat. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Verify SBD-ROOTCA01 is in a workgroup with no active network path. Prepare approved installation media, protected administration, correct time, and backup storage."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Place the reviewed CAPolicy.inf in %SystemRoot% before configuration. Tailor it to the root role: in this lab it sets CRLPeriod=Weeks and CRLPeriodUnits=26, and CRLDeltaPeriodUnits=0 so the root publishes no delta CRL. A self-signed root's own CDP/AIA choices differ from the extensions the root places into issued subordinate certificates."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Install the Certification Authority role with management tools. In the AD CS Configuration wizard choose Standalone CA, Root CA, and a new private key, then apply the approved provider, cryptography (RSA 4096 with SHA-256 in this lab), CA name, validity (20 years), database, and log settings."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Before signing anything, configure the root's issued-certificate AIA/CDP settings, the subordinate validity policy (CA\\ValidityPeriod Years and CA\\ValidityPeriodUnits 10 in this lab), and the base-CRL schedule (26 weeks with an overlap period, and no delta CRL). Hold requests for explicit approval. Produce and inspect a current root CRL, then capture a protected CA backup."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Inspect locally on SBD-ROOTCA01 during the approved root session.\nGet-CimInstance Win32_ComputerSystem | Select-Object Name, Domain, PartOfDomain\nGet-Service CertSvc\ncertutil -getreg CA\\CRLPublicationURLs\ncertutil -getreg CA\\CACertPublicationURLs\ncertutil -getreg CA\\ValidityPeriodUnits\ncertutil -getreg CA\\CRLPeriodUnits"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The host remains disconnected, the CA is standalone/root, requests require approval, and the planned subordinate URLs and 10-year subordinate validity are configured before signing."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record the root certificate identity, policy file, publication settings, CRL dates, backup location, and operator sign-off. Protect backups containing private keys."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · CAPolicy.inf syntax\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/core-network-guide/cncg/server-certs/prepare-the-capolicy-inf-file\n\nMicrosoft · Install the offline root\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/certificate-services-root-issuing-authority/2-install-offline-root\n\nMicrosoft · Perform the offline root signing ceremony\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/certificate-services-root-issuing-authority/4-perform-offline-root-signing-ceremony")], question: "When must the root's issued-certificate publication URLs be configured?", answers: ["After clients have enrolled", "Only after the first CRL expires", "Before signing the subordinate CA certificate"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Issued certificates retain their extensions. Changing the CA configuration later does not rewrite existing certificates.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why is the root a standalone CA rather than an enterprise CA?", ["Standalone CAs use stronger cryptography", "It is not domain-joined and must work disconnected from the directory", "Enterprise CAs cannot sign subordinate certificates"], 1, "An enterprise CA depends on Active Directory. The offline root lives in a workgroup with no network path, so it must be standalone."), QuizQuestion("Where does CAPolicy.inf belong before configuration?", ["The domain SYSVOL share", "The removable media only", "%SystemRoot% on the CA host"], 2, "Setup reads CAPolicy.inf from %SystemRoot% during configuration. Placing it afterward does not change the installed CA.")], recap: ["The root host stays disconnected, with controlled media, administration, and time.", "CAPolicy.inf is in place before the role is configured.", "Publication URLs, validity policy, the CRL schedule, and a protected backup exist before any signing."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 prepares the online issuing CA and its certificate request.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#root", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:request", title: "Create the issuing CA request", minutes: 25, summary: "Prepare the online issuing server, but keep issuance paused until its CA certificate has been signed and installed. The request carries the public key; the private key stays protected on the issuing system or its approved key provider.", objectives: ["Prepare the domain-joined issuing host with its final identity.", "Generate the subordinate CA request without exposing the private key.", "Verify and hash the request before it travels to the root."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.3 (identity, DNS and time, and the hardened baseline); verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server 2025 hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01 in a disconnected workgroup, SBD-SUBCA01 joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 as a hardened member server at 10.0.30.30 in the Servers OU (course 1.5 moves it, with the issuing CA, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, and template administration on its own seat. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Confirm SBD-SUBCA01 has its final hostname, domain membership, static address, internal DNS, and correct time. Use a dedicated host and the privileges approved for enterprise CA configuration."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Prepare its role-specific CAPolicy.inf (in this lab: CRLPeriod=Weeks, CRLPeriodUnits=1, CRLDeltaPeriod=Days, CRLDeltaPeriodUnits=1, and LoadDefaultTemplates=0) and protected request directory. Decide which templates may later be published instead of enabling a broad default enrollment set."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Install the CA role and management tools. In the AD CS Configuration wizard choose Enterprise CA, Subordinate CA, and the approved new-key/provider settings (RSA 4096 with SHA-256 in this lab). Enter the planned CA identity and storage locations."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Choose Save a certificate request to file. Inspect the resulting request's subject, public key, and CA constraints; record its SHA-256 hash before handing it to the media custodian."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Use the actual request path chosen in the wizard.\ncertutil -dump C:\\CAConfig\\IssuingCA.req\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\CAConfig\\IssuingCA.req"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The request matches the approved issuing identity and key profile. The CA is waiting for its signed certificate; do not troubleshoot that expected state by installing another CA."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Retain the request, its reviewed fields and hash, and the request-transfer record. Do not place private keys in the transfer package."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · CAPolicy.inf syntax\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/core-network-guide/cncg/server-certs/prepare-the-capolicy-inf-file\n\nMicrosoft · Deploy the issuing CA role\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/certificate-services-root-issuing-authority/3-deploy-issuing-certification-authority-role")], question: "What moves to the offline root for signing?", answers: ["The issuing CA's certificate request", "The issuing CA's private key", "A network cable connected to the domain"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The signed request contains the public key. Root signing does not require exporting the issuing CA private key.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why record the request’s SHA-256 hash before the media transfer?", ["So the root operator can confirm the file was not altered in transit", "It encrypts the request", "The configuration wizard requires it to continue"], 0, "An independently recorded hash lets the ceremony verify integrity. It does not encrypt anything — it proves the same file arrived."), QuizQuestion("The new issuing CA is waiting with its service stopped. What is correct?", ["Reinstall the role until the service starts", "That is the expected state until the signed certificate is installed", "Join the root to the domain to finish the setup"], 1, "A subordinate CA cannot run before its CA certificate exists. The waiting state is normal, not a failure to fix.")], recap: ["The issuing host has its final name, addressing, DNS, and time before the role install.", "The request file carries the public key only.", "The request’s fields and hash are reviewed and recorded before transfer."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 walks the request through the offline signing ceremony.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#request", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:sign", title: "Sign through the offline ceremony", minutes: 30, summary: "A signing ceremony connects an approved request to an approved CA identity. A matching filename alone is not proof that the root should trust the request.", objectives: ["Verify request integrity at the root before submission.", "Issue the subordinate certificate under the approved validity policy.", "Export and verify the public artifacts, then return the root to a secured state."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.3 (identity, DNS and time, and the hardened baseline); verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server 2025 hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01 in a disconnected workgroup, SBD-SUBCA01 joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 as a hardened member server at 10.0.30.30 in the Servers OU (course 1.5 moves it, with the issuing CA, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, and template administration on its own seat. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Use approved removable media and custody procedures to bring the request to the root. Recalculate its hash and compare it with the independently recorded value; inspect the request before submission."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Verify the root's issued-certificate validity policy and publication extensions. In Certification Authority, submit the request, review its pending entry, and issue it only after the required approval."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Export the subordinate certificate, root certificate, and current root CRL. Check identities, constraints, validity, signatures, URLs, and revocation data against the approved hierarchy. Record file hashes and the request ID."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Transfer only the approved public artifacts back to the connected environment. Match published filenames to the URLs embedded in certificates, retain the ceremony record and protected backup, then shut down and secure the root."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Inspect public artifacts; use your actual approved paths.\ncertutil -dump C:\\CAConfig\\IssuingCA.cer\ncertutil -dump C:\\CAConfig\\RootCA.crl\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\CAConfig\\IssuingCA.cer, C:\\CAConfig\\RootCA.cer, C:\\CAConfig\\RootCA.crl"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Artifact identities, signatures, validity, and custody checks pass. The root remains offline; a field dump alone is not end-to-end trust validation."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep approval, request ID, artifact hashes, verified certificate/CRL properties, media custody, and root shutdown evidence together."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Perform the offline root signing ceremony\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/certificate-services-root-issuing-authority/4-perform-offline-root-signing-ceremony")], question: "Which transfer preserves the root boundary?", answers: ["An automated shared folder on the root", "Approved removable media carrying the reviewed request and public artifacts", "Temporary direct internet access"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "This training architecture uses manual, human-controlled transfer. It never adds a live link to the root.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What leaves the root environment after the ceremony?", ["The root’s private-key backup", "A live network share for future requests", "Only approved public artifacts: the subordinate certificate, root certificate, and current CRL"], 2, "The boundary stays intact: public artifacts travel on approved media; keys and live connections never do."), QuizQuestion("The request’s recalculated hash does not match the recorded value. What now?", ["Stop and investigate; do not submit the request", "Submit it anyway and compare afterward", "Regenerate the hash until it matches"], 0, "A hash mismatch means the file is not the one that was approved. Submitting it would sign an unverified request.")], recap: ["Integrity is verified before submission, and issuance follows explicit approval.", "Artifact identities, validity, and URLs are checked against the plan.", "The ceremony record, hashes, and root shutdown are all evidence."], nextStep: "Lesson 5 brings the issuing CA online and publishes the trust data.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#sign", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:publish", title: "Activate the issuing CA and distribute trust", minutes: 25, summary: "A signed certificate is not yet a working CA. Install it, start Certificate Services, set the AIA and CDP locations every issued certificate will carry, and distribute the approved root through managed domain policy.", objectives: ["Install the signed certificate and start Certificate Services.", "Configure the AIA and CDP URLs before any certificate is issued.", "Distribute root trust through managed domain policy."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.3 (identity, DNS and time, and the hardened baseline); verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server 2025 hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01 in a disconnected workgroup, SBD-SUBCA01 joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 as a hardened member server at 10.0.30.30 in the Servers OU (course 1.5 moves it, with the issuing CA, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, and template administration on its own seat. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Verify the returned artifacts and import the approved root certificate into the issuing server's trusted root store. Complete CA certificate installation through Certification Authority and start Certificate Services."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Configure the issuing CA's AIA and CDP settings before issuing any certificate, pointing them at the planned pki.sbd.test locations. Every certificate the CA signs carries these URLs, so they are fixed now and served exactly as written in lessons 6 and 7."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Distribute the approved root trust through the managed domain policy and arrange the required intermediate/directory publication with delegated administrators."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Hold off on certificate templates: lessons 8 and 9 give template administration its own seat and publish only the templates the plan needs."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Inspect the issuing CA after installation and publication.\nGet-Service CertSvc\ncertutil -getreg CA\\CRLPublicationURLs\ncertutil -getreg CA\\CACertPublicationURLs\ncertutil -store Root\ncertutil -store CA"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Certificate Services is running, the AIA and CDP settings carry the planned pki.sbd.test URLs, and the approved root reaches domain clients through policy."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the CA installation record, the AIA and CDP settings, and the trust-distribution policy."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Publish trust data and validate clients\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/certificate-services-root-issuing-authority/6-publish-trust-data-validate-clients\n\nMicrosoft · Certutil reference\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/certutil")], question: "Why are the AIA and CDP URLs set before the issuing CA signs its first certificate?", answers: ["They can be edited on each certificate afterward", "Every issued certificate carries them for its whole life, so a later change strands the certificates already in use", "Certificate Services will not start until they are published"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Extensions are fixed at issuance. Setting the locations first means every certificate points at the pki.sbd.test paths lessons 6 and 7 will serve.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why must published filenames match the URLs embedded in certificates?", ["The CA service will not start otherwise", "Clients fetch exactly those URLs; a renamed file breaks chain building and revocation checks", "Filenames control template permissions"], 1, "Chain building and revocation checking follow the embedded AIA and CDP URLs literally. Publication must serve those names."), QuizQuestion("How does the approved root certificate reach domain clients?", ["Each user imports it manually from email", "It replicates automatically through DNS", "Through the managed domain policy trust distribution"], 2, "Group Policy distributes the trusted root deliberately and reversibly, which keeps trust decisions managed.")], recap: ["The issuing CA is running with its signed certificate.", "AIA and CDP URLs are set before the first certificate is issued.", "Root trust distributes through managed domain policy."], nextStep: "Lesson 6 gives those embedded URLs a real home: the pki.sbd.test publication site.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#publish", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:publication", title: "Build the pki.sbd.test publication site", minutes: 30, summary: "Every certificate the issuing CA has signed carries the pki.sbd.test URLs inside it. This lesson gives those URLs a real home: a small web site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01 serving certificates and revocation lists to the whole network.", objectives: ["Stand up the HTTP publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01.", "Confirm the DNS alias from course 1.2 points at the publication host.", "Explain why CDP and AIA publication uses HTTP, not HTTPS."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.3 (identity, DNS and time, and the hardened baseline); verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server 2025 hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01 in a disconnected workgroup, SBD-SUBCA01 joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 as a hardened member server at 10.0.30.30 in the Servers OU (course 1.5 moves it, with the issuing CA, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, and template administration on its own seat. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "On SBD-PKI-MGMT01, install the Web Server role and create a site (or virtual directory) whose folder will hold the published certificates and revocation lists. Serve .cer, .crt, and .crl files; nothing else belongs in this directory."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Confirm the pki.sbd.test alias from course 1.2 resolves to 10.0.30.30 from a client in another VLAN, and that the service-path register's HTTP row permits the path."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Leave the site on plain HTTP. Revocation checking has to work before any certificate can be trusted — putting the CRL behind HTTPS creates a loop where validating the site needs the very file it serves."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Restrict who can write to the publication folder: the CA's publishing process and the PKI administrators, nobody else. Clients read; they never write."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Resolve-DnsName pki.sbd.test\nTest-NetConnection pki.sbd.test -Port 80\nGet-Website | Select-Object Name, State, PhysicalPath"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "pki.sbd.test resolves to the publication host from outside VLAN 30, the site answers on port 80, and the publication folder's permissions separate writers from readers."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record the site configuration, folder permissions, the DNS resolution output from a cross-VLAN client, and the register row that permits the path."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · PKI design considerations\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-cs/pki-design-considerations\n\nMicrosoft · Certutil reference\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/certutil")], question: "Why does the publication site stay on plain HTTP?", answers: ["HTTPS licenses cost money", "Clients need the CRL to validate certificates — serving it over HTTPS creates a dependency loop", "HTTP is faster than HTTPS in every case"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Revocation data must be reachable before trust can be evaluated. The files are public and signed; integrity comes from the signatures, not the transport.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What belongs in the publication directory?", ["Public CA certificates and CRLs only", "The issuing CA's private key backup", "Administrator scripts and passwords"], 0, "The same rule that runs through the whole PKI: clients need the public trust artifacts; secrets never enter a client-readable path."), QuizQuestion("Which earlier work made this lesson a one-page job?", ["Nothing — it stands alone", "Course 1.1's domain controller promotion", "The DNS alias reserved in 1.2 and the HTTP row in the service-path register"], 2, "pki.sbd.test was reserved in course 1.2 and the path through the boundary is already in the register — the sequence pays off here.")], recap: ["pki.sbd.test now answers from SBD-PKI-MGMT01 over HTTP.", "Only public artifacts live in the folder; only PKI processes write to it.", "The transport is HTTP on purpose — revocation precedes trust."], nextStep: "Lesson 7 fills the site with the real trust data and proves the embedded URLs work.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#publication", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:publish-trust", title: "Publish the trust data at its advertised URLs", minutes: 25, summary: "A publication site is only correct when the filenames it serves match the URLs the certificates already carry. Copy the current certificates and CRLs into place, wire the issuing CA to keep them fresh, and prove retrieval from a client that has never cached anything.", objectives: ["Publish the root and issuing certificates and current CRLs at the exact embedded URLs.", "Configure the issuing CA to refresh its published files automatically.", "Prove live retrieval and chain building from a cross-VLAN client."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.3 (identity, DNS and time, and the hardened baseline); verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server 2025 hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01 in a disconnected workgroup, SBD-SUBCA01 joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 as a hardened member server at 10.0.30.30 in the Servers OU (course 1.5 moves it, with the issuing CA, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, and template administration on its own seat. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Compare the issuing CA's configured AIA and CDP URLs against the publication folder's layout. Every embedded URL must map to a real file path on the site — the filenames are not yours to improve."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Copy the current root certificate, root CRL, issuing certificate, and issuing CRL into the folder. The root's files still arrive by approved media; the issuing CA's can be written directly by its publishing process."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Configure the issuing CA's CRL publication to write into the site's folder on its schedule, so each new CRL replaces the old file at the same name before it expires."), LessonSection("Step 4", "From a clean client in another VLAN, fetch each URL and run the full verification: the chain must build and revocation data must come from the live site, not a cache."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "certutil -verify -urlfetch C:\\Evidence\\pki\\test.cer\ncertutil -URL http://pki.sbd.test/RootCA.crl\nGet-ChildItem C:\\inetpub\\pkiroot | Select-Object Name, LastWriteTime"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Every AIA and CDP URL embedded in issued certificates returns the current file, urlfetch verification passes from a cross-VLAN client, and the CRL refresh lands at the same filename automatically."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the URL-to-file mapping table, the folder listing with timestamps, and the urlfetch output from the clean client."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · PKI design considerations\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-cs/pki-design-considerations\n\nMicrosoft · Certutil reference\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/certutil")], question: "Why can't the published filenames be renamed to something tidier?", answers: ["The web server forbids renames", "Issued certificates already embed the exact URLs; clients fetch those literally", "The hardening baseline assigns the filenames"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The same lesson this course taught at signing time: extensions are fixed at issuance, so publication conforms to them, never the reverse.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("How do new root CRLs reach the site in this lab?", ["By approved removable media from the offline ceremony", "Over a direct network share from the root", "They never need refreshing"], 0, "The root has no network path. Its refreshed CRL travels like everything else that crosses that boundary — reviewed, hashed, carried."), QuizQuestion("What does certutil -verify -urlfetch add over opening the certificate?", ["It prints the certificate in color", "Nothing", "It follows the embedded URLs live, proving publication and reachability rather than cached trust"], 2, "The -urlfetch option is the end-to-end test: chain building plus live AIA and CDP retrieval through the routed network.")], recap: ["Published filenames mirror the embedded URLs exactly.", "The issuing CA refreshes its files on schedule; the root's arrive by media.", "A clean cross-VLAN client proves the chain and live revocation."], nextStep: "Lesson 8 moves template administration off the CA and onto its own seat.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#publish-trust", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:template-admin", title: "Separate template administration", minutes: 25, summary: "Certificate templates are directory objects, and whoever edits them decides what the PKI will sign. Give that power its own seat — SBD-PKI-MGMT01 — with named administrators, instead of leaving it wherever a CA console happens to be open.", objectives: ["Establish SBD-PKI-MGMT01 as the only administration seat for templates.", "Delegate template permissions to a named PKI administrators group.", "Record the template change process before any template changes."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.3 (identity, DNS and time, and the hardened baseline); verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server 2025 hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01 in a disconnected workgroup, SBD-SUBCA01 joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 as a hardened member server at 10.0.30.30 in the Servers OU (course 1.5 moves it, with the issuing CA, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, and template administration on its own seat. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Install the certificate management tools on SBD-PKI-MGMT01 and confirm the Certificate Templates console can read the directory's template store from there."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Create a PKI administrators group in the directory, place it under the Admin OU from course 1.3, and delegate the template permissions to that group — membership is the record of who may change what the CA signs."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Remove day-to-day template editing from everywhere else: the issuing CA's console stays for CA operations, not template design, and no Tier-0 account edits templates as routine work."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Write the change process: a template change names its requester, its reviewer, the versioning action (edit, duplicate, or supersede), and lands in the evidence record like any other configuration change."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ADGroup 'SBD PKI Admins' -Properties Members | Select-Object Name, Members\ncertutil -template | Select-String 'TemplatePropCommonName' | Select-Object -First 10"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The templates console works from SBD-PKI-MGMT01, the PKI administrators group holds the delegated permissions, and the written change process names requester, reviewer, and versioning action."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the delegation record, the group membership listing, and the signed-off template change process."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · PKI design considerations\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-cs/pki-design-considerations\n\nMicrosoft · Manage certificate templates\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-cs/manage-certificate-templates")], question: "Why do certificate templates deserve their own administration seat?", answers: ["Templates are directory objects that define what the CA will sign — editing them is a trust decision", "The CA console cannot display templates", "Templates take too much disk space on the CA"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "A template edit changes what enrollment can produce. Separating the seat and naming the editors makes that power visible and auditable.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Where does the PKI administrators group live?", ["The default Users container", "Under the Admin OU from course 1.3, with the other privileged groups", "On the offline root"], 1, "The OU design already has a home for privileged identity — this group joins it so delegation and policy follow the same structure."), QuizQuestion("A template needs a change. What is the first artifact?", ["The edited template itself", "A restart of Certificate Services", "The change record: requester, reviewer, and the versioning action"], 2, "The record precedes the change, the same discipline as the network's service-path register — configuration follows documentation, not the other way around.")], recap: ["SBD-PKI-MGMT01 is the template administration seat.", "A named group under the Admin OU holds the delegated power.", "Template changes have a written process before they have edits."], nextStep: "Lesson 9 uses that process to publish the lab's first working templates.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#template-admin", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:first-templates", title: "Publish the first working templates", minutes: 25, summary: "The default template set is a menu, not a deployment. Duplicate the two templates this network actually needs — computer authentication and server authentication — set their cryptography deliberately, and publish only those.", objectives: ["Duplicate and configure the computer and web server templates against the approved plan.", "Set key sizes and validity that satisfy the Windows Server 2025 minimums.", "Publish only the needed templates, scoped to a pilot group first."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.3 (identity, DNS and time, and the hardened baseline); verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server 2025 hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01 in a disconnected workgroup, SBD-SUBCA01 joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 as a hardened member server at 10.0.30.30 in the Servers OU (course 1.5 moves it, with the issuing CA, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, and template administration on its own seat. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "From the administration seat, duplicate the Computer template as SBD Computer Authentication. Set the approved key provider, a key size of at least 2,048 bits, a validity period within lesson 1's one-to-two-year example, subject built from directory information, and autoenrollment permission for a pilot computer group only."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Duplicate the Web Server template as SBD Server Authentication for the business services in course 1.9: enrollment permission for the server administrators, no autoenrollment, and exportable keys only if the recovery plan explicitly says so."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Record each template's version, settings, and justification through the lesson-3 change process before publishing."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Publish exactly these two templates on the issuing CA and remove any default template the plan does not call for — an unpublished template cannot be mis-enrolled."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "certutil -CATemplates -config 'SBD-SUBCA01\\SBD Issuing CA'\ncertutil -template 'SBDComputerAuthentication' | Select-String 'msPKI-Certificate-Name-Flag','msPKI-Minimal-Key-Size'"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The CA publishes exactly the two planned templates, each shows the approved key size and permissions, and both carry a completed change record."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep each template's configuration export, its change record, and the CA's published-template listing."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Manage certificate templates\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-cs/manage-certificate-templates\n\nMicrosoft · Configure certificate autoenrollment\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/core-network-guide/cncg/server-certs/configure-server-certificate-autoenrollment\n\nMicrosoft · What's new in Windows Server 2025\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/get-started/whats-new-windows-server-2025")], question: "Why duplicate the built-in templates instead of editing them?", answers: ["Built-ins cannot be published at all", "Duplication preserves the originals and gives your version its own name, version, and record", "Duplicates issue faster"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Working on a named copy keeps the baseline intact and makes your settings — and their history — unmistakably yours.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What floor applies to the template key sizes on this network?", ["512 bits for speed", "1,024 bits", "2,048-bit RSA at minimum — Windows Server 2025 enforces it for TLS server authentication"], 2, "Windows Server 2025 requires at least 2,048-bit RSA keys for TLS server authentication, so a plan below that produces certificates the platform itself will reject."), QuizQuestion("Why publish to a pilot group before the whole domain?", ["A small blast radius proves the template before autoenrollment touches fifty machines in course 1.7", "Pilots are a licensing requirement", "Templates expire if unused"], 0, "The fleet course will turn autoenrollment loose at scale. Proving the template on a pilot first is the same test-then-widen habit as the Group Policy work.")], recap: ["Two named templates exist, configured on purpose, nothing else published.", "Key sizes meet the 2025 floor; permissions are pilot-scoped.", "Every template carries its change record."], nextStep: "The final lesson proves enrollment and revocation end to end and runs the delta scans.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#first-templates", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:validate", title: "Validate enrollment, revocation, and recovery", minutes: 30, summary: "Finish from a clean domain client the way a relying system sees it: a pilot machine enrolls on its own, validates against the live publication site, and rejects a certificate you revoke — then the change-triggered scans record what this course did.", objectives: ["Prove pilot autoenrollment through the published template.", "Verify chain building and live revocation retrieval from pki.sbd.test, and prove revocation works.", "Schedule the renewals that keep the PKI alive, run the delta scans, and file the evidence."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.3 (identity, DNS and time, and the hardened baseline); verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server 2025 hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01 in a disconnected workgroup, SBD-SUBCA01 joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 as a hardened member server at 10.0.30.30 in the Servers OU (course 1.5 moves it, with the issuing CA, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, and template administration on its own seat. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Enable the autoenrollment policy for the pilot computer group, refresh policy on a pilot machine, and confirm it receives an SBD Computer Authentication certificate without a human touching the request."), LessonSection("Step 2", "On that machine, export the public certificate and run the full verification: the chain must reach the approved root and every AIA and CDP URL must answer from pki.sbd.test with current CRL dates — cached success is not evidence of publication."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Enroll a disposable test certificate, revoke only that one, publish an updated issuing CRL, and verify that a fresh client evaluation detects the revocation. Diagnose retrieval or stale-publication problems rather than turning off revocation checks."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Record owners and reminders for root and issuing CRL publication, certificate renewal, backup, and an isolated recovery rehearsal. Schedule offline root ceremonies early enough to transfer and publish replacement CRLs before expiry."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Rescan SBD-SUBCA01 and take SBD-PKI-MGMT01's first baseline with the same benchmark and tool versions as always; the publication site and template changes are the expected deltas."), LessonSection("Step 6", "File the course evidence: enrollment output, urlfetch and revocation results, template records, renewal owners, and the scan set — the PKI chapter is finished."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "gpupdate /force\ncertutil -pulse\nGet-ChildItem Cert:\\LocalMachine\\My | Select-Object Subject, NotAfter, Thumbprint\ncertutil -verify -urlfetch C:\\Evidence\\pki\\pilot.cer\n# After revoking the disposable test certificate and publishing the new CRL:\ncertutil -urlfetch -verify test.cer"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The pilot enrolls hands-free and verifies with live retrieval from pki.sbd.test; after revocation and publication the test certificate is rejected for the expected reason; both VLAN 30 hosts have current, attributed scan results."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Finish the PKI checklist with the pilot's certificate details, urlfetch and revocation results, renewal dates and recovery owners, and the delta-scan comparison for SUBCA01 plus the PKI-MGMT01 baseline."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Publish trust data and validate clients\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/certificate-services-root-issuing-authority/6-publish-trust-data-validate-clients\n\nMicrosoft · Certutil reference\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/certutil\n\nMicrosoft · Configure certificate autoenrollment\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/core-network-guide/cncg/server-certs/configure-server-certificate-autoenrollment")], question: "What does a hands-free pilot enrollment prove?", answers: ["Template permissions, autoenrollment policy, the CA, DNS, and the network path all agree — the whole chain works", "Only that Group Policy refreshed", "That the root CA is online"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Autoenrollment succeeds only when every layer this path has built cooperates — which is exactly why it is the closing test.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("A valid certificate fails because its CRL cannot be retrieved. What is the next step?", ["Fix and retest the publication or reachability problem", "Disable revocation checking for all clients", "Copy the root private key to the client"], 0, "Reliable revocation publication is part of the service. Bypassing the check conceals the failed dependency."), QuizQuestion("Why schedule offline root ceremonies ahead of CRL expiry?", ["A replacement CRL must be transferred and published before the old one expires", "The root certificate expires monthly", "Clients delete their trust store on expiry"], 0, "If the root CRL lapses, revocation checking fails across the hierarchy. Manual transfer takes lead time, so it is scheduled early."), QuizQuestion("Which hosts get scanned at the end of this course?", ["Only the pilot workstation", "SBD-SUBCA01 (rescan) and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 (first baseline)", "The offline root over the network"], 1, "The cadence is change-triggered: the CA changed and a new host joined, so one rescan and one baseline.")], recap: ["Pilot autoenrollment works end to end, with live revocation retrieval from pki.sbd.test.", "A revoked test certificate is rejected for the expected reason.", "Renewal, backup, and recovery each have an owner and a date; delta scans close the PKI chapter."], nextStep: "That completes Configure the two-tier PKI. Course 1.5 brings DHCP and the tiered administration plane online before any fleet exists.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#validate", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "tiered-admin", number: "1.5", title: "Addressing and tiered administration", subtitle: "DHCP, admin seats & where privilege may sign in", category: "Network build", description: "Build the control plane before the fleet: DHCP with its core relay for the workstation VLAN, three dedicated administration systems in VLAN 60, and directory-enforced tier restrictions proven by both successful in-tier sessions and logged out-of-tier denials.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "6 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 6,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "tiered-admin-guide", title: "Addressing and tiered administration · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "DHCP, admin seats & where privilege may sign in", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/tiered-admin", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "tiered-admin-pdf", title: "Addressing and tiered administration · Workbook PDF", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "17 pages · edition 1.3", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/tiered-admin/SecureByDesign-tiered-admin-Workbook.pdf", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "tiered-admin-word", title: "Addressing and tiered administration · Editable workbook (Word)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Make the workbook your own.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/tiered-admin/SecureByDesign-tiered-admin-Workbook.docx", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "tiered-admin-checklist-page", title: "Addressing and tiered administration · Printable checklist", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Print the configuration and evidence checklist.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/tiered-admin/checklist", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "tiered-admin-checklist", title: "Addressing and tiered administration · Configuration checklist", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Download a Markdown checklist for your configuration and evidence.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/tiered-admin/configuration-checklist.md", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:tiered-admin:design", title: "Design the tiers and the address plan", minutes: 20, summary: "Tiering is one sentence with teeth: credentials that control a tier never touch a less-trusted one. Write that policy against the OU structure, then plan the DHCP scope the fleet will live in — both before any host is built.", objectives: ["Define the tiers and map each to systems, OUs, and admin accounts.", "Write the credential rule each tier enforces.", "Plan the VLAN 80 DHCP scope, options, and relay."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.4: the finished two-tier PKI with its publication site and templates, plus course 1.3's OU structure with the Admin OU in place. The network's routing and default-deny segmentation, with its access control list (ACL) register, are already in place.\n\nProvision Windows Server 2025 hosts for SBD-DHCP01 (10.0.20.10) and the three administration systems in VLAN 60: AdminJumpBox01 (.10), SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (.20), SBD-ADMIN-PC01 (.30).\n\nDecide the privileged accounts: separate admin identities per tier, never daily-driver accounts with admin rights."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the control plane before the fleet exists: DHCP for the workstation VLAN, and a tiered administration model where privileged sessions only ever start from dedicated hosts. The model's rule is strict — the jump systems administer; they browse nothing, read no mail, and accept no inbound sessions from user space."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Define three tiers against what exists, using Microsoft's rule: anything that can control a Tier 0 system is Tier 0. Tier 0 is the identity and trust fabric (DCs, both CAs, PKI-MGMT01) plus what controls it — the hypervisor hosts and their console, the core router's management, and the DC-facing parts of the tools that patch, protect, scan, monitor, and back up the DCs. Tier 1 is the member servers; Tier 2 the user endpoints. Map each tier to its OUs: Tier 0 to the Domain Controllers OU plus a new Tier 0 Servers OU for SBD-SUBCA01 and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 (lesson 4 moves them out of the Servers OU), Tier 1 to the Servers OU, and Tier 2 to the Workstations OU."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Write the credential rule per tier: a Tier 0 account signs in only on Tier 0 systems and AdminJumpBox01; Tier 1 admins work from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01; Tier 2 from SBD-ADMIN-PC01. The audit custodian uses separate accounts and sessions on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 that ordinary server operators cannot use. Each administrator gets separate per-tier accounts — the daily account holds no admin rights anywhere."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Plan the DHCP design: scope 10.0.80.100–199 for the workstation VLAN — 100 addresses, room for the 50 PCs, the test clients, and replacement machines whose old leases have not yet expired — with options for router 10.0.80.1, the two DCs as DNS, and the sbd.test domain; the core router relays VLAN 80 broadcasts to SBD-DHCP01; everything else stays static."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Add the register rows this course needs: VLAN 60 to each tier's management ports (rules A01–A04 in Inside the Network), DHCP relay from the core to SBD-DHCP01 (F04), and explicit denies from VLAN 80 into VLAN 60 — user space never initiates toward the admin plane."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Capture the design inputs: OU targets and current account separation.\nGet-ADOrganizationalUnit -Filter * | Select-Object Name, DistinguishedName\nGet-ADGroupMember 'Domain Admins' | Select-Object Name, SamAccountName"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The tier map names systems, OUs, admin hosts, and accounts for every tier; the DHCP plan is written; and the register has the new permit and deny rows with owners."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the tier access matrix, the per-administrator account plan, the DHCP scope design, and the updated register rows."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/dhcp/dhcp-top\n\nMicrosoft · Privileged access workstations and the access model\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/security/privileged-access-workstations/overview\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "What is the one-sentence tiering rule?", answers: ["Admins should use strong passwords", "Credentials that control a tier never sign in to a less-trusted tier", "Every admin needs Domain Admin rights"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Everything else in the model — hosts, OUs, logon policies — exists to enforce that single sentence.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why does each administrator get separate per-tier accounts?", ["So a credential captured on a lower tier cannot open a higher one", "Licensing counts accounts per tier", "Password policies require it"], 0, "Credential theft travels with sign-ins. Separate accounts bound what any one capture is worth."), QuizQuestion("Which direction does the register explicitly deny?", ["Admin VLAN to the tiers it manages", "DCs to anything", "User VLAN 80 initiating into the admin VLAN 60"], 2, "The admin plane reaches down to manage; user space never reaches up. The deny row makes that a logged fact, not an intention."), QuizQuestion("SBD-WSUS01 approves the updates the domain controllers install. Which tier administers the domain controllers' update ring?", ["Tier 1, because WSUS is a member server", "Tier 0, because anything that can push code to a DC can control it", "Tier 2, because updates start on workstations"], 1, "Microsoft's tier model treats systems that patch, scan, monitor, protect, back up, or host Tier 0 as Tier 0. The DC-facing scope of a tool belongs to Tier 0 even when the server itself sits in a member-server VLAN.")], recap: ["Three tiers, each with its systems, OU, admin host, and accounts.", "The credential rule is written before any host enforces it.", "DHCP scope, options, relay, and register rows are planned."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 builds the DHCP service the fleet will depend on.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/tiered-admin#design", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:tiered-admin:dhcp", title: "Build SBD-DHCP01 and the relay", minutes: 25, summary: "Addressing is the first service fifty machines will ask for. Build the DHCP server, authorize it in the directory so rogue servers stand out, and relay VLAN 80's broadcasts through the core.", objectives: ["Install and authorize the DHCP role on SBD-DHCP01.", "Create the VLAN 80 scope with the planned options.", "Configure the core relay and prove a lease end to end."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.4: the finished two-tier PKI with its publication site and templates, plus course 1.3's OU structure with the Admin OU in place. The network's routing and default-deny segmentation, with its access control list (ACL) register, are already in place.\n\nProvision Windows Server 2025 hosts for SBD-DHCP01 (10.0.20.10) and the three administration systems in VLAN 60: AdminJumpBox01 (.10), SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (.20), SBD-ADMIN-PC01 (.30).\n\nDecide the privileged accounts: separate admin identities per tier, never daily-driver accounts with admin rights."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the control plane before the fleet exists: DHCP for the workstation VLAN, and a tiered administration model where privileged sessions only ever start from dedicated hosts. The model's rule is strict — the jump systems administer; they browse nothing, read no mail, and accept no inbound sessions from user space."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Install the DHCP role on SBD-DHCP01 in the Servers OU and complete directory authorization — an unauthorized Windows DHCP server refuses to serve domain networks, the directory's built-in check against rogue DHCP servers."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Create the IPv4 scope 10.0.80.100–199 with the planned options: router 10.0.80.1, DNS 10.0.10.10 and 10.0.10.11, domain sbd.test. Leave every other VLAN static as designed."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Configure dynamic DNS updates using a dedicated low-privilege credential so client records register securely in the sbd.test zone from course 1.2."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Enable the DHCP relay on the core router's VLAN 80 interface toward 10.0.20.10, confirm the register row (F04) permits it, then pull a lease with a temporary test VM in VLAN 80 and watch its A record appear."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-DhcpServerInDC\nGet-DhcpServerv4Scope | Select-Object ScopeId, StartRange, EndRange, State\nGet-DhcpServerv4OptionValue -ScopeId 10.0.80.0 | Select-Object OptionId, Value\nResolve-DnsName sbd-test80.sbd.test"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The server shows as authorized, the scope serves the planned range and options through the relay, and the test client's secure dynamic DNS record exists on both DCs."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record the authorization output, scope and option configuration, the relay setting on the core, and the test lease with its DNS registration."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/dhcp/dhcp-top\n\nMicrosoft · Configure a firewall for Active Directory domains\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/config-firewall-for-ad-domains-and-trusts")], question: "What does directory authorization of a DHCP server give you?", answers: ["Faster leases", "A built-in check that makes unauthorized Windows DHCP servers refuse to serve the domain", "Larger scopes"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Authorization is the directory's rogue-DHCP control — a one-click setting with a real security story behind it.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why does the relay exist at all?", ["DHCP broadcasts do not cross the router, so the core forwards VLAN 80's requests to the server in VLAN 20", "It encrypts DHCP traffic", "Relays make addresses permanent"], 0, "Broadcasts stop at the VLAN boundary. The relay is the deliberate, registered exception that carries them to SBD-DHCP01."), QuizQuestion("Why use a dedicated credential for dynamic DNS updates?", ["The server cannot update DNS otherwise", "It speeds up record creation", "Record ownership and updates trace to one low-privilege identity instead of a powerful computer account"], 2, "Secure updates from course 1.2 are only as accountable as the identity writing them — a scoped credential keeps that clean.")], recap: ["SBD-DHCP01 is authorized, scoped, and serving through the relay.", "Options hand out the gateway, both DCs, and the domain.", "A test lease proved addressing and secure DNS registration together."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 builds the three systems the administrators will work from.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/tiered-admin#dhcp", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:tiered-admin:admin-hosts", title: "Build the administration systems", minutes: 35, summary: "Privileged work gets dedicated, hardened seats: one jump host for Tier 0, one workstation for server administration, one for endpoint administration — each in the Admin OU, each doing nothing else.", objectives: ["Link the hardening policies to the Admin OU's computer sub-OUs, then build the three VLAN 60 systems into them.", "Keep them single-purpose: hardened, minimal, no user-space services.", "Register and restrict their management paths."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.4: the finished two-tier PKI with its publication site and templates, plus course 1.3's OU structure with the Admin OU in place. The network's routing and default-deny segmentation, with its access control list (ACL) register, are already in place.\n\nProvision Windows Server 2025 hosts for SBD-DHCP01 (10.0.20.10) and the three administration systems in VLAN 60: AdminJumpBox01 (.10), SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (.20), SBD-ADMIN-PC01 (.30).\n\nDecide the privileged accounts: separate admin identities per tier, never daily-driver accounts with admin rights."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the control plane before the fleet exists: DHCP for the workstation VLAN, and a tiered administration model where privileged sessions only ever start from dedicated hosts. The model's rule is strict — the jump systems administer; they browse nothing, read no mail, and accept no inbound sessions from user space."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Create two computer sub-OUs under the Admin OU — Admin Servers for AdminJumpBox01 and SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, Admin Workstations for SBD-ADMIN-PC01 — and link course 1.3's Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) to them before any seat joins: the member-server policies to Admin Servers and the Windows 11 policies from the same package to Admin Workstations. Course 1.3 linked them only to the Domain Controllers and Servers OUs, so without this step the seats would boot unhardened."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Build AdminJumpBox01, SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, and SBD-ADMIN-PC01 as clean Windows Server 2025 (or Windows 11, for the endpoint seat) installs and join each into its sub-OU, so the hardening applies from first boot."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Strip them to purpose: no browsing to anything but internal admin interfaces, no mail, no file shares hosted, and the management tools for exactly their tier — Remote Server Administration Tools (RSAT) on the server seats, endpoint tooling on the PC seat."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Register the management paths: VLAN 60 to Tier 0 and Tier 1 ports (remote desktop 3389, remote management 5985–5986, SSH 22 for the domain-joined Linux servers) from the specific admin host each tier allows, and the router's management on VLAN 100 from AdminJumpBox01 only. Keep the VLAN 80 → VLAN 60 deny from lesson 1 in force."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Require the admin hosts' own local firewalls to accept management inbound from nowhere — sessions originate here, they never terminate here."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ADComputer -SearchBase 'OU=Admin,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' -Filter * | Select-Object Name, DistinguishedName\nGet-GPInheritance -Target 'OU=Admin Servers,OU=Admin,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test'\nGet-GPInheritance -Target 'OU=Admin Workstations,OU=Admin,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test'\nTest-NetConnection SBD-DC01.sbd.test -Port 5985\ngpresult /r /scope:computer"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "All three hosts sit in their Admin sub-OUs with the STIG GPOs linked there and applied, management ports answer from the correct admin host per tier, and nothing in user space can open a session toward VLAN 60."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record each host's build sheet, OU placement, the sub-OU GPO links, gpresult output, and the register rows for its permitted management paths."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Privileged access workstations and the access model\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/security/privileged-access-workstations/overview\n\nMicrosoft · Configure a firewall for Active Directory domains\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/config-firewall-for-ad-domains-and-trusts")], question: "Why do the admin hosts run nothing but administration?", answers: ["Extra software costs licenses", "Every extra capability — mail, browsing, documents — is a way to phish or poison the most valuable sessions on the network", "Admin tools conflict with browsers"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "These hosts carry privileged credentials. Minimizing what runs on them minimizes what can steal from them.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Which way do management sessions flow?", ["From the admin hosts outward to their tier — never inbound to the admin hosts", "In both directions equally", "From workstations to the jump box"], 0, "The admin plane originates; it never serves. Inbound-nothing is what keeps a compromised endpoint from riding into VLAN 60."), QuizQuestion("Why link the STIG GPOs to the Admin sub-OUs before the seats join?", ["The seats cannot join the domain otherwise", "The OU name requires it", "Course 1.3 linked them only to the Domain Controllers and Servers OUs, so the seats would otherwise boot unhardened"], 2, "Policy follows links, not names. Linking first means each seat is STIG-configured from its first boot, before it ever does privileged work.")], recap: ["Three single-purpose admin seats, hardened from first boot.", "Management flows outward only; VLAN 60 accepts nothing inbound.", "Every management path is a register row."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 makes the credential rule something the directory enforces.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/tiered-admin#admin-hosts", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:tiered-admin:logon-restrictions", title: "Restrict where privilege can sign in", minutes: 40, summary: "The tier rule becomes real when the directory refuses the wrong sign-in. Give Tier 0 servers their own OU first, deny privileged logons on lower tiers by policy, protect the highest accounts, and make the refusal something you can show in a log.", objectives: ["Move the Tier 0 member servers into their own OU before any deny policy lands.", "Enforce per-tier sign-in restrictions with Group Policy logon rights, and record the one network-logon exception.", "Protect Tier 0 accounts and prove a cross-tier sign-in is denied and logged."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.4: the finished two-tier PKI with its publication site and templates, plus course 1.3's OU structure with the Admin OU in place. The network's routing and default-deny segmentation, with its access control list (ACL) register, are already in place.\n\nProvision Windows Server 2025 hosts for SBD-DHCP01 (10.0.20.10) and the three administration systems in VLAN 60: AdminJumpBox01 (.10), SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (.20), SBD-ADMIN-PC01 (.30).\n\nDecide the privileged accounts: separate admin identities per tier, never daily-driver accounts with admin rights."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the control plane before the fleet exists: DHCP for the workstation VLAN, and a tiered administration model where privileged sessions only ever start from dedicated hosts. The model's rule is strict — the jump systems administer; they browse nothing, read no mail, and accept no inbound sessions from user space."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Create the Tier 0 Servers OU (OU=Tier 0 Servers,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test) with deletion protection, link course 1.3's member-server STIG GPOs to it so nothing loses its hardening, and move SBD-SUBCA01 and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 there. Both are Tier 0, and the Servers OU is about to deny Tier 0 sign-ins — left there, they would lock Tier 0 out of its own systems."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Create one tier logon GPO for the Servers OU — now Tier 1 only — and one for the Workstations OU, and never link either to the Domain Controllers or Tier 0 Servers OUs. User rights don't merge across GPOs: for each right, the highest-precedence GPO that sets it wins, so each list repeats the entries course 1.3's STIG GPO sets for that right (such as Domain Admins and Enterprise Admins), and the tier GPO links above the STIG GPO. On the Servers OU, give the Tier 0 admin group (SBD Tier 0 Admins in this sample) Deny log on locally, Deny log on through Remote Desktop Services, Deny log on as a batch job, and Deny log on as a service. On the Workstations OU, give both the Tier 0 and Tier 1 groups those four denies plus Deny access to this computer from the network."), LessonSection("Step 3", "On the Servers OU, set Deny access to this computer from the network — the Tier 0 group plus the STIG's entries — in a separate GPO that excludes the SBD Tier 0 Console Hosts group through security filtering (deny Apply group policy). Course 1.8 adds SBD-WSUS01 and SBD-EPO01 to that group, so those two keep only the STIG's own setting. From AdminJumpBox01, Tier 0 manages the domain controllers' WSUS update ring and their agent policy in Trellix ePolicy Orchestrator (ePO) through those two consoles, using Tier 0 task accounts outside Domain Admins and Enterprise Admins (which the STIG denies network logon on member servers); a Kerberos network logon leaves no reusable credentials on the server. Record the exception — the two servers, the accounts, the reason, and its owner — in the course 1.3 exception record."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Add Tier 0 administrators to the Protected Users group so the domain refuses their weaker authentication paths, and note the Windows Server 2025 behavior that supports this design: RC4 ticket-granting tickets are gone and legacy remote password changes are blocked for protected accounts. Protected Users accounts also cannot be delegated, which keeps the console exception free of reusable credentials."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Keep the built-in safety valves: one documented break-glass account, excluded from the deny policies, with its password sealed and its every use alarmed in a later logging course."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Record the multifactor decision the model calls for on VLAN 60 — smart card or certificate-based sign-in for privileged accounts is the natural follow-on to course 1.4's templates; write the decision and its target date even if implementation waits."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ADComputer -SearchBase 'OU=Tier 0 Servers,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' -Filter * | Select-Object Name\nGet-ADGroupMember 'Protected Users' | Select-Object Name\n# On the Tier 2 test machine: export the effective logon rights and read the deny lines.\nsecedit /export /mergedpolicy /cfg C:\\Evidence\\tiered-admin\\user-rights.inf /areas user_rights\nSelect-String -Path C:\\Evidence\\tiered-admin\\user-rights.inf -Pattern 'SeDeny'\n(Get-ADGroup 'SBD Tier 0 Admins').SID.Value   # from an admin seat: this SID must appear on those lines\n# After the refused Tier 0 sign-in: event 4625 with status 0xC000015B (logon type not granted).\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=4625; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddHours(-1)} | Where-Object Message -match '0xC000015B' | Select-Object TimeCreated, Id"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "SBD-SUBCA01 and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 sit in the Tier 0 Servers OU and still accept Tier 0 sign-ins; on a Tier 2 test machine the deny rights list the Tier 0 group's SID, and a Tier 0 sign-in there is refused with Security event 4625 (status 0xC000015B); the console network-logon exception is in the exception record; Protected Users contains the Tier 0 admins; and the break-glass and MFA decisions are written."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the Tier 0 Servers OU move, the logon-rights GPO reports with their links and security filtering, the user-rights export, the refused sign-in's 4625 event, the exception record entry, the Protected Users membership, and the break-glass and MFA decision records."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Privileged access workstations and the access model\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/security/privileged-access-workstations/overview\n\nMicrosoft · User Rights Assignment\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/security-policy-settings/user-rights-assignment\n\nMicrosoft · Administrative tools and logon types\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/securing-privileged-access/reference-tools-logon-types\n\nMicrosoft · Protected Users security group\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/credentials-protection-and-management/protected-users-security-group\n\nMicrosoft · secedit /export\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/secedit-export\n\nMicrosoft · 4625(F): An account failed to log on\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/auditing/event-4625\n\nMicrosoft · What's new in Windows Server 2025\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/get-started/whats-new-windows-server-2025")], question: "What turns the tier rule from policy into enforcement?", answers: ["An email to administrators", "Deny-logon rights applied by GPO at each lower tier's OU", "Renaming the admin accounts"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The OU-linked deny rights are the mechanism: the directory itself refuses the out-of-tier sign-in.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What does Protected Users membership buy a Tier 0 account?", ["Faster sign-ins", "Exemption from password changes", "The domain refuses its weaker authentication paths, shrinking what a credential thief can replay"], 2, "Protected Users trades convenience for resistance — appropriate exactly for the accounts whose theft ends the network."), QuizQuestion("Why does a break-glass account exist outside the deny policies?", ["So a policy mistake cannot lock every administrator out — and its sealed, alarmed use keeps the exception honest", "Because every admin wants one", "Deny policies cannot apply to more than ten accounts"], 0, "Strict tiering needs one documented escape hatch. Sealing and alarming it is what keeps the hatch from becoming a habit."), QuizQuestion("Why do SBD-SUBCA01 and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 move out of the Servers OU before the deny policy is linked?", ["The Servers OU has reached its size limit", "They are Tier 0: the Servers OU now denies Tier 0 sign-ins, so they would lock Tier 0 out of its own systems", "Certificate Services cannot run in an OU with linked policies"], 1, "Tier follows control, not hostname. Systems that control Tier 0 need an OU where Tier 0 can sign in and the lower-tier denies never apply."), QuizQuestion("Why may Tier 0 still use a network logon on SBD-WSUS01 and SBD-EPO01?", ["Tier 0 manages the DC update ring and DC agent policy there, and a Kerberos network logon leaves no reusable credentials on the server", "Network logons are never logged", "Those two servers are Tier 0 systems"], 0, "Interactive, Remote Desktop, batch, and service logons leave reusable credentials behind; a Kerberos network logon does not, and Protected Users accounts cannot be delegated. The exception is narrow, named, and recorded.")], recap: ["Tier 0 servers have their own OU, so the deny policies never lock Tier 0 out.", "Lower tiers refuse higher-tier credentials by policy, with events to prove it; the console network-logon exception is recorded.", "Tier 0 accounts sit in Protected Users, backed by 2025's stricter defaults.", "Break-glass and MFA are written decisions, not loose ends."], nextStep: "Lesson 5 tests the whole plane — the paths that must work and the sign-ins that must fail.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/tiered-admin#logon-restrictions", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:tiered-admin:prove", title: "Prove the tiers and the addressing", minutes: 25, summary: "Run the matrix: every tier's administration works from its seat, every cross-tier attempt fails with an event, and the addressing service behaves exactly as registered.", objectives: ["Verify each tier's management path from its designated seat.", "Capture the denial evidence for out-of-tier attempts.", "Re-prove DHCP, relay, and dynamic DNS under the finished rules."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.4: the finished two-tier PKI with its publication site and templates, plus course 1.3's OU structure with the Admin OU in place. The network's routing and default-deny segmentation, with its access control list (ACL) register, are already in place.\n\nProvision Windows Server 2025 hosts for SBD-DHCP01 (10.0.20.10) and the three administration systems in VLAN 60: AdminJumpBox01 (.10), SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (.20), SBD-ADMIN-PC01 (.30).\n\nDecide the privileged accounts: separate admin identities per tier, never daily-driver accounts with admin rights."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the control plane before the fleet exists: DHCP for the workstation VLAN, and a tiered administration model where privileged sessions only ever start from dedicated hosts. The model's rule is strict — the jump systems administer; they browse nothing, read no mail, and accept no inbound sessions from user space."), LessonSection("Step 1", "From AdminJumpBox01, manage a DC and SBD-SUBCA01 with a Tier 0 account — the Tier 0 Servers OU kept that access; from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, manage SBD-DHCP01 with a Tier 1 account; from SBD-ADMIN-PC01, manage the VLAN 80 test client with a Tier 2 account. Record each success with its session details, including the target's Security event 4624 (and 4672, which marks a logon that received special privileges)."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Attempt the forbidden combinations — Tier 0 credentials at the test client, a session from VLAN 80 toward VLAN 60 — and capture both the refusal the user sees and the record the infrastructure writes: Security event 4625 with status 0xC000015B on the test client for the refused sign-in, and the router's deny-log entry for the VLAN 80 session."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Renew the test client's lease and confirm options, registration, and the relay still behave with all restrictions active; the control plane must not have broken the service plane."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Reconcile the tier access matrix against what you just proved and file it with the register — the two documents together are the admin plane's assessable record."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Enter-PSSession SBD-DHCP01.sbd.test   # from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, Tier 1 account\nipconfig /renew   # on the VLAN 80 test client\n# On the test client: refused sign-ins in the last hour (status 0xC000015B = logon type not granted).\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=4625; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddHours(-1)} | Where-Object Message -match '0xC000015B' | Select-Object TimeCreated, Id"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Every in-tier path succeeds, every tested out-of-tier path fails with captured evidence, and DHCP with dynamic DNS still works end to end under the full rule set."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the completed tier matrix with timestamps, the denial captures, and the post-restriction lease and registration proof."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Privileged access workstations and the access model\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/security/privileged-access-workstations/overview\n\nMicrosoft · 4625(F): An account failed to log on\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/auditing/event-4625\n\nMicrosoft · Get-WinEvent\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.diagnostics/get-winevent\n\nMicrosoft · Configure a firewall for Active Directory domains\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/config-firewall-for-ad-domains-and-trusts")], question: "What pairs make the denial evidence complete?", answers: ["Two screenshots of the same error", "The user-visible refusal plus the infrastructure's event or deny-log entry", "A verbal report"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Same standard as every boundary check: the client's view shows the attempt, the log shows the boundary deciding — together they prove enforcement.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why re-test DHCP after the restrictions landed?", ["Hardening the control plane can accidentally break the service plane — proving both still work is the point", "Leases expire during lessons", "The relay resets daily"], 0, "Every tightening risks collateral damage. The matrix ends with services proven healthy, not presumed."), QuizQuestion("What is the admin plane's assessable record?", ["The jump box's wallpaper", "A list of administrator names", "The tier access matrix reconciled with the ACL register"], 2, "Who may manage what, from where, over which path — matrix plus register answers it in a form an assessor can test.")], recap: ["In-tier works, out-of-tier fails, and both are on record.", "Addressing and registration survived the restrictions.", "Matrix and register reconcile as one assessable story."], nextStep: "The final lesson runs the scans and hands a managed, addressable network to course 1.6.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/tiered-admin#prove", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:tiered-admin:scan", title: "Delta scan and hand off", minutes: 20, summary: "Four new systems joined and the directory's logon policy changed — the cadence fires again. Baseline the newcomers, rescan what changed, and hand the next course a network that can finally be watched from somewhere safe.", objectives: ["Baseline the four new hosts and rescan the changed ones.", "Fold the course's decisions into the standing records.", "Write the handoff course 1.6 builds on."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.4: the finished two-tier PKI with its publication site and templates, plus course 1.3's OU structure with the Admin OU in place. The network's routing and default-deny segmentation, with its access control list (ACL) register, are already in place.\n\nProvision Windows Server 2025 hosts for SBD-DHCP01 (10.0.20.10) and the three administration systems in VLAN 60: AdminJumpBox01 (.10), SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (.20), SBD-ADMIN-PC01 (.30).\n\nDecide the privileged accounts: separate admin identities per tier, never daily-driver accounts with admin rights."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the control plane before the fleet exists: DHCP for the workstation VLAN, and a tiered administration model where privileged sessions only ever start from dedicated hosts. The model's rule is strict — the jump systems administer; they browse nothing, read no mail, and accept no inbound sessions from user space."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Run first baselines on SBD-DHCP01 and the three admin hosts with the standard benchmark and tool versions, and rescan SBD-SUBCA01 and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 — the move to the Tier 0 Servers OU is their change, and the rescan should show their hardening intact."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Add this course's rows to the living records: the deny-logon and Protected Users decisions into the exception record where they interact with STIG values, beside lesson 4's console network-logon exception, and the new permit and deny rows into the register with owners."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Confirm the admin hosts appear in the hardening course's quarterly cadence; privileged seats are rescanned first from now on, not last."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Write the handoff: administration now has a home, which means the logging course can require that SIEM consoles and collectors are managed only from VLAN 60 — with the DC-facing scopes from the Tier 0 seat and audit retention under the custodian's separate accounts — say so explicitly for course 1.6."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\tiered-admin -Recurse | Select-Object Name, LastWriteTime\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\tiered-admin\\*"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Four baselines and the Tier 0 server rescans are filed with attributed deltas, the exception record and register carry the new rows, and the handoff names the logging course's management constraint."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Finish the checklist: scan set, updated records, the tier matrix, and the handoff note."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "NIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Which systems are rescanned, not baselined, this time?", answers: ["SBD-SUBCA01 and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 — they moved to the Tier 0 Servers OU", "The brand-new admin hosts", "The offline root"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "New host, new baseline; changed host, rescan. The two Tier 0 servers changed OU, so the rescan proves the move kept their hardening.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why do the privileged seats move to the front of the quarterly cadence?", ["They have the fastest disks", "They carry the credentials that control everything else — drift there costs the most", "Scanners work alphabetically"], 1, "Scan priority follows consequence. The hosts that hold Tier 0 sessions earn the first look every quarter."), QuizQuestion("What does this course hand to course 1.6?", ["Fifty workstations", "The business services", "A safe management plane — collectors and SIEM consoles can now be administered only from VLAN 60"], 2, "Logging infrastructure is a target; building it after the admin plane means it is managed safely from day one.")], recap: ["Baselines, rescans, and attributed deltas are on file.", "Exception record and register absorbed the course's decisions.", "Course 1.6 inherits a managed, addressable network."], nextStep: "That completes course 1.5. Course 1.6 builds the logging pipeline — collect, protect, analyze — before the fleet arrives to be watched.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/tiered-admin#scan", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "logging-pipeline", number: "1.6", title: "See everything: the logging pipeline", subtitle: "Collect, protect & analyze before the fleet arrives", category: "Network build", description: "Build the model's collect → protect → analyze chain: Windows Event Forwarding and router syslog into SBD-SYSLOG01, hashed archives under a no-delete rule on SBD-AUDIT01, and working searches with one owned alert on SBD-SPLUNK01 — proven by a single event traced end to end.", icon: "monitoring", published: true, studyTime: "6 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 6,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "logging-pipeline-guide", title: "See everything: the logging pipeline · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Collect, protect & analyze before the fleet arrives", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/logging-pipeline", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "logging-pipeline-pdf", title: "See everything: the logging pipeline · Workbook PDF", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "16 pages · edition 1.3", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/logging-pipeline/SecureByDesign-logging-pipeline-Workbook.pdf", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "logging-pipeline-word", title: "See everything: the logging pipeline · Editable workbook (Word)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Make the workbook your own.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/logging-pipeline/SecureByDesign-logging-pipeline-Workbook.docx", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "logging-pipeline-checklist-page", title: "See everything: the logging pipeline · Printable checklist", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Print the configuration and evidence checklist.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/logging-pipeline/checklist", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "logging-pipeline-checklist", title: "See everything: the logging pipeline · Configuration checklist", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Download a Markdown checklist for your configuration and evidence.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/logging-pipeline/configuration-checklist.md", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:logging-pipeline:design", title: "Design collect, protect, analyze", minutes: 20, summary: "Three systems, three distinct jobs: SBD-SYSLOG01 gathers, SBD-AUDIT01 preserves, SBD-SPLUNK01 answers questions. Decide what is collected first, how long it lives, and who may touch which stage — before a single event flows.", objectives: ["Assign the three roles and the first collection scope.", "Decide retention, protection, and access per stage.", "Register the pipeline's network paths."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.5: the admin plane exists, and this course's systems are managed only from VLAN 60.\n\nProvision SBD-SYSLOG01 (10.0.20.20) as Windows Server 2025, SBD-SPLUNK01 (10.0.50.10) on Linux joined to the sbd.test domain with its time service (chrony or ntpd) pointed at both domain controllers, and SBD-AUDIT01 (10.0.50.40) as Windows Server 2025 joined to the domain.\n\nHave the network's service-path register ready for the new rows: event forwarding to the collector, syslog from the core router, and the forwarder path into the analysis system."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the model's collect → protect → analyze chain before the fleet arrives, so every later system is observed from its first boot. Collection is configuration you can show; protection is permissions and retention you can defend; analysis is a search someone actually runs."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Write the role sentence for each host: the collector receives Windows events and the core router's syslog; the repository holds the protected, long-retention copy; the analysis system indexes a working copy for search and alerting. One copy to keep, one copy to question."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Scope the first collection to what already produces evidence: both DCs' Security logs, the core router's deny log, and the admin hosts' sign-in events — the fleet joins this scope in the next course, not now."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Decide the AU-family answers and write them down: retention length per stage, who may read each stage, and that nobody — including administrators — writes or deletes in the repository outside the archival process (that is the AU-9 protection story)."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Add the register rows: DC and admin-host event forwarding to the collector (WinRM 5985), router syslog UDP 514 to the collector, and the forwarder path from collector to the analysis system; all three systems are managed only from VLAN 60 per course 1.5's handoff."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Capture the before state: what the DCs currently audit and where nothing is forwarded yet.\nauditpol /get /category:* | Select-String 'Logon|Account Management|Directory Service|Policy Change'\nGet-Service Wecsvc -ComputerName SBD-SYSLOG01"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The design names each stage's job, scope, retention, and readers; the repository's no-delete rule is written; and the register carries the three pipeline paths with owners."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the pipeline design sheet, the retention and access table, and the new register rows."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Use Windows Event Forwarding to help with intrusion detection\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/threat-protection/use-windows-event-forwarding-to-assist-in-intrusion-detection\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (AU family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why separate the repository from the analysis system?", answers: ["The protected record and the working copy have different jobs — tampering with searches must never mean tampering with evidence", "Splunk cannot store data", "Two systems are cheaper than one"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Analysis needs flexibility; the record needs integrity. Splitting them means a compromised search tier still leaves the evidence intact.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What is in the first collection scope?", ["Every system including the unbuilt fleet", "The sources that already produce evidence: DC Security logs, the router's deny log, and admin-host sign-ins", "Only the collector's own log"], 1, "Collect what exists and matters most. The fleet widens the scope in course 1.7 — into a pipeline already proven."), QuizQuestion("Who may delete from SBD-AUDIT01?", ["Any Domain Admin", "The analysis system automatically", "Nobody outside the written archival process — that restriction is the protection control"], 2, "AU-9 in one sentence: the record survives its administrators. Deletion happens only by the documented retention process.")], recap: ["Collector gathers, repository preserves, analysis answers.", "Scope starts with the DCs, the router denies, and the admin plane.", "Retention, readers, and the no-delete rule are written; paths are registered."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 makes sure the right events exist before anything forwards them.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/logging-pipeline#design", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:logging-pipeline:auditing", title: "Turn on the right auditing", minutes: 25, summary: "Forwarding garbage faster is not monitoring. Set the advanced audit policy so the events that tell the network's story — sign-ins, account changes, directory changes, policy changes — actually get written.", objectives: ["Apply the advanced audit policy categories the design needs.", "Reconcile audit settings with the course 1.3 Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) values.", "Verify effective auditing on the DCs and admin hosts."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.5: the admin plane exists, and this course's systems are managed only from VLAN 60.\n\nProvision SBD-SYSLOG01 (10.0.20.20) as Windows Server 2025, SBD-SPLUNK01 (10.0.50.10) on Linux joined to the sbd.test domain with its time service (chrony or ntpd) pointed at both domain controllers, and SBD-AUDIT01 (10.0.50.40) as Windows Server 2025 joined to the domain.\n\nHave the network's service-path register ready for the new rows: event forwarding to the collector, syslog from the core router, and the forwarder path into the analysis system."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the model's collect → protect → analyze chain before the fleet arrives, so every later system is observed from its first boot. Collection is configuration you can show; protection is permissions and retention you can defend; analysis is a search someone actually runs."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Review what the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Group Policy objects (GPOs) from course 1.3 already audit — much of the advanced audit policy arrived with the STIGs. This lesson reconciles and fills gaps rather than starting over."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Confirm the categories the design depends on are at least at the STIG values on the DCs: Logon/Logoff, Account Management, Directory Service Changes, and Policy Change — success and failure where the design says so."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Extend the same policy to the admin hosts' OU so privileged sessions write the sign-in trail lesson 5 of course 1.5 started capturing by hand."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Any place this course wants more auditing than the STIG sets, make it a documented addition in the GPO structure — never a local, invisible tweak — and note it in the exception/addition record."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "auditpol /get /subcategory:'Logon','User Account Management','Directory Service Changes','Audit Policy Change'\ngpresult /r /scope:computer"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Effective audit policy on the DCs and admin hosts covers the designed categories at or above the STIG values, applied through GPO, with any additions documented."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the auditpol output per host class, the GPO report, and the documented additions."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Use Windows Event Forwarding to help with intrusion detection\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/threat-protection/use-windows-event-forwarding-to-assist-in-intrusion-detection\n\nMicrosoft · Auditpol\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/auditpol")], question: "Why start from the course 1.3 GPOs instead of a fresh audit policy?", answers: ["Fresh policies are forbidden", "The STIGs already set most of it — reconciling avoids conflicts and double-maintenance", "auditpol cannot show existing settings"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The hardening baseline was the first word on auditing. This lesson's job is alignment and gaps, not a rival policy.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Where do audit-policy additions get made?", ["In the GPO structure, documented — never as local tweaks", "Directly in each server's local policy", "In the collector's configuration"], 0, "Local tweaks drift and hide. Policy through GPO keeps the audit posture visible, scanned, and reproducible."), QuizQuestion("Which event families does the design treat as the core story?", ["Print spooler events", "Disk defragmentation logs", "Sign-ins, account management, directory changes, and policy changes"], 2, "Who authenticated, who changed an account, who touched the directory, who altered policy — that is the identity network's narrative.")], recap: ["Audit policy is reconciled with the STIG baseline, not reinvented.", "DCs and admin hosts write the designed event families.", "Additions are GPO-visible and documented."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 builds the collector those events will flow to.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/logging-pipeline#auditing", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:logging-pipeline:collector", title: "Build the collector", minutes: 30, summary: "SBD-SYSLOG01 becomes the funnel: Windows Event Forwarding brings the DC and admin-host events in by subscription, and the core router's denies arrive as syslog — one host where the network's record first lands.", objectives: ["Configure the Windows Event Collector service and subscription.", "Point the source hosts at the collector by GPO.", "Receive the router's syslog deny stream."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.5: the admin plane exists, and this course's systems are managed only from VLAN 60.\n\nProvision SBD-SYSLOG01 (10.0.20.20) as Windows Server 2025, SBD-SPLUNK01 (10.0.50.10) on Linux joined to the sbd.test domain with its time service (chrony or ntpd) pointed at both domain controllers, and SBD-AUDIT01 (10.0.50.40) as Windows Server 2025 joined to the domain.\n\nHave the network's service-path register ready for the new rows: event forwarding to the collector, syslog from the core router, and the forwarder path into the analysis system."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the model's collect → protect → analyze chain before the fleet arrives, so every later system is observed from its first boot. Collection is configuration you can show; protection is permissions and retention you can defend; analysis is a search someone actually runs."), LessonSection("Step 1", "On SBD-SYSLOG01, enable the Windows Event Collector service and create a source-initiated subscription for the designed event IDs from the DCs and admin hosts, delivered into the Forwarded Events log."), LessonSection("Step 2", "By GPO on the relevant OUs, set the subscription manager to the collector and grant the Network Service read access to the Security log — the sources push; the collector never reaches into them."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Add a syslog intake on the collector for UDP 514 and point the core router's deny logging at it, so the network boundary's decisions land beside the Windows record."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Confirm the register rows are the only paths in use: WinRM from the source OUs to the collector, syslog from the core, and nothing listening that the design didn't ask for."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "wecutil es\nwecutil gs SBD-ForwardedSecurity\nGet-WinEvent -LogName ForwardedEvents -MaxEvents 5 | Select-Object TimeCreated, Id, MachineName"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The subscription is active, events from both DCs and the admin hosts appear in Forwarded Events with correct source machine names, and router deny entries arrive on the syslog intake."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record the subscription configuration, the GPO that enrolls sources, the first forwarded events per source, and a received router deny entry."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Use Windows Event Forwarding to help with intrusion detection\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/threat-protection/use-windows-event-forwarding-to-assist-in-intrusion-detection\n\nMicrosoft · Wecutil (Windows Event Collector)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wecutil")], question: "Why source-initiated subscriptions rather than the collector pulling?", answers: ["Pulling is impossible on Windows", "Sources push to one known collector, so no account with read-everything rights roams the network", "Push is the only mode GPO supports"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Collector-initiated collection needs a credential that can read every source — exactly the kind of credential tiering just eliminated.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What joins the Windows events on this host?", ["Internet threat feeds", "The workstation fleet's browsing history", "The core router's deny log over syslog"], 2, "The boundary's refusals are evidence too. Landing them beside the sign-in record lets analysis correlate both views."), QuizQuestion("How do you know a forwarded event really came from DC02?", ["The MachineName on the event record in Forwarded Events", "All events look identical", "The collector renames events randomly"], 0, "Forwarded events keep their source identity — which is what makes the central copy usable as a record at all.")], recap: ["Sources push by subscription; the collector reaches into nothing.", "DC, admin-host, and router records land in one place.", "Only registered paths carry the pipeline."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 gives the record its protected home.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/logging-pipeline#collector", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:logging-pipeline:protect", title: "Protect the record on SBD-AUDIT01", minutes: 25, summary: "The repository is where the record outlives mistakes and attackers alike: scheduled archives from the collector, hashed on arrival, readable by few, deletable by process only.", objectives: ["Archive the collector's logs to the repository on schedule.", "Hash and catalog every archive on arrival.", "Enforce the read-few, delete-by-process-only access model."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.5: the admin plane exists, and this course's systems are managed only from VLAN 60.\n\nProvision SBD-SYSLOG01 (10.0.20.20) as Windows Server 2025, SBD-SPLUNK01 (10.0.50.10) on Linux joined to the sbd.test domain with its time service (chrony or ntpd) pointed at both domain controllers, and SBD-AUDIT01 (10.0.50.40) as Windows Server 2025 joined to the domain.\n\nHave the network's service-path register ready for the new rows: event forwarding to the collector, syslog from the core router, and the forwarder path into the analysis system."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the model's collect → protect → analyze chain before the fleet arrives, so every later system is observed from its first boot. Collection is configuration you can show; protection is permissions and retention you can defend; analysis is a search someone actually runs."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Create the archival job: on schedule, the collector exports its Forwarded Events and syslog intake to dated archive files and transfers them to SBD-AUDIT01's repository share — the only inbound write path the repository allows."), LessonSection("Step 2", "On arrival, hash each archive and append the hash to a catalog kept with the archives; an archive whose hash no longer matches is a finding, not a file."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Set the permissions to match lesson 1's decision: the archival process writes, a named auditors group reads, and no interactive account holds delete rights — retention-driven deletion runs as its own documented, logged process, administered only under the audit custodian's separate accounts."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Fill in the AU table with what now exists: where each stage's data lives, its retention clock, and who can touch it — this table is the kind of artifact course 2.2 shows joining the Body of Evidence for the audit controls."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ChildItem \\\\SBD-AUDIT01\\LogArchive | Select-Object Name, Length, LastWriteTime -First 5\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 \\\\SBD-AUDIT01\\LogArchive\\*.evtx | Select-Object -First 3\nicacls \\\\SBD-AUDIT01\\LogArchive"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Dated archives arrive on schedule with catalog hashes that verify, the permission listing matches the access model exactly, and the AU table reflects reality."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the archival job definition, the hash catalog excerpt, the permissions output, and the completed AU table."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Wecutil (Windows Event Collector)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wecutil\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (AU family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "What does the hash catalog actually protect against?", answers: ["Disk space exhaustion", "Silent modification — an archive that no longer matches its recorded hash announces itself as tampered or corrupted", "Slow searches"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Integrity is only provable if you recorded what intact looked like. The catalog is that record.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("How does anything ever get deleted from the repository?", ["Any administrator's right-click", "The analysis system prunes it", "Only the documented, logged retention process"], 2, "The point of the repository is surviving its administrators. Deletion exists — as a process with a log, never a privilege."), QuizQuestion("Where does the AU table go after this course?", ["Into the Body of Evidence for the audit controls — course 2.2 shows how artifacts like it are organized", "Back into course 1.1, retroactively", "Nowhere — it is decorative"], 0, "Where the records live, how long, and who reads them is precisely what the audit control family asks an assessor to verify.")], recap: ["Archives land on schedule and prove their own integrity.", "Readers are named; deletion is a process, not a right.", "The AU table is written for the authorization path."], nextStep: "Lesson 5 puts a working copy in front of an analyst.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/logging-pipeline#protect", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:logging-pipeline:analyze", title: "Analyze in SBD-SPLUNK01", minutes: 30, summary: "Analysis earns its place with three working searches and one alert: failed privileged sign-ins, the boundary's denies, and the health of time — questions this network can already answer about itself.", objectives: ["Index the collector's streams into the analysis system.", "Build the first three searches against real events.", "Create one alert with a named responder."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.5: the admin plane exists, and this course's systems are managed only from VLAN 60.\n\nProvision SBD-SYSLOG01 (10.0.20.20) as Windows Server 2025, SBD-SPLUNK01 (10.0.50.10) on Linux joined to the sbd.test domain with its time service (chrony or ntpd) pointed at both domain controllers, and SBD-AUDIT01 (10.0.50.40) as Windows Server 2025 joined to the domain.\n\nHave the network's service-path register ready for the new rows: event forwarding to the collector, syslog from the core router, and the forwarder path into the analysis system."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the model's collect → protect → analyze chain before the fleet arrives, so every later system is observed from its first boot. Collection is configuration you can show; protection is permissions and retention you can defend; analysis is a search someone actually runs."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Install the analysis platform on SBD-SPLUNK01 and bring in the collector's two streams — the forwarded Windows events and the router syslog — over the registered forwarder path; the repository stays out of this flow by design."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Build search one: failed sign-ins (event 4625) involving the tier admin accounts, grouped by source host — the tiering course's denials now have a dashboard."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Build search two: router denies by source VLAN and destination port — the network boundary, now trending; and search three: time-service events and offsets, so clock drift surfaces before Kerberos complains."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Turn the first search into an alert with a threshold, a named responder from the admin plane, and a written first-response step — an alert nobody owns is a log entry with extra steps. Console access to all of this: VLAN 60 only, per the handoff."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Test-NetConnection SBD-SPLUNK01.sbd.test -Port 9997\n# In the analysis console, confirm the three saved searches return today's events\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security';Id=4625} -MaxEvents 3 | Select-Object TimeCreated, MachineName"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Both streams index with correct timestamps, the three searches return real current events, and the alert fires in a test with its responder and first step documented."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record the input configuration, each search with a result sample, and the alert definition with its responder."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Use Windows Event Forwarding to help with intrusion detection\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/threat-protection/use-windows-event-forwarding-to-assist-in-intrusion-detection\n\nSplunk · product documentation\nhttps://docs.splunk.com/Documentation")], question: "Why does the repository stay out of the analysis flow?", answers: ["The protected copy's integrity never depends on the search tier — analysis reads the working stream, not the record", "Splunk cannot read archives", "Repositories are read-only hardware"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Lesson 1's separation, now visible in the plumbing: question the working copy freely; the record stays untouched.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What makes an alert real rather than decorative?", ["A red icon", "A named responder and a written first step", "Firing at least hourly"], 1, "Detection without ownership is noise. The responder and the first step are what turn a search into a control."), QuizQuestion("Why is a time-health search in the first three?", ["Clocks are easy to chart", "Dashboards need a third panel", "Drift breaks Kerberos quietly — course 1.2 set up the clock, this search watches it"], 2, "The pipeline watches the dependencies the network actually has. Time is one failure that looks like everything else until you chart it.")], recap: ["Two streams indexed; the protected record untouched.", "Three searches answer real questions from day one.", "One alert, one responder, one written first step."], nextStep: "The final lesson proves the chain end to end and runs the scans.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/logging-pipeline#analyze", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:logging-pipeline:prove", title: "Prove the chain and delta scan", minutes: 20, summary: "One deliberate event, followed all the way: a failed privileged sign-in should appear on the source, the collector, the repository's next archive, and the analyst's screen — then the cadence scans close the course.", objectives: ["Trace a test event through all three stages.", "Verify the router-deny stream the same way.", "Baseline the new hosts and file the course evidence."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.5: the admin plane exists, and this course's systems are managed only from VLAN 60.\n\nProvision SBD-SYSLOG01 (10.0.20.20) as Windows Server 2025, SBD-SPLUNK01 (10.0.50.10) on Linux joined to the sbd.test domain with its time service (chrony or ntpd) pointed at both domain controllers, and SBD-AUDIT01 (10.0.50.40) as Windows Server 2025 joined to the domain.\n\nHave the network's service-path register ready for the new rows: event forwarding to the collector, syslog from the core router, and the forwarder path into the analysis system."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the model's collect → protect → analyze chain before the fleet arrives, so every later system is observed from its first boot. Collection is configuration you can show; protection is permissions and retention you can defend; analysis is a search someone actually runs."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Generate the test event: one failed sign-in on a DC with a wrong password for a tier admin account. Find it in the DC's Security log, in Forwarded Events on the collector, flag it for the next archive's verification on the repository, and watch the alert fire in analysis."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Repeat with the boundary: probe one unregistered port, then find that deny in the syslog stream and in search two — the boundary's evidence pair — attempt and deny — now assembles itself."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Run the cadence: first baselines for SBD-SYSLOG01, SBD-AUDIT01, and SBD-SPLUNK01 (Windows hosts with the standard benchmark; SBD-SPLUNK01 runs Linux, so scan it with the DISA STIG benchmark for its distribution), plus a DC rescan for the audit-policy deltas."), LessonSection("Step 4", "File the end-to-end trace as this course's signature evidence and write the handoff: the fleet course inherits a pipeline that will see its first domain join happen."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='ForwardedEvents'; Id=4625; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddHours(-1)} | Select-Object TimeCreated, MachineName\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\logging-pipeline -Recurse | Select-Object Name, LastWriteTime\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\logging-pipeline\\*"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The test event appears at every stage with consistent timestamps, the deny trace matches, the new hosts have baselines, and the DC rescan deltas are attributed to the audit policy."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the four-stage trace of the test event, the deny trace, the scan set, and the handoff note to course 1.7."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Wecutil (Windows Event Collector)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wecutil\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (AU family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "What does the single traced event prove that stage-by-stage checks don't?", answers: ["That events have timestamps", "That the stages connect — one real event surviving source to analyst is the pipeline working as a system", "That 4625 exists"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Components can each pass while the chain fails. The traced event is the integration test.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("How is SBD-SPLUNK01, a domain-joined Linux server, scanned in this lab?", ["It is exempt from evidence", "With the Windows Server benchmark anyway", "With the DISA STIG benchmark for its Linux distribution, on the same cadence as the Windows servers"], 2, "Linux distributions have their own DISA STIGs and Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) content. A domain-joined Linux server is held to the same scan cadence as every other server."), QuizQuestion("Why insist the pipeline exists before the fleet course?", ["So fifty domain joins happen in view of the pipeline — observed from the first boot instead of retrofitted later", "Fleets cannot join without Splunk", "DHCP requires a SIEM"], 0, "The ordering argument of this whole path: build the watcher, then the watched. Course 1.7 arrives to a network already paying attention.")], recap: ["One event, four stages, consistent timestamps — the chain is real.", "Boundary denies assemble their own evidence now.", "Baselines, rescans, and the handoff to course 1.7 close the course."], nextStep: "That completes course 1.6. Course 1.7 brings in the fifty-workstation fleet — watched, addressed, and policy-managed from first boot.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/logging-pipeline#prove", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "workstation-fleet", number: "1.7", title: "Bring in the fleet", subtitle: "50 Windows 11 endpoints, managed from first boot", category: "Network build", description: "Join the fifty-workstation fleet to a network that is ready for it: DHCP addressing, OU landing with STIG policy at first boot, Windows LAPS for every local administrator, certificate autoenrollment at scale, and a sampled proof with the whole rollout observed by the logging pipeline.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "7 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 7,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "workstation-fleet-guide", title: "Bring in the fleet · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "50 Windows 11 endpoints, managed from first boot", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "workstation-fleet-pdf", title: "Bring in the fleet · Workbook PDF", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "18 pages · edition 1.3", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet/SecureByDesign-workstation-fleet-Workbook.pdf", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "workstation-fleet-word", title: "Bring in the fleet · Editable workbook (Word)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Make the workbook your own.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet/SecureByDesign-workstation-fleet-Workbook.docx", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "workstation-fleet-checklist-page", title: "Bring in the fleet · Printable checklist", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Print the configuration and evidence checklist.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet/checklist", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "workstation-fleet-checklist", title: "Bring in the fleet · Configuration checklist", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Download a Markdown checklist for your configuration and evidence.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet/configuration-checklist.md", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:workstation-fleet:prepare", title: "Prepare the fleet rollout", minutes: 30, summary: "Fifty of anything needs a factory, not fifty craft projects. Settle the image, the names, the join account, and the policy links so every endpoint is born identical — then prove it on paper before proving it on metal.", objectives: ["Finalize the reference image and naming plan for fifty endpoints.", "Create a least-privilege join account scoped to the Workstations OU.", "Link the Windows 11 STIG policies and the workstation firewall policy to the Workstations OU before any join."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.4–1.6: working PKI services, DHCP with its relay, the tiered admin plane, and the logging pipeline, on the network's routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nPrepare a patched Windows 11 reference image (or template) for the fifty endpoints, obtained through the approved offline media process.\n\nConfirm the Workstations OU, the container redirects from course 1.3, and the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Windows 11 Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) from the course 1.3 package are ready to link.\n\nConfirm the VLAN 80 access switch supports 802.1X port authentication, including a monitor (audit) mode that logs each result without blocking traffic. No new server is needed: the Network Policy Server (NPS) role goes on SBD-DHCP01."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Fifty endpoints join a network that is finally ready for them: addressed by DHCP, landed by OU redirect, hardened by policy at first boot, certified by autoenrollment, and watched by the pipeline from their first sign-in. The sample names run SBD-PC01 through SBD-PC50; match them to your own plan."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Finalize the Windows 11 reference image: current patches via the approved media process, no preinstalled extras, and a generalized state so each clone takes its own identity. Record the image version and hash — it is the fleet's common ancestor and belongs in evidence."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Write the naming and rollout plan: SBD-PC01–SBD-PC50, addressed by the course 1.5 DHCP scope, joined in waves (pilot of three, then batches) rather than all fifty at once."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Create a dedicated domain-join account delegated only the create-computer-objects right on the Workstations OU — no broader privilege rides along with fifty join operations. The course 1.3 redirect already guarantees machines land in that OU."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Link the DISA Windows 11 STIG policies from the course 1.3 package to the Workstations OU now, so the very first pilot boots into the hardened state — and note that Windows 11's own secure defaults will already satisfy some checks, just as Server 2025 did."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Create the workstation firewall GPO (SBD Workstation Firewall in this sample) on the Workstations OU: Windows Defender Firewall on for every profile with inbound traffic blocked by default, and inbound rules that allow SMB (TCP 445), Remote Desktop (TCP 3389), and remote management (TCP 5985–5986) only from SBD-ADMIN-PC01 at 10.0.60.30 — course 1.8 adds SBD-NESSUS01 for credentialed scans. No rule allows SMB or Remote Desktop between workstations: traffic inside VLAN 80 never reaches the router, so this policy, not the core router's rules, enforces the register's peer denies."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Staging\\Win11-Reference.vhdx\nGet-ADOrganizationalUnit -Identity 'OU=Workstations,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test'\nGet-GPInheritance -Target 'OU=Workstations,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test'\nGet-GPOReport -Name 'SBD Workstation Firewall' -ReportType Html -Path C:\\Evidence\\workstation-fleet\\workstation-firewall.html"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The image is hashed and recorded, the join account holds only the delegated right on the Workstations OU, and the STIG and firewall GPO links show on that OU before any machine exists in it."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the image record with its hash, the naming and wave plan, the delegation report for the join account, the OU's GPO inheritance output, and the firewall GPO report."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "DISA · STIG Group Policy Objects package\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/gpo\n\nMicrosoft · Configure Windows Firewall rules with Group Policy\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/operating-system-security/network-security/windows-firewall/configure\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why does the join account get only create-computer rights on one OU?", answers: ["Join operations fail with more rights", "Fifty joins should carry the least privilege possible — a stolen join credential then creates computer objects, nothing more", "Delegation is faster than Domain Admin"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Scale multiplies exposure. Scoping the account bounds what the rollout's most-used credential is worth to an attacker.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why link the STIG policies before the first machine joins?", ["So every endpoint's first boot is already the hardened state — no window where an unmanaged machine exists", "GPO links expire if unused", "The OU cannot be created otherwise"], 0, "The redirect lands them in the OU, and the pre-linked policy means compliance from minute one — the course 1.3 design, now paying off at scale."), QuizQuestion("What makes the reference image part of the evidence?", ["Images are always evidence", "It contains the domain password", "Its recorded version and hash are what make fifty 'identical' endpoints a claim you can verify"], 2, "A configuration record for the fleet starts with the common ancestor. The hash is how an assessor — or future you — confirms descent."), QuizQuestion("What stops one workstation from opening SMB or Remote Desktop to another?", ["The router's access control lists", "Each PC's own firewall, set by the workstation firewall GPO — traffic inside VLAN 80 never reaches the router", "Nothing until course 1.8"], 1, "Two PCs in VLAN 80 talk through the access switch, so the router never sees their traffic. Each PC's Windows Defender Firewall enforces the register's peer denies, which Inside the Network shows as rules D01 and D02.")], recap: ["One hashed image, one naming plan, waves instead of a big bang.", "The join credential can create computer objects and nothing else.", "The Workstations OU is armed with policy before it holds a machine.", "Peer SMB and Remote Desktop are blocked by each PC's own firewall, set by policy before the first join."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 joins the pilot and inspects everything a first boot should bring.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet#prepare", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:workstation-fleet:pilot", title: "Join the pilot and inspect first boot", minutes: 30, summary: "Three machines go first, and everything the path has built shows up at once: a DHCP lease through the relay, an OU landing, STIG policy, a DNS record — and the join itself appearing on the analyst's screen.", objectives: ["Join a three-machine pilot with the scoped account.", "Verify lease, OU placement, policy, and DNS registration per machine.", "Confirm the joins appear in the logging pipeline."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.4–1.6: working PKI services, DHCP with its relay, the tiered admin plane, and the logging pipeline, on the network's routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nPrepare a patched Windows 11 reference image (or template) for the fifty endpoints, obtained through the approved offline media process.\n\nConfirm the Workstations OU, the container redirects from course 1.3, and the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Windows 11 Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) from the course 1.3 package are ready to link.\n\nConfirm the VLAN 80 access switch supports 802.1X port authentication, including a monitor (audit) mode that logs each result without blocking traffic. No new server is needed: the Network Policy Server (NPS) role goes on SBD-DHCP01."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Fifty endpoints join a network that is finally ready for them: addressed by DHCP, landed by OU redirect, hardened by policy at first boot, certified by autoenrollment, and watched by the pipeline from their first sign-in. The sample names run SBD-PC01 through SBD-PC50; match them to your own plan."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Deploy SBD-PC01 through SBD-PC03 from the image into VLAN 80, let each take its DHCP lease, and join sbd.test with the delegated account — nothing about the join should require a privileged credential."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Per machine, verify the inheritance chain: the computer object sits in the Workstations OU (the redirect working), gpresult shows the STIG policies applied, and the secure dynamic DNS record exists on both DCs."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Confirm the deny side holds: a pilot machine cannot reach another pilot machine's SMB or RDP — the lesson 1 firewall policy refuses it on the destination PC, because traffic inside VLAN 80 never reaches the router — and cannot open anything toward VLAN 60, which the core router's rules deny at the boundary."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Find the three joins in the logging pipeline: the domain controller events for the computer-account creations should already be on the collector and searchable in analysis — the first machines this network has watched arrive."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ADComputer -SearchBase 'OU=Workstations,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' -Filter * | Select-Object Name, DNSHostName\ngpresult /r /scope:computer\nResolve-DnsName SBD-PC01.sbd.test\nTest-NetConnection SBD-PC02.sbd.test -Port 445   # expected: TcpTestSucceeded False"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "All three pilots sit in the Workstations OU with STIG policy applied and DNS registered, peer SMB/RDP fails as designed, and the join events are visible in analysis."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep each pilot's placement, gpresult, and DNS proof; the peer-deny output; and the analysis search showing the three computer-account creations."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Configure certificate autoenrollment\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/core-network-guide/cncg/server-certs/configure-server-certificate-autoenrollment\n\nDISA · STIG Group Policy Objects package\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/gpo\n\nMicrosoft · Use Windows Event Forwarding to help with intrusion detection\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/threat-protection/use-windows-event-forwarding-to-assist-in-intrusion-detection\n\nMicrosoft · Configure Windows Firewall rules with Group Policy\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/operating-system-security/network-security/windows-firewall/configure")], question: "How does a brand-new machine end up hardened without anyone touching it?", answers: ["Windows 11 hardens itself completely", "Redirect lands it in the Workstations OU, where the pre-linked STIG policies apply at first boot", "The DHCP server pushes the STIG"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Three courses cooperate in that one moment: the 1.3 redirect, the 1.3 GPO package, and this lesson's pre-linked OU.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why test pilot-to-pilot SMB and expect failure?", ["SMB is removed from Windows 11", "The pilots are in different domains", "Each PC's firewall policy denies peer SMB and Remote Desktop — lateral movement inside VLAN 80 is cut at the destination host"], 2, "Peer traffic is switched inside VLAN 80 and never reaches the router, so the register's peer denies (D01 and D02) are enforced by each PC's own firewall — the policy lesson 1 linked before any join."), QuizQuestion("What does seeing the joins in analysis demonstrate?", ["The ordering worked — the pipeline built in 1.6 observed the fleet's very first machines arriving", "That Splunk licenses cover 50 hosts", "Nothing; joins are not logged"], 0, "Build the watcher, then the watched. The pilot's appearance on the analyst's screen is the sequencing argument made visible.")], recap: ["Three pilots: leased, landed, hardened, registered — untouched by hand.", "Peer traffic fails by design; the denies are the fleet's first evidence.", "The pipeline watched the fleet being born."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 gives every endpoint a managed local administrator.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet#pilot", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:workstation-fleet:laps", title: "Manage local administrators with Windows LAPS", minutes: 25, summary: "Fifty identical machines must not share one local administrator password. Windows Local Administrator Password Solution (Windows LAPS) gives each endpoint its own rotating secret, stored in the directory, readable by the people the tier model says may have it.", objectives: ["Prepare the directory schema and permissions for Windows LAPS.", "Apply the LAPS policy to the Workstations OU.", "Retrieve and rotate a managed password under the tier rules."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.4–1.6: working PKI services, DHCP with its relay, the tiered admin plane, and the logging pipeline, on the network's routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nPrepare a patched Windows 11 reference image (or template) for the fifty endpoints, obtained through the approved offline media process.\n\nConfirm the Workstations OU, the container redirects from course 1.3, and the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Windows 11 Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) from the course 1.3 package are ready to link.\n\nConfirm the VLAN 80 access switch supports 802.1X port authentication, including a monitor (audit) mode that logs each result without blocking traffic. No new server is needed: the Network Policy Server (NPS) role goes on SBD-DHCP01."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Fifty endpoints join a network that is finally ready for them: addressed by DHCP, landed by OU redirect, hardened by policy at first boot, certified by autoenrollment, and watched by the pipeline from their first sign-in. The sample names run SBD-PC01 through SBD-PC50; match them to your own plan."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Extend the directory schema for Windows LAPS (Update-LapsADSchema) and grant the Workstations OU's computers the right to update their own password attributes — a one-time Tier 0 action, performed from the jump host and recorded like any schema change."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Grant read access on the managed passwords to the Tier 2 admin group only — exactly the people SBD-ADMIN-PC01 exists for — and nobody else; the tier model decides who may know an endpoint's local secret."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Create the LAPS GPO on the Workstations OU: managed built-in administrator, the password complexity and age the plan sets, and post-authentication reset so a used password does not live on."), LessonSection("Step 4", "On a pilot, confirm the policy applied, retrieve the password from the admin seat, use it once, and watch rotation — one cycle, fully observed, is the proof the other forty-nine inherit."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-LapsADPassword -Identity SBD-PC01 -AsPlainText   # from SBD-ADMIN-PC01, Tier 2 admin\nGet-ADComputer SBD-PC01 -Properties msLAPS-PasswordExpirationTime | Select-Object Name, msLAPS-PasswordExpirationTime\ngpresult /r /scope:computer"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The schema holds the LAPS attributes, only the Tier 2 group can read passwords, the policy shows on the pilots, and one retrieve-use-rotate cycle is recorded."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the schema-change record, the permission delegation, the LAPS policy report, and the observed rotation cycle."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Windows LAPS overview\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/laps/laps-overview\n\nMicrosoft · What's new in Windows Server 2025\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/get-started/whats-new-windows-server-2025")], question: "What problem does LAPS remove from a fifty-machine fleet?", answers: ["Slow sign-ins", "One shared local administrator password — where a single capture would open every endpoint", "Password complexity rules"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Identical images tempt identical secrets. Per-machine rotating passwords make one compromised endpoint worth exactly one endpoint.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Who may read a managed endpoint password?", ["Any authenticated user", "The machine's primary user", "The Tier 2 admin group, from its admin seat — the tier model decides"], 2, "LAPS storage is directory data; the reading right is a tiering decision, which course 1.5 already made."), QuizQuestion("Why does post-authentication reset matter?", ["A password that was just used stops being valid — whoever saw it holds an expired secret", "It makes passwords longer", "It disables the account"], 0, "The rotation on use closes the window between 'retrieved for support' and 'floating around in a notes file.'")], recap: ["Every endpoint owns its own rotating local secret.", "Reading rights follow the tier model, from the Tier 2 seat.", "One full cycle is observed and on record."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 hands the fleet its certificates.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet#laps", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:workstation-fleet:certificates", title: "Autoenroll the fleet's certificates", minutes: 25, summary: "Course 1.4 proved autoenrollment on a pilot group; this lesson turns it loose. Widen the template permission to the fleet, let policy do the enrolling, and verify a machine certificate chains and checks revocation across the boundary.", objectives: ["Widen the computer template's autoenrollment to the fleet.", "Confirm hands-free enrollment at Group Policy refresh.", "Verify chain and live revocation from an endpoint."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.4–1.6: working PKI services, DHCP with its relay, the tiered admin plane, and the logging pipeline, on the network's routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nPrepare a patched Windows 11 reference image (or template) for the fifty endpoints, obtained through the approved offline media process.\n\nConfirm the Workstations OU, the container redirects from course 1.3, and the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Windows 11 Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) from the course 1.3 package are ready to link.\n\nConfirm the VLAN 80 access switch supports 802.1X port authentication, including a monitor (audit) mode that logs each result without blocking traffic. No new server is needed: the Network Policy Server (NPS) role goes on SBD-DHCP01."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Fifty endpoints join a network that is finally ready for them: addressed by DHCP, landed by OU redirect, hardened by policy at first boot, certified by autoenrollment, and watched by the pipeline from their first sign-in. The sample names run SBD-PC01 through SBD-PC50; match them to your own plan."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Through the course 1.4 change process, update the SBD Computer Authentication template's autoenrollment permission from the pilot group to the Workstations OU's computers — the change record names the widening and its approver."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Confirm the autoenrollment policy already applies through the Workstations OU, refresh policy on the pilots, and watch certificates arrive with no human in the loop."), LessonSection("Step 3", "On an enrolled endpoint, run the full verification: the chain must reach the approved root, and the AIA and CDP fetches must answer live from pki.sbd.test through the register's HTTP row."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Record the issuing CA's view: the issued-certificate log now shows the fleet enrolling — volume the CA was designed for, finally arriving, and itself a record worth sampling into evidence."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "gpupdate /force\nGet-ChildItem Cert:\\LocalMachine\\My | Select-Object Subject, NotAfter\ncertutil -verify -urlfetch C:\\Evidence\\fleet\\SBD-PC01.cer"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Pilot endpoints enroll hands-free at policy refresh, verification passes with live publication retrieval, and the CA's issued log reflects the fleet with the widened template version."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the template change record, an endpoint's certificate details, the urlfetch output, and a sample of the CA's issued log."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Configure certificate autoenrollment\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/core-network-guide/cncg/server-certs/configure-server-certificate-autoenrollment\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "What governs widening the template from pilot to fleet?", answers: ["The course 1.4 change process — requester, reviewer, versioning, record", "A quick edit on the CA console", "Nothing; templates are self-service"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The pilot-then-widen plan was written into the template's own record. Scaling it is a change like any other — documented, not improvised.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What does the endpoint's urlfetch verification exercise, end to end?", ["Only the local certificate store", "Template, CA, DNS alias, publication site, and the registered HTTP path — five courses in one command", "The DHCP relay"], 1, "One passing verification on a fleet machine is the integration test for everything the PKI path built."), QuizQuestion("Why sample the CA's issued-certificate log into evidence?", ["Logs expire otherwise", "The CA deletes it weekly", "What the CA actually signed — and under which template version — is the PKI's ground-truth record"], 2, "Enrollment at scale is where template mistakes would surface. The issued log is where you would see it, so it joins the record.")], recap: ["The widening went through the change process, not around it.", "Enrollment is hands-free and verified live across the boundary.", "The CA's issued log joins the evidence."], nextStep: "Lesson 5 puts those certificates to work at the switch port, starting in monitor mode.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet#certificates", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:workstation-fleet:nac", title: "Admit only enrolled devices with 802.1X", minutes: 30, summary: "Until now, anything plugged into a VLAN 80 port got an address. With 802.1X, the switch asks each machine to prove who it is with the certificate it enrolled. This lesson turns that check on in monitor mode — every result is logged, nothing is blocked yet — because 47 PCs have no certificate until they join the domain; lesson 6 enforces it once the whole fleet has enrolled. No new server: the NPS role joins DHCP on SBD-DHCP01.", objectives: ["Add the Network Policy Server (NPS) role to SBD-DHCP01 for port authentication.", "Configure Extensible Authentication Protocol–Transport Layer Security (EAP-TLS) with machine certificates on the VLAN 80 access ports, in monitor mode.", "Prove the pilots authenticate and that NPS logs every result."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.4–1.6: working PKI services, DHCP with its relay, the tiered admin plane, and the logging pipeline, on the network's routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nPrepare a patched Windows 11 reference image (or template) for the fifty endpoints, obtained through the approved offline media process.\n\nConfirm the Workstations OU, the container redirects from course 1.3, and the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Windows 11 Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) from the course 1.3 package are ready to link.\n\nConfirm the VLAN 80 access switch supports 802.1X port authentication, including a monitor (audit) mode that logs each result without blocking traffic. No new server is needed: the Network Policy Server (NPS) role goes on SBD-DHCP01."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Fifty endpoints join a network that is finally ready for them: addressed by DHCP, landed by OU redirect, hardened by policy at first boot, certified by autoenrollment, and watched by the pipeline from their first sign-in. The sample names run SBD-PC01 through SBD-PC50; match them to your own plan."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Add the Network Policy Server role to SBD-DHCP01 and register it in Active Directory. Give it a server certificate with the Server Authentication purpose from the issuing CA. It handles port admission only; router logins stay named local accounts, so the server stays in the member-server tier."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Add the VLAN 80 access switch as a RADIUS client with a long, unique shared secret, then create the network policy: wired 802.1X, EAP-TLS only, machine certificates that chain to the approved root and carry the Client Authentication purpose, from the Domain Computers group."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Create quarantine VLAN 90 with no gateway and no routes and set it as the ports' failure and guest VLAN, but enable 802.1X on the VLAN 80 access ports in monitor (audit) mode: the switch runs every authentication and reports the result, yet keeps forwarding on VLAN 80 whatever the outcome (vendors name this mode differently). The other 47 PCs have no machine certificate until they join the domain in lesson 6, so enforcing now would strand them in VLAN 90, with no route to a domain controller. Push the wired 802.1X client settings to the Workstations OU by Group Policy (Wired AutoConfig, EAP-TLS, the approved root as the trusted CA)."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Prove monitor mode with the pilots: each authenticates with EAP-TLS and NPS logs a grant (event 6272), while a failed attempt is logged — as event 6273 when the request reaches NPS — but still forwards on VLAN 80. Add register row F20 with its owner, and confirm the NPS events reach the course 1.6 collector through the existing event forwarding subscription."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On an enrolled pilot: the wired interface authenticated with EAP-TLS.\nnetsh lan show interfaces\n# On SBD-DHCP01: recent NPS grants (6272) and denials (6273).\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=6272,6273} -MaxEvents 5 | Select-Object TimeCreated, Id"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Every pilot authenticates with EAP-TLS and NPS logs the grant (6272); a failed authentication is logged but, in monitor mode, the port still forwards on VLAN 80; the ports name VLAN 90 as their failure VLAN; and the NPS events appear in the collector."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the network policy export, the RADIUS client list (without the secret), the monitor-mode port configuration, the pilots' grant events and the failed test, and the F20 register row."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Network Policy Server overview\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/nps/nps-top\n\nMicrosoft · Configure RADIUS clients\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/nps/nps-radius-clients-configure\n\nMicrosoft · Certificate requirements for EAP-TLS\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/certificate-requirements-eap-tls-peap")], question: "Once 802.1X is enforced, what lets a workstation onto a VLAN 80 port?", answers: ["Knowing a shared password", "Its machine certificate, checked by NPS through EAP-TLS", "Being plugged in before the switch boots"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "EAP-TLS proves the device holds the private key for a certificate the approved CA issued. No certificate, no access.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Once enforcement is on in lesson 6, where does an unknown device end up?", ["Quarantine VLAN 90, which has no routes", "VLAN 80, with a warning", "The domain controller VLAN, so it can enroll"], 0, "The failure VLAN gives an unknown device a place to be that leads nowhere: VLAN 90 has no gateway, so nothing it sends is routed."), QuizQuestion("Why does NPS on SBD-DHCP01 handle port admission only, not router logins?", ["NPS cannot authenticate people", "Router logins need a separate license", "If it authenticated the router's administrators, it could control Tier 0 and would have to be managed as Tier 0"], 2, "Anything that can control a Tier 0 system is Tier 0. Keeping NPS to port admission keeps SBD-DHCP01 in the member-server tier."), QuizQuestion("Why does this lesson start 802.1X in monitor mode instead of enforcing it?", ["Monitor mode is faster", "The other 47 PCs have no machine certificate until they join the domain; enforcing now would strand them in VLAN 90, where they could never reach a domain controller", "NPS cannot enforce on wired ports"], 1, "A PC enrolls its certificate only after it joins, and it can join only if the port lets it reach a domain controller. Monitor mode proves the configuration with the pilots without locking the rest of the fleet out.")], recap: ["802.1X runs on every VLAN 80 port in monitor mode: every result is logged, nothing is blocked yet.", "The pilots authenticate with machine certificates; enforcement waits until the whole fleet has enrolled.", "No new server: NPS runs on SBD-DHCP01, and its decisions are logged with everything else."], nextStep: "Lesson 6 scales from three machines to fifty, then switches the ports to enforcement.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet#nac", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:workstation-fleet:scale", title: "Scale to fifty, enforce 802.1X, and prove by sample", minutes: 30, summary: "Roll the remaining waves while the ports are still in monitor mode, confirm every PC enrolled its machine certificate and authenticates, then switch the ports to enforcement — and prove the fleet the only honest way at scale: a defined sample, the same checks as the pilot, and a written rule for what a sample failure means.", objectives: ["Roll out the remaining endpoints in planned waves.", "Confirm fleet-wide enrollment, then enforce 802.1X and prove an unenrolled device is quarantined.", "Verify a defined sample against the pilot's full check set and widen event forwarding to the fleet."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.4–1.6: working PKI services, DHCP with its relay, the tiered admin plane, and the logging pipeline, on the network's routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nPrepare a patched Windows 11 reference image (or template) for the fifty endpoints, obtained through the approved offline media process.\n\nConfirm the Workstations OU, the container redirects from course 1.3, and the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Windows 11 Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) from the course 1.3 package are ready to link.\n\nConfirm the VLAN 80 access switch supports 802.1X port authentication, including a monitor (audit) mode that logs each result without blocking traffic. No new server is needed: the Network Policy Server (NPS) role goes on SBD-DHCP01."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Fifty endpoints join a network that is finally ready for them: addressed by DHCP, landed by OU redirect, hardened by policy at first boot, certified by autoenrollment, and watched by the pipeline from their first sign-in. The sample names run SBD-PC01 through SBD-PC50; match them to your own plan."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Deploy the remaining machines in the planned waves while the VLAN 80 ports are still in monitor mode, watching the DHCP scope's utilization and the join events arriving in analysis as each wave lands — the pipeline is now the rollout's progress bar."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Confirm every PC enrolled its machine certificate and authenticates: compare the issuing CA's issued certificates and the NPS grant events (6272) against the fifty computer objects, and fix any machine that is missing before going further."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Switch the VLAN 80 access ports from monitor mode to enforcement, and prove it both ways: an enrolled PC still authenticates and gets its normal lease, and an unenrolled test laptop lands in quarantine VLAN 90 and reaches nothing. Record the switch's port status and the NPS events for both."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Define the sample before checking it: for example one machine per wave plus any machine that logged an error — then run the pilot's full set on each sampled host (OU, policy, DNS, LAPS, certificate, 802.1X admission, peer-deny)."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Write the sample rule down: one failed check in the sample widens the sample; a repeated failure stops the rollout and becomes a finding. A sample without a failure rule is a formality."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Extend the course 1.6 forwarding subscription to the Workstations OU so all fifty endpoints report their security events — and confirm fleet sign-ins now appear in the analyst's searches."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "(Get-ADComputer -SearchBase 'OU=Workstations,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' -Filter *).Count\n# On SBD-DHCP01: authentication results in the last hour (6272 granted, 6273 denied).\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=6272,6273; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddHours(-1)} | Group-Object Id | Select-Object Name, Count\nGet-DhcpServerv4ScopeStatistics -ScopeId 10.0.80.0 | Select-Object PercentageInUse, AddressesInUse\nGet-WinEvent -LogName ForwardedEvents -MaxEvents 5 | Select-Object MachineName, Id"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Fifty computer objects sit in the Workstations OU, every PC enrolled and authenticates, the ports enforce 802.1X with the unenrolled laptop quarantined in VLAN 90, scope utilization matches the rollout, every sampled host passes the full check set, and fleet events flow through the pipeline."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the wave log, the enrollment reconciliation, the enforcement change with both admission results and their records, the sample definition with its failure rule, the per-sample check results, and the widened subscription proof."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Configure certificate autoenrollment\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/core-network-guide/cncg/server-certs/configure-server-certificate-autoenrollment\n\nMicrosoft · Use Windows Event Forwarding to help with intrusion detection\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/threat-protection/use-windows-event-forwarding-to-assist-in-intrusion-detection\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final\n\nMicrosoft · Network Policy Server overview\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/nps/nps-top")], question: "Why prove the fleet by sample instead of checking all fifty by hand?", answers: ["A defined sample with a failure rule is verifiable and honest at scale — and identical provenance is what the image hash bought you", "Checking is impossible on Windows 11", "Only pilots can be checked"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The factory model earns the sampling model. The written failure rule is what keeps the sample from being theater.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What turns a sampled failure into more than a shrug?", ["Deleting the failed machine", "The written rule: failures widen the sample, repeats stop the rollout and become findings", "Rebooting twice"], 1, "The rule was written before the checks ran — which is exactly what makes acting on it defensible."), QuizQuestion("What is the pipeline's role during the rollout itself?", ["It pauses to save disk", "It only matters after completion", "Live progress and anomaly surface — each wave's joins and errors appear as they happen"], 2, "Course 1.6's investment turns deployment into something observed, not something reconstructed afterward."), QuizQuestion("When is it safe to switch the VLAN 80 ports from monitor mode to enforcement?", ["As soon as the first pilot authenticates", "Once every PC has enrolled its machine certificate and authenticates in monitor mode", "Never; monitor mode is enough"], 1, "Enforce only after the whole fleet has proven it authenticates. Then a missing certificate cannot strand a machine in VLAN 90, and every unknown device that appears later is quarantined.")], recap: ["Fifty machines, rolled in waves, watched as they landed.", "Every PC enrolled before enforcement; an unenrolled device now lands in VLAN 90.", "A defined sample with a failure rule carried the proof, and the fleet reports into the pipeline."], nextStep: "The final lesson runs the fleet's scans and hands off to course 1.8.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet#scale", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:workstation-fleet:scan", title: "Delta scan the fleet and hand off", minutes: 20, summary: "The cadence meets its first population: Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) baselines on the sampled endpoints with the Windows 11 benchmark, rescans where the course changed infrastructure, and a fleet inventory that course 1.8's tooling will take over.", objectives: ["Baseline sampled endpoints with the Windows 11 STIG benchmark.", "Rescan the infrastructure this course touched.", "Hand the fleet inventory to course 1.8's management tooling."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.4–1.6: working PKI services, DHCP with its relay, the tiered admin plane, and the logging pipeline, on the network's routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nPrepare a patched Windows 11 reference image (or template) for the fifty endpoints, obtained through the approved offline media process.\n\nConfirm the Workstations OU, the container redirects from course 1.3, and the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Windows 11 Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) from the course 1.3 package are ready to link.\n\nConfirm the VLAN 80 access switch supports 802.1X port authentication, including a monitor (audit) mode that logs each result without blocking traffic. No new server is needed: the Network Policy Server (NPS) role goes on SBD-DHCP01."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Fifty endpoints join a network that is finally ready for them: addressed by DHCP, landed by OU redirect, hardened by policy at first boot, certified by autoenrollment, and watched by the pipeline from their first sign-in. The sample names run SBD-PC01 through SBD-PC50; match them to your own plan."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Run the SCAP Compliance Checker (SCC) with the Windows 11 STIG benchmark against the sampled endpoints — the same sample set as lesson 6 — and note how much the pre-linked policies and the OS defaults already satisfy, exactly as the server baselines taught."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Rescan what changed: the DCs (schema and LAPS attributes), the issuing CA (fleet enrollment volume), and SBD-DHCP01 (the new NPS role and a scope in daily use); attribute each delta to its lesson."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Assemble the fleet inventory — names, image ancestry, certificate thumbprints, sample status — as the course's closing artifact; course 1.8's management tooling will take ownership of keeping it current."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Write the handoff: the fleet exists, watched and hardened, but updates itself not at all — patching, endpoint protection, and vulnerability coverage are precisely what course 1.8 builds next."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\workstation-fleet -Recurse | Select-Object Name, LastWriteTime\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\workstation-fleet\\*"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Sampled endpoints hold dated Windows 11 baselines, the infrastructure rescans carry attributed deltas, and the fleet inventory is complete and filed."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Finish the checklist: endpoint baselines, infrastructure rescans, the fleet inventory, and the handoff note to course 1.8."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "DISA · STIG Group Policy Objects package\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/gpo\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Which benchmark do the endpoints get?", answers: ["Windows Server 2025, close enough", "The router's config export", "The Windows 11 STIG benchmark — the fleet's own standard from the same DISA package"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The GPO package staged in course 1.3 carried the endpoint STIGs too; the fleet is scanned against its own content.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why does the issuing CA get a rescan now?", ["Fifty enrollments changed its operational posture — change-triggered means this too", "CAs are rescanned weekly by rule", "It was missed earlier"], 0, "The cadence follows change wherever it lands. Enrollment at fleet scale is a change worth measuring."), QuizQuestion("What does the fleet still lack as this course closes?", ["Certificates and policy", "Updates, endpoint protection, and vulnerability coverage — course 1.8's entire agenda", "Network addresses"], 1, "Honest handoffs name the gap. Fifty hardened machines that never patch would drift out of the state just proven — which is why 1.8 is next.")], recap: ["Endpoint baselines by sample; infrastructure deltas attributed.", "The fleet inventory is the closing artifact.", "The gap — patching and protection — is named for course 1.8."], nextStep: "That completes course 1.7. Course 1.8 keeps the fleet alive: updates, approved software, endpoint protection, and the scanner that watches for weakness.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet#scan", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "patch-protect", number: "1.8", title: "Patch, protect, and watch for weakness", subtitle: "WSUS rings, approved sources, ePO & credentialed scanning", category: "Network build", description: "Keep the proven state from drifting: Windows updates through WSUS rings fed across the air gap, one approved source per software type with SBD-REPO01 for packages, a living inventory on SBD-MGMT01, managed endpoint protection through SBD-EPO01, and SBD-NESSUS01 turning the scan cadence into routine.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "6 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 6,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "patch-protect-guide", title: "Patch, protect, and watch for weakness · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "WSUS rings, approved sources, ePO & credentialed scanning", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/patch-protect", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "patch-protect-pdf", title: "Patch, protect, and watch for weakness · Workbook PDF", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "16 pages · edition 1.3", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/patch-protect/SecureByDesign-patch-protect-Workbook.pdf", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "patch-protect-word", title: "Patch, protect, and watch for weakness · Editable workbook (Word)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Make the workbook your own.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/patch-protect/SecureByDesign-patch-protect-Workbook.docx", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "patch-protect-checklist-page", title: "Patch, protect, and watch for weakness · Printable checklist", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Print the configuration and evidence checklist.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/patch-protect/checklist", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "patch-protect-checklist", title: "Patch, protect, and watch for weakness · Configuration checklist", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Download a Markdown checklist for your configuration and evidence.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/patch-protect/configuration-checklist.md", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:patch-protect:design", title: "Design the rings and the software rules", minutes: 20, summary: "Decide how change reaches the network before any of it does: update rings that try things on the expendable before the essential, one approved source per software type, and register rows for every new path.", objectives: ["Define the update rings and their order.", "Set the one-approved-source rule per software type.", "Register the course's service paths."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.7: the fleet exists, inventoried and reporting into the logging pipeline.\n\nProvision SBD-WSUS01 (10.0.20.30), SBD-REPO01 (10.0.70.20, Linux), SBD-MGMT01 (10.0.70.10), SBD-EPO01 (10.0.50.30), and SBD-NESSUS01 (10.0.50.20) per the model's placements.\n\nKeep the approved-media process ready: update content, package mirrors, protection definitions, and scanner plugins all cross the air gap the same reviewed, hashed way."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course keeps the proven state from drifting: updates and software arrive only through controlled channels, every endpoint runs managed protection, and a credentialed scanner turns the course 1.3 cadence into routine. In an air-gapped lab, every one of these systems feeds on content carried across the boundary — which is the recurring lesson."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Define three rings and their order: a pilot ring (the course 1.7 sample machines plus one member server), the broad ring (fleet and member servers), and the critical ring last (domain controllers and both VLAN 30 hosts) — updates earn their way up, never down."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Write the software rule: Windows updates come only from SBD-WSUS01, Linux packages only from SBD-REPO01, protection definitions only through SBD-EPO01, and anything else arrives as a documented exception — one source per type is what makes 'unauthorized software' a checkable claim."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Decide the air-gap feeding cadence: what content crosses on media, how often, hashed by whom — the course 1.3 transfer procedure, now on a schedule."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Add the register rows: every Windows system to WSUS (8530/8531), domain controllers included; the domain-joined Linux servers (SBD-SPLUNK01 and SBD-NESSUS01) to REPO01 (HTTP, GPG-signed packages); every Windows system's agent to Trellix ePolicy Orchestrator (ePO) on SBD-EPO01 on its agent-server secure port, TCP 443, with the agent starting each connection; and the scanner's credentialed reach into each VLAN (SMB 445 and WMI to Windows, SSH 22 to Linux) — the one deliberate wide permit, owned and logged."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Capture the before state: where machines currently look for updates (nowhere internal yet).\nGet-ItemProperty 'HKLM:\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue\nGet-ADComputer -Filter * -SearchBase 'OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' | Measure-Object"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The ring table names every system's ring, the one-source rule and feeding cadence are written, and the register carries the course's rows with owners."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the ring assignments, the software source rules, the feeding cadence, and the new register rows."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Windows Server Update Services (WSUS)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-server-update-services/get-started/windows-server-update-services-wsus\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why do domain controllers sit in the last ring?", answers: ["They update fastest", "A bad update should burn a pilot machine, not the identity fabric — criticality decides order", "DCs cannot be updated"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Rings are a blast-radius design: change proves itself on what the network can lose before touching what it cannot.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What does one-approved-source-per-type actually buy?", ["Cheaper licensing", "Faster downloads", "'Unauthorized software' becomes checkable — anything not from the named source is a finding by definition"], 2, "A rule you can test is a control; a preference you cannot is a hope. One source per type draws the testable line."), QuizQuestion("Which register row in this course is deliberately wide, and what disciplines it?", ["The scanner's credentialed reach — owned, logged, and justified as the cadence's instrument", "The fleet's path to WSUS", "There are no wide rows"], 0, "A vulnerability scanner must reach broadly to be worth having. The register makes that breadth a documented decision, not an accident.")], recap: ["Pilot → broad → critical: updates earn their way up.", "One named source per software type, exceptions documented.", "Every path — including the scanner's wide one — is a register row."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 builds the Windows update channel.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/patch-protect#design", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:patch-protect:wsus", title: "Build SBD-WSUS01 and the ring policies", minutes: 30, summary: "WSUS becomes the only road Windows updates travel: content imported across the air gap, approvals per ring, and Group Policy objects (GPOs) that point every OU at the server — with a pilot ring that proves each approval first.", objectives: ["Install WSUS and import content through the offline process.", "Map rings to WSUS groups and OU-linked policies.", "Approve and verify a first update cycle through the pilot ring."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.7: the fleet exists, inventoried and reporting into the logging pipeline.\n\nProvision SBD-WSUS01 (10.0.20.30), SBD-REPO01 (10.0.70.20, Linux), SBD-MGMT01 (10.0.70.10), SBD-EPO01 (10.0.50.30), and SBD-NESSUS01 (10.0.50.20) per the model's placements.\n\nKeep the approved-media process ready: update content, package mirrors, protection definitions, and scanner plugins all cross the air gap the same reviewed, hashed way."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course keeps the proven state from drifting: updates and software arrive only through controlled channels, every endpoint runs managed protection, and a credentialed scanner turns the course 1.3 cadence into routine. In an air-gapped lab, every one of these systems feeds on content carried across the boundary — which is the recurring lesson."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Install the WSUS role on SBD-WSUS01, scope its products and classifications to what the network actually runs — Windows Server 2025, Windows 11, and defender definitions — and nothing speculative. Microsoft announced in September 2024 that WSUS is deprecated and no longer actively developed, but it is still included in Windows Server 2025 and supported for production deployments, with security and quality updates under the product lifecycle."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Feed it offline: export the needed content on the connected side, hash it, carry it across, and import — the WSUS offline servicing path is the course 1.3 media procedure wearing an update hat."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Create WSUS computer groups matching the rings, and link ring GPOs at the OUs: update source SBD-WSUS01, the ring's group assignment, and install/restart windows that respect what each ring does for a living. The critical ring's GPO links to the Domain Controllers and Tier 0 Servers OUs."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Approve one update cycle for the pilot ring only, watch it install and report, then promote the approval ring by ring — the critical ring installs only what two rings already survived. Pilot and broad-ring approvals are Tier 1 work from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01; critical-ring approvals for the domain controllers and the two Tier 0 servers are Tier 0 work from AdminJumpBox01. Add SBD-WSUS01 to the SBD Tier 0 Console Hosts group and restart it so the new membership applies: the console reaches the server as a Kerberos network logon, the one Tier 0 logon type course 1.5's deny policy leaves open there, as its exception record states."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-WsusServer | Get-WsusComputer -ComputerTargetGroups 'Ring 1 - Pilot' | Select-Object FullDomainName, LastReportedStatusTime\ngpresult /r /scope:computer\nGet-WindowsUpdateLog -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Every system reports to its ring's group, the pilot ring installed and reported the first approved cycle, and the promotion to later rings followed the written order."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record the product scope, the offline import with hashes, the ring GPO reports, and the first cycle's per-ring approval and install timeline."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Windows Server Update Services (WSUS)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-server-update-services/get-started/windows-server-update-services-wsus\n\nMicrosoft · Features removed or no longer developed in Windows Server\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/get-started/removed-deprecated-features-windows-server\n\nDISA · SRG/STIG tools (SCC and STIG Viewer)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/srg-stig-tools")], question: "How does update content reach this WSUS server?", answers: ["Direct from Microsoft Update over the internet", "By the approved offline export-import process — hashed media across the air gap", "It generates updates locally"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The air gap holds for updates too. WSUS's offline servicing path exists for exactly this network shape.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What gates an update's arrival at the domain controllers?", ["The calendar date alone", "The DC's own download speed", "Survival in the pilot and broad rings first — approval promotes ring by ring"], 2, "The ring design from lesson 1 becomes mechanics here: the critical ring installs only proven approvals."), QuizQuestion("Why scope products and classifications tightly?", ["Unused content is imported, stored, and reviewed for nothing — and every artifact crossing the boundary should earn its trip", "WSUS breaks above ten products", "Licensing forbids more"], 0, "In an air-gapped lab, every megabyte is carried by hand. Scope is both hygiene and respect for the boundary process.")], recap: ["Updates travel one road: offline import, ring approvals, OU policies.", "The pilot ring absorbs the risk; the critical ring inherits the proof.", "The first full cycle is on record, ring by ring."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 gives the Linux side its equivalent road.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/patch-protect#wsus", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:patch-protect:repo", title: "Build SBD-REPO01 for approved packages", minutes: 20, summary: "The lab's Linux hosts and tooling need packages, and the rule from lesson 1 applies to them too: one internal repository, fed across the boundary, serving only what was reviewed in.", objectives: ["Stand up the internal package repository on SBD-REPO01.", "Mirror the approved package set through the offline process.", "Point the Linux consumers at the repository and nothing else."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.7: the fleet exists, inventoried and reporting into the logging pipeline.\n\nProvision SBD-WSUS01 (10.0.20.30), SBD-REPO01 (10.0.70.20, Linux), SBD-MGMT01 (10.0.70.10), SBD-EPO01 (10.0.50.30), and SBD-NESSUS01 (10.0.50.20) per the model's placements.\n\nKeep the approved-media process ready: update content, package mirrors, protection definitions, and scanner plugins all cross the air gap the same reviewed, hashed way."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course keeps the proven state from drifting: updates and software arrive only through controlled channels, every endpoint runs managed protection, and a credentialed scanner turns the course 1.3 cadence into routine. In an air-gapped lab, every one of these systems feeds on content carried across the boundary — which is the recurring lesson."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Build SBD-REPO01 per your Linux platform plan and serve a repository directory over HTTP at 10.0.70.20 — the register row from lesson 1 already permits the path from its consumers."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Mirror the approved package set on the connected side, hash the set, carry it across, and verify before publishing — same ceremony, different payload; record the repository's snapshot date as its version."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Configure each Linux consumer (the analysis platform's host if Linux, the scanner appliance's feeds where applicable) to use only the internal repository — remove or disable every external source definition so the one-source rule is configuration, not convention."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Document the repository's own administration under the tier model — managed from VLAN 60, changes recorded — and its assessable record: the hashed snapshot catalog, like the router's config export."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Test-NetConnection 10.0.70.20 -Port 80\n# On a Linux consumer: confirm the only configured source is the internal repository\n# dnf repolist  (or the platform equivalent) — capture the output into the evidence set\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\patch-protect\\repo-snapshot-catalog.txt"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The repository answers on its registered path, consumers list the internal source and only the internal source, and the snapshot catalog with hashes is filed."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the snapshot date and hash catalog, the consumer source listings, and the repository's administration record."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Red Hat · product documentation\nhttps://docs.redhat.com/en\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "What is the repository's version, for evidence purposes?", answers: ["The hashed snapshot date of its mirrored content", "The server's uptime", "The newest package's name"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "A mirror is a moment in time. The dated, hashed snapshot is what lets anyone say exactly which content the lab could install.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("How is the one-source rule enforced on a Linux consumer?", ["By asking administrators nicely", "External source definitions are removed or disabled — the configuration itself allows only the internal repository", "By firewall rules alone"], 1, "Both layers help, but the consumer's own configuration is the primary control — and the thing the scan or config review verifies."), QuizQuestion("What stands as this host's assessable record?", ["A screenshot of the login page", "Nothing — Linux hosts are exempt", "The hashed snapshot catalog and administration record — the router pattern again"], 2, "No Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) content for a role does not mean no evidence: verified configuration and content catalogs carry the proof.")], recap: ["One internal repository, fed by the same boundary ceremony.", "Consumers can only see the approved source — by configuration.", "The dated, hashed snapshot is the version of record."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 gives the inventory a keeper.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/patch-protect#repo", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:patch-protect:mgmt", title: "SBD-MGMT01 and the living inventory", minutes: 25, summary: "The course 1.7 fleet inventory was a snapshot; SBD-MGMT01 makes it a living record — every system, its ring, its sources, its last-seen state — maintained from the management seat the model placed in VLAN 70.", objectives: ["Stand up the management tooling host under the tier model.", "Build the component inventory covering all modeled systems.", "Define how the inventory stays current and who owns it."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.7: the fleet exists, inventoried and reporting into the logging pipeline.\n\nProvision SBD-WSUS01 (10.0.20.30), SBD-REPO01 (10.0.70.20, Linux), SBD-MGMT01 (10.0.70.10), SBD-EPO01 (10.0.50.30), and SBD-NESSUS01 (10.0.50.20) per the model's placements.\n\nKeep the approved-media process ready: update content, package mirrors, protection definitions, and scanner plugins all cross the air gap the same reviewed, hashed way."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course keeps the proven state from drifting: updates and software arrive only through controlled channels, every endpoint runs managed protection, and a credentialed scanner turns the course 1.3 cadence into routine. In an air-gapped lab, every one of these systems feeds on content carried across the boundary — which is the recurring lesson."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Build SBD-MGMT01 in the Servers OU, administered from VLAN 60 per the tier rules, with your chosen configuration and inventory tooling — the record matters more than the brand."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Load the inventory with every system the network now has — the 1.7 fleet list, the servers, the admin plane, the router — each with its OU, VLAN, address, ring, software sources, and scan status: the CM-8 component inventory, in one place."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Define currency: joins and retirements update the inventory as part of their process, and a monthly reconciliation compares the inventory against the directory and the DHCP leases — drift between them is a finding."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Name the owner and file the inventory's first reconciliation as evidence; course 2.2 shows how a record like this one joins the Body of Evidence for the configuration-management controls."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ADComputer -Filter * -SearchBase 'OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' | Measure-Object\nGet-DhcpServerv4Lease -ScopeId 10.0.80.0 | Measure-Object\n# Compare both counts against the inventory's totals and record the reconciliation"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The inventory lists every modeled system with its attributes, the first directory-and-DHCP reconciliation matches (or its mismatches are findings), and ownership and cadence are written."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the populated inventory export, the reconciliation record, and the ownership and cadence note."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "NIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "What makes an inventory 'living' rather than a stale spreadsheet?", answers: ["Pretty formatting", "Update-on-change plus a scheduled reconciliation against sources that cannot lie — the directory and the leases", "Monthly printing"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Processes keep it current; reconciliation proves it stayed current. Both are written down, so both are checkable.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("A machine appears in DHCP leases but not the inventory. What is that?", ["Normal churn to ignore", "A reason to restart DHCP", "A finding — either an undocumented system or a broken process, and both matter"], 2, "The reconciliation exists to surface exactly this. Unknown hardware on the network is the classic first question of any assessment."), QuizQuestion("Which control family consumes this record most directly?", ["Configuration management — CM-8's component inventory is literally this artifact", "Physical security", "Awareness training"], 0, "The authorization path's CM controls ask 'what do you have and how do you know' — this lesson is the answer being built.")], recap: ["One record of everything, with ring, sources, and scan status.", "Reconciliation against directory and leases keeps it honest.", "Owned, scheduled, and ready to join the Body of Evidence."], nextStep: "Lesson 5 puts managed protection on every endpoint.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/patch-protect#mgmt", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:patch-protect:epo", title: "Endpoint protection through SBD-EPO01", minutes: 30, summary: "Protection becomes a managed fact, not fifty local settings: the ePO server deploys agents ring by ring, policy comes from one console, and the fleet's protection state is something the dashboard can simply show.", objectives: ["Stand up the ePO server and its agent deployment.", "Apply the protection and DLP policy baseline by ring.", "Verify coverage and wire detections into the pipeline."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.7: the fleet exists, inventoried and reporting into the logging pipeline.\n\nProvision SBD-WSUS01 (10.0.20.30), SBD-REPO01 (10.0.70.20, Linux), SBD-MGMT01 (10.0.70.10), SBD-EPO01 (10.0.50.30), and SBD-NESSUS01 (10.0.50.20) per the model's placements.\n\nKeep the approved-media process ready: update content, package mirrors, protection definitions, and scanner plugins all cross the air gap the same reviewed, hashed way."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course keeps the proven state from drifting: updates and software arrive only through controlled channels, every endpoint runs managed protection, and a credentialed scanner turns the course 1.3 cadence into routine. In an air-gapped lab, every one of these systems feeds on content carried across the boundary — which is the recurring lesson."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Build SBD-EPO01 in VLAN 50, fed its definitions through the offline process on the lesson-1 cadence, with its console reachable only from the admin plane — the register row from lesson 1 carries the agents' check-ins, and with the agent's peer-to-peer and RelayServer features turned off, endpoints open no listener for the server (TCP 8081, UDP 8082)."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Deploy agents to the pilot ring first, confirm check-ins, then follow the ring order through the fleet, the member servers, and the critical ring — the same earn-your-way-up discipline as updates. Agent policy for the domain controllers is a Tier 0 scope in ePO, changed only from AdminJumpBox01 with a Tier 0 account. Add SBD-EPO01 to the SBD Tier 0 Console Hosts group and restart it, so course 1.5's recorded network-logon exception covers that console."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Apply the policy baseline: on-access protection, the scan schedule, and the data-loss-prevention policy scoped to what the model's DLP role calls for — each setting a decision in the record, not a default accepted silently."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Verify coverage against the living inventory — every endpoint present and checking in — and forward the server's threat events into the course 1.6 pipeline so detections land beside everything else; a standard test file makes the whole path fire once, on record."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Test-NetConnection SBD-EPO01.sbd.test -Port 443\nGet-Service -ComputerName SBD-PC01 -Name 'masvc' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Select-Object Status\nGet-WinEvent -LogName ForwardedEvents -MaxEvents 5 | Select-Object TimeCreated, MachineName"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Every inventoried endpoint shows a checking-in agent, the policy baseline is applied and recorded, and a test detection traveled from endpoint to console to the analyst's search."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the coverage-vs-inventory comparison, the policy baseline record, and the traced test detection."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Trellix · product documentation\nhttps://docs.trellix.com/\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "What answers 'is every endpoint protected'?", answers: ["Asking the help desk", "The console's coverage reconciled against the living inventory — agents checking in, compared to machines that exist", "The install media's label"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Coverage is a comparison, not a feeling. Lesson 4's inventory is the denominator that makes the claim checkable.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why do protection detections flow into the 1.6 pipeline?", ["One place to look: detections correlate with sign-ins and boundary denies on the same analyst screen", "The console cannot store events", "Syslog is faster than consoles"], 0, "A detection next to the surrounding sign-ins and denies tells a story; a detection alone tells a fact."), QuizQuestion("How do definition updates reach this server?", ["Live from the vendor cloud", "They never update in a lab", "On the written offline cadence — the same hashed-media ceremony as everything else"], 2, "Protection content ages fast, which is why the feeding cadence was a lesson-1 decision with a schedule, not an afterthought.")], recap: ["Agents by ring, policy from one console, console from the admin plane.", "Coverage is proven against the inventory, not assumed.", "Detections join the pipeline — one screen tells the whole story."], nextStep: "The final lesson adds the scanner and makes the cadence routine.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/patch-protect#epo", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:patch-protect:nessus", title: "SBD-NESSUS01 and the standing cadence", minutes: 25, summary: "The course 1.3 cadence grows its instrument: credentialed scans per VLAN on schedule, findings flowing into the pipeline and the Plan of Action and Milestones (POA&M) habit, and this course's five new systems getting their own baselines on the way out.", objectives: ["Stand up the scanner with least-privilege credentialed access.", "Run the first full-network credentialed scan and triage it.", "Fold scanning into the standing cadence and close the course's scans."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.7: the fleet exists, inventoried and reporting into the logging pipeline.\n\nProvision SBD-WSUS01 (10.0.20.30), SBD-REPO01 (10.0.70.20, Linux), SBD-MGMT01 (10.0.70.10), SBD-EPO01 (10.0.50.30), and SBD-NESSUS01 (10.0.50.20) per the model's placements.\n\nKeep the approved-media process ready: update content, package mirrors, protection definitions, and scanner plugins all cross the air gap the same reviewed, hashed way."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course keeps the proven state from drifting: updates and software arrive only through controlled channels, every endpoint runs managed protection, and a credentialed scanner turns the course 1.3 cadence into routine. In an air-gapped lab, every one of these systems feeds on content carried across the boundary — which is the recurring lesson."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Build SBD-NESSUS01 on Linux in VLAN 50, joined to the sbd.test domain with its time taken from both domain controllers, plugins fed on the offline cadence, with dedicated scan accounts — least privilege for credentialed checks, its use logged, its register row the deliberate wide permit from lesson 1. The account that scans the domain controllers is a Tier 0 credential, kept apart from the member-server scan account and used only for DC scan jobs. Allow the scanner through the host firewalls it must reach — on the fleet, add SBD-NESSUS01 to the course 1.7 workstation firewall policy for SMB 445 and WMI."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Run the first credentialed scan VLAN by VLAN against the inventory, and triage like the course 1.3 rescan taught: every finding becomes fix-now, documented deviation, or POA&M candidate — never unexplained."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Wire results outward: findings into the analysis pipeline for trending, the triage into the living exception record, and the scan schedule into the cadence — quarterly with the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) release cycle, plus change-triggered, now automated enough to keep."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Close this course's own books: baselines for WSUS01, REPO01 (config-export pattern), MGMT01, EPO01, and NESSUS01, rescans where agents and policies changed existing systems, and the handoff — the network now sustains itself; course 1.9 finally gives it something to serve."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Test-NetConnection SBD-NESSUS01.sbd.test -Port 8834\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\patch-protect -Recurse | Select-Object Name, LastWriteTime\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\patch-protect\\*"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The credentialed scan covered the inventory with the scan account's use logged, every finding is classified, results and schedule are wired into pipeline and cadence, and the course's baselines and rescans are filed."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the scan summary with its triage, the scan-account and register records, the cadence entry, and the course's scan set."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Tenable · Nessus documentation\nhttps://docs.tenable.com/nessus/\n\nDISA · SRG/STIG tools (SCC and STIG Viewer)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/srg-stig-tools\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why credentialed scans rather than unauthenticated ones?", answers: ["They run quieter on the network", "Logged-in checks see the real configuration state — patch levels and settings — instead of guessing from the outside", "Credentials are required by law"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The cadence wants configuration truth, which only an authenticated look provides; the scan account's scoping and logging keep that power honest.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Where does a scanner finding end up?", ["In one of three classified places: fixed, documented as a deviation, or on the POA&M path", "Deleted if inconvenient", "In the scanner only"], 0, "The triage rule from 1.3 scales up unchanged: open items end classified, and the classifications feed the kind of records course 2.2 teaches you to assemble."), QuizQuestion("What did this course change about the cadence itself?", ["It ended the cadence", "It moved scans to the fleet's discretion", "It gave it an instrument — scheduled credentialed scanning makes the quarterly-plus-change rhythm sustainable"], 2, "Course 1.3 set the rhythm by hand; the scanner is what lets a real operation keep the beat.")], recap: ["Credentialed, least-privilege, logged — the scanner earns its wide row.", "Findings end classified and flow into pipeline and records.", "This course's systems are baselined; the network now maintains itself."], nextStep: "That completes course 1.8. Course 1.9 stands up the business services the users actually came for — file, database, licensing, and web.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/patch-protect#nessus", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "business-services", number: "1.9", title: "Stand up the business services", subtitle: "Files, database, licensing & web — by documented paths", category: "Network build", description: "Give the users what the network exists for: SBD-FS01's role-based shares over verified SMB security, SBD-WEB01 serving internal HTTPS from a hardened, domain-joined Linux Apache, SBD-APPS01's licensing service and application API, and SBD-SQL01 running as a managed identity behind exactly one caller — every hop a documented, proven register row.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "5 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 5,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "business-services-guide", title: "Stand up the business services · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Files, database, licensing & web — by documented paths", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/business-services", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "business-services-pdf", title: "Stand up the business services · Workbook PDF", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "14 pages · edition 1.2", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/business-services/SecureByDesign-business-services-Workbook.pdf", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "business-services-word", title: "Stand up the business services · Editable workbook (Word)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Make the workbook your own.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/business-services/SecureByDesign-business-services-Workbook.docx", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "business-services-checklist-page", title: "Stand up the business services · Printable checklist", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Print the configuration and evidence checklist.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/business-services/checklist", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "business-services-checklist", title: "Stand up the business services · Configuration checklist", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Download a Markdown checklist for your configuration and evidence.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/business-services/configuration-checklist.md", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:business-services:design", title: "Design the service paths and identities", minutes: 30, summary: "Four services, and before any of them exist: who talks to what over which port, which identity each service runs as, and which groups own access — the register and the directory answer all three before a role installs.", objectives: ["Write the register rows for every business service path.", "Choose managed service identities instead of password accounts.", "Define the access groups that will own each service's data."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.4–1.8: working PKI services and the watched, managed network with the ring/source rules in force, on the routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nProvision the VLAN 40 hosts: SBD-FS01 (10.0.40.10), SBD-SQL01 (10.0.40.20), SBD-APPS01 (10.0.40.30), and SBD-WEB01 (10.0.40.40, Linux with Apache, joined to the sbd.test domain with SSSD like SBD-SPLUNK01 and SBD-NESSUS01).\n\nHave the network's service-path register and the course 1.4 SBD Server Authentication template ready — every service here gets a documented path and a real certificate."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The users finally get what the network exists for: files, licensing, and an internal web application with its application tier and database. The model's rule governs the whole course — permit only documented business service paths — so every service arrives with its register rows, its service identity, and its certificate, and nothing reaches it any other way."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Write the service-path rows: fleet to FS01 (SMB 445); fleet to APPS01 on its licensing port, recorded per application; fleet to WEB01 (HTTPS 443); WEB01 to APPS01 for the application API (HTTPS 443); and APPS01 — and only APPS01 — to SQL01 (TCP 1433 with TLS). Add an explicit deny from WEB01 to SQL01: the web tier has no database dependency. All four servers share VLAN 40, so the router never sees their traffic to each other — each server's host firewall enforces those rows. Everything else toward VLAN 40 stays denied, including endpoint-to-SQL directly."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Register WEB01's Linux maintenance rows as well: packages from SBD-REPO01 only (HTTP with GPG-signed packages) and syslog over TLS to the collector, SBD-SYSLOG01 (TCP 6514) — the same paths the other domain-joined Linux servers, SBD-SPLUNK01 and SBD-NESSUS01, use. Course 1.8's repository row named only those two because WEB01 did not exist yet."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Choose the service identities: group managed service accounts (gMSAs) for the SQL and application services, so passwords rotate themselves — and note Windows Server 2025's delegated managed service accounts as the migration path for any legacy service account this lab later inherits."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Create the access groups under the course 1.3 Groups OU — per-share and per-application role groups — because permissions will be granted to roles, never to named humans."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Request the server certificates through the course 1.4 process: one SBD Server Authentication certificate each for SQL01, APPS01 (for its application API), and WEB01, enrolled from the template, with the publication site serving their revocation story."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ADServiceAccount -Filter * | Select-Object Name, Enabled\nGet-ADGroup -SearchBase 'OU=Groups,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' -Filter * | Select-Object Name\nTest-NetConnection 10.0.40.20 -Port 1433   # expected before build: TcpTestSucceeded False"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The register carries every service row — the SQL path restricted to APPS01 alone, the WEB01-to-SQL01 deny, and WEB01's repository and syslog rows — the gMSAs and role groups exist, and the three server certificates are issued and recorded."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the service-path rows, the identity and group design, and the certificate enrollment records."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Group managed service accounts\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts-overview\n\nMicrosoft · What's new in Windows Server 2025\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/get-started/whats-new-windows-server-2025\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Who may reach SQL01 on 1433?", answers: ["Any domain member", "WEB01 and APPS01", "Only APPS01 — the application tier is the database's whole audience"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Browsers talk to the web tier, the web tier calls the application API, and only the application tier queries the database. The register encodes that chain — including an explicit deny from WEB01 to SQL01 — and denies shortcuts around it.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why gMSAs instead of a user account named svc-sql?", ["The directory manages and rotates the password itself — no human knows it, nothing expires unnoticed", "gMSAs run queries faster", "Password accounts cannot start services"], 0, "Service account passwords are a classic standing weakness. Managed accounts remove the password from human hands entirely."), QuizQuestion("Why grant access to role groups rather than people?", ["Groups are alphabetical", "Access follows the job: membership changes are one record, and permissions never accumulate on individuals", "Permissions cannot hold user names"], 1, "Role-based access is what makes 'who can touch this data' answerable from group membership instead of archaeology.")], recap: ["Every path is a row; the database hears only SBD-APPS01.", "Service identities are managed accounts, not passwords.", "Access belongs to roles; certificates are already in hand."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 builds the file service the fleet will actually use.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/business-services#design", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:business-services:fileserver", title: "Build SBD-FS01 with least-privilege shares", minutes: 30, summary: "The file server is where access control meets daily life. Build the shares against the role groups, require SMB signing on the server instead of trusting client defaults, and make 'who can read this' a one-lookup question.", objectives: ["Create the shares with role-group permissions and no individual grants.", "Confirm the SMB signing and encryption posture end to end.", "Verify access and denial per role from the fleet."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.4–1.8: working PKI services and the watched, managed network with the ring/source rules in force, on the routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nProvision the VLAN 40 hosts: SBD-FS01 (10.0.40.10), SBD-SQL01 (10.0.40.20), SBD-APPS01 (10.0.40.30), and SBD-WEB01 (10.0.40.40, Linux with Apache, joined to the sbd.test domain with SSSD like SBD-SPLUNK01 and SBD-NESSUS01).\n\nHave the network's service-path register and the course 1.4 SBD Server Authentication template ready — every service here gets a documented path and a real certificate."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The users finally get what the network exists for: files, licensing, and an internal web application with its application tier and database. The model's rule governs the whole course — permit only documented business service paths — so every service arrives with its register rows, its service identity, and its certificate, and nothing reaches it any other way."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Build SBD-FS01 in the Servers OU and create the planned shares, granting NTFS permissions to the lesson-1 role groups only — share permissions stay simple, the filesystem carries the detail, and no named human appears in an access control list (ACL)."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Enable access-based enumeration so users see only what their roles permit — the quiet UX of least privilege."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Confirm the SMB posture. Windows 11 version 24H2 Pro, Enterprise, and Education require SMB signing for both inbound and outbound connections, but Windows Server 2025 requires it only for its own outbound connections — so require signing on SBD-FS01 as well (RequireSecuritySignature in its SMB server configuration), and opt the sensitive share into SMB encryption. Verify both on a live connection from a fleet machine rather than assuming the defaults."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Test as members of two different roles from fleet machines: each sees and opens exactly its shares, the other's are invisible and denied, and the denial lands in the pipeline like every other refusal on this network."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-SmbShare | Select-Object Name, Path, FolderEnumerationMode, EncryptData   # on FS01\nGet-SmbServerConfiguration | Select-Object RequireSecuritySignature   # on FS01: expected True\nGet-SmbConnection -ServerName SBD-FS01 | Select-Object ShareName, Dialect, Signed, Encrypted   # on the fleet machine, during the test session\nicacls D:\\Shares\\Projects | Select-String 'SBD'"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Shares grant to role groups only, FS01 requires signing, a live connection from the fleet shows Signed True — or, on the encrypted share, Encrypted True, where Signed reads False even though an encrypted connection is also signed — and the two-role test proves both the access and the invisible-denied behavior."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the share and NTFS permission reports, FS01's signing setting and the live connection's signing/encryption output, and the two-role test record with its pipeline entry."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · SMB security enhancements\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/storage/file-server/smb-security\n\nMicrosoft · SMB signing\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/storage/file-server/smb-signing\n\nMicrosoft · Get-SmbConnection\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/smbshare/get-smbconnection\n\nMicrosoft · MSFT_SmbConnection class (Signed and Encrypted)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/desktop/smb/msft-smbconnection\n\nMicrosoft · What's new in Windows Server 2025\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/get-started/whats-new-windows-server-2025")], question: "Where does the fine-grained permission detail live?", answers: ["In the share permissions", "On the NTFS filesystem, granted to role groups — shares stay simple on top", "In a spreadsheet"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The classic split: simple share grants, precise filesystem ACLs, and roles everywhere — auditable at a glance.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What does the live Get-SmbConnection check prove that settings review doesn't?", ["That the actual connection negotiated signing or encryption — posture in effect, not posture on paper", "That the share has files", "Nothing extra"], 0, "Defaults differ by edition and direction — Windows Server 2025 requires signing only for its own outbound connections — so the evidence standard of this whole path is observed behavior: inspect the connection itself."), QuizQuestion("A user can't see a share their colleague uses. What is that?", ["A replication failure", "A help desk ticket to grant access immediately", "Access-based enumeration doing its job — visibility follows role membership"], 2, "Invisible-unless-permitted is the designed behavior; the role group, not an exception, is the path to access.")], recap: ["Roles own the ACLs; humans own role memberships.", "Signing and encryption are verified on a live connection.", "Both the access and the denial are on record."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 builds the database the applications will depend on.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/business-services#fileserver", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:business-services:database", title: "Build SBD-SQL01 behind its applications", minutes: 30, summary: "The database runs as a managed identity, encrypts its connections with a real certificate, and answers exactly one caller, SBD-APPS01 — the narrowest audience on the network, by design.", objectives: ["Install the database service under its gMSA.", "Bind the SBD Server Authentication certificate for encrypted connections.", "Prove the one-caller rule: SBD-APPS01 connects; SBD-WEB01 and the fleet are refused."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.4–1.8: working PKI services and the watched, managed network with the ring/source rules in force, on the routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nProvision the VLAN 40 hosts: SBD-FS01 (10.0.40.10), SBD-SQL01 (10.0.40.20), SBD-APPS01 (10.0.40.30), and SBD-WEB01 (10.0.40.40, Linux with Apache, joined to the sbd.test domain with SSSD like SBD-SPLUNK01 and SBD-NESSUS01).\n\nHave the network's service-path register and the course 1.4 SBD Server Authentication template ready — every service here gets a documented path and a real certificate."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The users finally get what the network exists for: files, licensing, and an internal web application with its application tier and database. The model's rule governs the whole course — permit only documented business service paths — so every service arrives with its register rows, its service identity, and its certificate, and nothing reaches it any other way."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Install the database engine on SBD-SQL01 with the lesson-1 gMSA as its service identity — the install itself demonstrates the managed account working where a password account used to live."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Bind the issued certificate to the instance and require encrypted connections; the chain validates against pki.sbd.test like every other certificate on this network."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Create the application databases with role-based database access for the APPS01 application identity only — least privilege continues inside the engine, not just at its port."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Prove the audience: a connection from APPS01 succeeds encrypted. The same attempt from SBD-WEB01 is refused by SBD-SQL01's host firewall — the two share VLAN 40, so the router never sees it — and one from a fleet machine dies at the router with a deny-log entry. Turn on the host firewall's dropped-packet logging if it is off, so both refusals leave a record."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-Service MSSQLSERVER -ComputerName SBD-SQL01 | Select-Object Status\n# From SBD-APPS01, the registered caller: expected TcpTestSucceeded True.\nTest-NetConnection SBD-SQL01.sbd.test -Port 1433\n# From a fleet machine: expected TcpTestSucceeded False.\nTest-NetConnection SBD-SQL01.sbd.test -Port 1433\n# From SBD-WEB01 (Linux): expected to be refused or time out.\nnc -zv SBD-SQL01.sbd.test 1433\ncertutil -verify -urlfetch C:\\Evidence\\services\\sql01.cer"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The service runs as the gMSA, connections are certificate-encrypted with a validating chain, APPS01's access works by database role, and both direct attempts fail with a record: SBD-WEB01's at SQL01's host firewall, the fleet's at the router."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the service identity proof, the certificate binding and urlfetch output, the database role grants, and APPS01's successful connection paired with both refusals and their log entries."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Securing SQL Server\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/security/securing-sql-server\n\nMicrosoft · Group managed service accounts\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts-overview")], question: "What demonstrates the gMSA actually working?", answers: ["The service running under it — a production role started by an identity no human holds a password for", "A password in the evidence folder", "The account's description field"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The running service is the proof: managed identity in use, with rotation handled by the directory.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why does the database get a certificate at all?", ["Certificates speed up queries", "Connection encryption with a validating internal chain — the PKI path's work, consumed by a real service", "SQL refuses to install without one"], 1, "This is the payoff moment for course 1.4: a business service encrypting with a certificate the lab issued, published, and can revoke."), QuizQuestion("What is the complete evidence for the one-caller rule?", ["The register row alone", "A diagram of VLAN 40", "APPS01's successful encrypted connection plus the refused attempts from SBD-WEB01 and a fleet machine, each with its log entry"], 2, "Same standard as every boundary check: the permit shown working and the deny shown refusing, each with its record."), QuizQuestion("Why does SBD-WEB01's attempt on SQL01 never reach the router?", ["The router is offline during the test", "WEB01 and SQL01 share VLAN 40, so the traffic is switched inside the VLAN and SQL01's host firewall has to refuse it", "SQL Server only accepts UDP"], 1, "Traffic between two hosts on the same VLAN never crosses the routed boundary. That is why the VLAN 40 rows are enforced by each server's host firewall, and why the WEB01-to-SQL01 deny is written down explicitly.")], recap: ["A managed identity runs the engine; no password exists to leak.", "Connections encrypt under the lab's own validated chain.", "One caller in: SBD-APPS01. SBD-WEB01 is refused at SQL01's host firewall and the fleet at the router."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 stands up the services users actually touch.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/business-services#database", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:business-services:apps-web", title: "Build SBD-APPS01 and SBD-WEB01", minutes: 40, summary: "The application server and the internal web server complete the dependency chain: fleet to web, web to application, application to database — each hop a register row, the web tier hardened against its Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) guidance, and HTTPS served from the lab's own trust.", objectives: ["Stand up SBD-APPS01's licensing service and application API on their registered paths.", "Build the domain-joined Linux Apache server hardened per its DISA Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG), serving HTTPS with the internal certificate.", "Prove the full chain: fleet → web → application → database, with the web-to-database shortcut refused."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.4–1.8: working PKI services and the watched, managed network with the ring/source rules in force, on the routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nProvision the VLAN 40 hosts: SBD-FS01 (10.0.40.10), SBD-SQL01 (10.0.40.20), SBD-APPS01 (10.0.40.30), and SBD-WEB01 (10.0.40.40, Linux with Apache, joined to the sbd.test domain with SSSD like SBD-SPLUNK01 and SBD-NESSUS01).\n\nHave the network's service-path register and the course 1.4 SBD Server Authentication template ready — every service here gets a documented path and a real certificate."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The users finally get what the network exists for: files, licensing, and an internal web application with its application tier and database. The model's rule governs the whole course — permit only documented business service paths — so every service arrives with its register rows, its service identity, and its certificate, and nothing reaches it any other way."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Build SBD-APPS01 in the Servers OU and run both of its services under managed identities: the licensing service, verified from the fleet on its registered vendor port, and the application API on HTTPS 443 with its SBD Server Authentication certificate, answering SBD-WEB01 only. APPS01 is the network's one SQL consumer — and the template for any future service: identity, row, evidence."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Build SBD-WEB01 on Linux and join it to the sbd.test domain with SSSD, as SBD-SPLUNK01 and SBD-NESSUS01 are joined. Point its packages at SBD-REPO01 only, its syslog over TLS at the collector, and its time service at both domain controllers, and harden the web tier against DISA's Apache guidance — the server's assessable record follows the Linux pattern: hardened configuration exported and hashed, checklist items answered in STIG Viewer."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Install the SBD Server Authentication certificate with its chain and serve the internal application over HTTPS — the 2,048-bit key floor from course 1.4's templates applies here as everywhere."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Prove the chain from a fleet machine: the HTTPS page loads with a validating certificate, and its data travels fleet → WEB01 → APPS01 → SQL01, every hop a register row. Then prove the shortcuts stay shut: a direct attempt from SBD-WEB01 to SQL01 on 1433 is refused by SQL01's host firewall, and the fleet still cannot touch 1433 at all — three courses of design visible in one browser request."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Test-NetConnection SBD-WEB01.sbd.test -Port 443   # from a fleet machine\ncertutil -verify -urlfetch C:\\Evidence\\services\\web01.cer\nTest-NetConnection SBD-SQL01.sbd.test -Port 1433   # from the fleet: expected False\n# On SBD-WEB01 (Linux): domain membership, the API answers, the database does not.\nrealm list\ncurl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\n' https://SBD-APPS01.sbd.test/   # use your application's health path\nnc -zv SBD-SQL01.sbd.test 1433   # expected: refused or timed out"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The licensing path works as registered, SBD-WEB01 is domain-joined with SSSD and patches only from SBD-REPO01, the Apache host serves HTTPS with a validating internal chain from a hardened, hashed configuration, and the end-to-end request proves fleet → web → application → database with both shortcuts denied."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep APPS01's identity and path proof, WEB01's domain-join and repository settings, its hardened config export with hashes and checklist, the certificate validation output, the end-to-end chain test, and the refused WEB01-to-SQL01 attempt."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Securing SQL Server\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/security/securing-sql-server\n\nDISA · STIGs and GPO package (incl. Apache guidance)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/gpo\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Where do WEB01's packages come from?", answers: ["Any public mirror that responds", "A USB stick from home", "SBD-REPO01 — the one approved source the 1.8 rule named for Linux software"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The one-source rule follows software wherever it runs; the web tier installs only what crossed the boundary through the repository, on the row lesson 1 registered for it.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What is the Apache host's assessable record?", ["A screenshot of the home page", "Its hardened configuration export with hashes plus the answered STIG checklist — the established Linux pattern", "Its uptime counter"], 1, "The router, the repository, and now the web tier: no Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) automation does not mean no evidence standard."), QuizQuestion("What does one successful page load from the fleet actually demonstrate?", ["The whole designed chain — the registered fleet-to-web path, validating internal HTTPS, the web tier's call to the application API, and the application tier's registered path into the database", "Only that Apache started", "That the fleet can reach the database directly"], 0, "The request traverses three register rows (fleet to WEB01, WEB01 to APPS01, APPS01 to SQL01) and the PKI in one go — which is why it is the lesson's closing test."), QuizQuestion("SBD-WEB01 tries SQL01 directly on 1433. What happens, and why?", ["It connects, because both servers sit in VLAN 40", "SQL01's host firewall refuses it: WEB01 has no database dependency, and APPS01 is the only registered SQL consumer", "The router drops it at the core"], 1, "The web tier reaches data only through the application API. The explicit WEB01-to-SQL01 deny, enforced on SQL01 because both servers share a VLAN, is what keeps a compromised web server from going straight for the database.")], recap: ["APPS01 serves licensing and the application API; WEB01 joins the domain like the other Linux servers.", "The web tier is hardened to its guidance and serves the lab's own trust.", "One page load proves fleet → web → application → database; the shortcuts stay shut."], nextStep: "The final lesson scans the new servers and reconciles the register against reality one last time.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/business-services#apps-web", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:business-services:prove-scan", title: "Reconcile, scan, and hand off", minutes: 30, summary: "The business services close the way every build course has: the register reconciled against live behavior, the new systems baselined, the inventory updated — and for the first time, the network is functionally complete.", objectives: ["Reconcile the full register against observed permits and denies.", "Baseline the four service hosts and rescan what changed.", "Update the living records and write the course's handoff."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.4–1.8: working PKI services and the watched, managed network with the ring/source rules in force, on the routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nProvision the VLAN 40 hosts: SBD-FS01 (10.0.40.10), SBD-SQL01 (10.0.40.20), SBD-APPS01 (10.0.40.30), and SBD-WEB01 (10.0.40.40, Linux with Apache, joined to the sbd.test domain with SSSD like SBD-SPLUNK01 and SBD-NESSUS01).\n\nHave the network's service-path register and the course 1.4 SBD Server Authentication template ready — every service here gets a documented path and a real certificate."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The users finally get what the network exists for: files, licensing, and an internal web application with its application tier and database. The model's rule governs the whole course — permit only documented business service paths — so every service arrives with its register rows, its service identity, and its certificate, and nothing reaches it any other way."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Run the full-register reconciliation one more time: every business-service row proven from its real source, every adjacent deny probed and logged, including WEB01 to SQL01 — the VLAN 40 section of the matrix joins the evidence beside course 1.5's."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Run the cadence: SCAP baselines for FS01, SQL01, and APPS01; the config-export record for WEB01; a credentialed scan of VLAN 40 from SBD-NESSUS01 with its findings triaged into the three classifications."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Update the living records: the four hosts, their identities, certificates, and rings into the SBD-MGMT01 inventory; the new rows' owners into the register; any service-hardening deviations into the exception record."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Write the handoff: every modeled system except the recovery design is now built, watched, patched, and evidenced — course 1.10 proves the network can come back from the dead, and then path 2 takes the whole record to authorization."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\business-services -Recurse | Select-Object Name, LastWriteTime\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\business-services\\*"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The matrix covers every VLAN 40 row with paired permit/deny proof, the four hosts carry baselines or config records with triaged scan findings, and inventory, register, and exception record all reflect the finished services."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Finish the checklist: the reconciliation matrix, the scan and triage set, the updated living records, and the handoff note to course 1.10."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "DISA · STIGs and GPO package (incl. Apache guidance)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/gpo\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why re-run the whole register reconciliation now?", answers: ["The register deleted itself", "Four services changed what the boundary must permit — the reconciliation proves intent and behavior still match after the biggest change yet", "Reconciliations are decorative"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Each course's additions could silently break the last course's guarantees. The periodic full reconciliation is how the register stays true.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What distinguishes WEB01's closing evidence from FS01's?", ["WEB01 has none", "FS01 is exempt from scans", "FS01 gets a SCAP baseline; WEB01 gets the hashed config export and answered checklist — same standard, platform-appropriate instrument"], 2, "The evidence standard is constant; the tool varies by platform, exactly as the course has practiced since the router."), QuizQuestion("What remains before the authorization path begins?", ["Proving recovery — course 1.10 — so the Body of Evidence includes the network coming back, not just running", "Nothing at all", "Rebuilding the domain controllers"], 0, "A network that runs but cannot recover is half a story. The last build course makes restoration a demonstrated fact.")], recap: ["Register and reality reconcile across the finished network.", "Every service host carries its platform-appropriate evidence.", "One course remains: proving the network can be brought back."], nextStep: "That completes course 1.9. Course 1.10 closes the Build path by proving recovery — directory, CA, policy, and logs — before authorization begins.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/business-services#prove-scan", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "prove-recovery", number: "1.10", title: "Prove you can recover", subtitle: "Backups with custody, one rehearsed restore & the closing record", category: "Network build", description: "Close the Build path by proving the network can come back: scheduled directory and CA backups under ceremony-grade custody, policy, boundary, logs, and the living records in one hashed catalog, a timed restore rehearsal in isolation, and the recovery record that completes the Body of Evidence.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "6 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 6,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "prove-recovery-guide", title: "Prove you can recover · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Backups with custody, one rehearsed restore & the closing record", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "prove-recovery-pdf", title: "Prove you can recover · Workbook PDF", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "16 pages · edition 1.3", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery/SecureByDesign-prove-recovery-Workbook.pdf", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "prove-recovery-word", title: "Prove you can recover · Editable workbook (Word)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Make the workbook your own.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery/SecureByDesign-prove-recovery-Workbook.docx", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "prove-recovery-checklist-page", title: "Prove you can recover · Printable checklist", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Print the configuration and evidence checklist.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery/checklist", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "prove-recovery-checklist", title: "Prove you can recover · Configuration checklist", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Download a Markdown checklist for your configuration and evidence.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery/configuration-checklist.md", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:prove-recovery:requirements", title: "Decide what recovery must prove", minutes: 20, summary: "Recovery starts as a list of promises: which systems and records must be restorable, how old a restored copy may be, how long restoration may take — written as commitments the rest of the course exists to keep.", objectives: ["Name the recovery set: what must come back, in what order.", "Set the recovery point and time objectives per item.", "Decide where backups live and who can touch them."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.9: the full network, its records, and the protected log archive all exist.\n\nProvision a protected backup volume on each server, written only by its backup job, an offline backup cabinet with removable media and a custody register, and an isolated recovery network segment (or host) for the restore rehearsal.\n\nHave the custody records handy: the Directory Services Restore Mode (DSRM) passwords from course 1.1 and the CA key protection decisions from course 1.4 — recovery is where those promises come due."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The model's final chapter: a network that cannot come back was never really built. This course backs up what matters — directory, certificate authority, policy, boundary, and the log record — proves one real restore in isolation, and files the tested-recovery evidence the authorization path expects."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Write the recovery set in dependency order — the same order the path built it: the directory first (everything trusts it), then the CA's database and keys, Group Policy, the router configuration, the log archive, and the living records themselves (register, inventory, exception record)."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Set the two numbers per item: how much history may be lost (recovery point) and how long restoration may take (recovery time) — lab-honest values you will actually measure against in lesson 5, not aspirations."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Decide backup placement: each server's protected backup volume is written only by the backup process and readable by the recovery role, and a custodian carries copies to the offline backup cabinet on media — the air-gapped network's version of an off-site copy. There is no network path to the cabinet."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Confirm the standing custody items: the DSRM passwords are sealed and findable, and the root CA's protected backup from the course 1.4 ceremony is accounted for — recovery depends on secrets that must exist before the bad day."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Capture the current protection state before this course changes it.\nwbadmin get versions\nGet-ChildItem E:\\Backups -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Measure-Object   # this server's protected backup volume"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The recovery set is ordered by dependency with point and time objectives per item, the backup placement and access rules are written, and the DSRM and root-key custody records check out."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the recovery requirements sheet, the objectives table, the placement and access decisions, and the custody verification notes."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Active Directory forest recovery guide\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/forest-recovery-guide/ad-forest-recovery-guide\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (CP family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why does the directory lead the recovery order?", answers: ["It has the most data", "Everything else authenticates against it — services restored before identity have nothing to trust", "Alphabetical order"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Recovery order mirrors build order for the same reason: the dependency chain starts at identity.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What makes a recovery time objective honest?", ["Making it very small", "Leaving it unstated", "Setting a value you will actually measure a rehearsal against"], 2, "Lesson 5 times a real restore. An objective nobody measures is marketing, not a commitment."), QuizQuestion("What is the offline media copy for?", ["Surviving a failure that takes the online backup with it — the air-gapped answer to off-site", "Faster restores", "Decoration for the evidence binder"], 0, "Replication and online backups share failure modes with what they protect. One custody-tracked copy stands apart.")], recap: ["The recovery set is ordered by dependency, directory first.", "Point and time objectives exist to be measured, not admired.", "Backups get a protected home plus one offline, custody-tracked copy."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 makes the directory's safety net real.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery#requirements", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:prove-recovery:directory", title: "Back up the directory, on schedule", minutes: 25, summary: "System state from both controllers, on a schedule, to the protected target — plus the quiet feature that handles the most common 'disaster' of all: the Recycle Bin for deleted directory objects.", objectives: ["Schedule system state backups on both domain controllers.", "Verify and catalog each backup with hashes.", "Enable the Active Directory Recycle Bin for object-level recovery."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.9: the full network, its records, and the protected log archive all exist.\n\nProvision a protected backup volume on each server, written only by its backup job, an offline backup cabinet with removable media and a custody register, and an isolated recovery network segment (or host) for the restore rehearsal.\n\nHave the custody records handy: the Directory Services Restore Mode (DSRM) passwords from course 1.1 and the CA key protection decisions from course 1.4 — recovery is where those promises come due."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The model's final chapter: a network that cannot come back was never really built. This course backs up what matters — directory, certificate authority, policy, boundary, and the log record — proves one real restore in isolation, and files the tested-recovery evidence the authorization path expects."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Schedule Windows Server Backup jobs on SBD-DC01 and SBD-DC02 to each server's protected backup volume, including the system state and all critical volumes (wbadmin's -allCritical) so the same backup also supports a full server (bare-metal) recovery to different hardware — two controllers, two independent backups, because replication is availability and was never recovery (course 1.1's point, now kept)."), LessonSection("Step 2", "After the first run, verify each backup's completion, record its version identifier, and hash the catalog entry into the evidence set — an unverified backup is a hope with a timestamp."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Enable the Active Directory Recycle Bin (a one-way, forest-level action from the Tier 0 seat, recorded as the change it is) so a deleted user or OU comes back intact without touching system state at all."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Write the restore decision tree: deleted object → Recycle Bin; corrupted DC on its own hardware → system state restore in DSRM (lesson 5's rehearsal); DC whose hardware is gone → full server (bare-metal) recovery from the critical-volumes backup; whole-forest loss → the Microsoft forest recovery guide, referenced and staged with the evidence rather than improvised on the day."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "wbadmin get versions\nGet-ADOptionalFeature -Filter {Name -like 'Recycle Bin*'} | Select-Object Name, EnabledScopes\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\recovery\\backup-catalog.txt"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Both DCs show scheduled, completed system state versions on the protected target, the Recycle Bin reports an enabled scope, and the decision tree names the tool for each failure class."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the backup schedules and version listings, the hashed catalog, the Recycle Bin enablement record, and the restore decision tree."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Active Directory forest recovery guide\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/forest-recovery-guide/ad-forest-recovery-guide\n\nMicrosoft · Wbadmin (Windows Server Backup)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wbadmin\n\nMicrosoft · AD forest recovery: perform a full server recovery\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/forest-recovery-guide/ad-forest-recovery-perform-full-server-recovery")], question: "Both DCs replicate — why back up both?", answers: ["Replication copies mistakes as faithfully as data; independent backups are the history replication doesn't keep", "Backups run faster in pairs", "Only one actually holds the directory"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The course 1.1 teaching point, finally implemented: availability and recoverability are different capabilities with different tools.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("An administrator deletes an OU by mistake. First tool?", ["Rebuild the forest", "The Recycle Bin — object-level restore with attributes intact, no system state needed", "Ask users to recreate their accounts"], 1, "The decision tree's first branch: match the restore to the loss. Most disasters are small, and the small tool is already enabled."), QuizQuestion("What makes a backup more than a hope?", ["A long retention setting", "Its file size", "Verified completion, a recorded version, and a hashed catalog entry — and eventually a rehearsed restore"], 2, "The evidence standard applies to safety nets too: recorded, verifiable, and — in lesson 5 — actually exercised.")], recap: ["Two independent system state schedules, verified and cataloged.", "The Recycle Bin handles the everyday disaster in seconds.", "A decision tree matches each failure class to its tool."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 protects the trust fabric and the supporting state.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery#directory", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:prove-recovery:trust-state", title: "Back up the CA, policy, boundary, and records", minutes: 30, summary: "The directory is not the only thing the network cannot re-derive: the issuing CA's database and key, the Group Policy set, the router's configuration, the log archive, and the living records each get their protected, cataloged copy.", objectives: ["Back up the issuing CA's database and key to its protected backup volume under ceremony-grade protection.", "Refresh the Group Policy object (GPO) and router configuration backups into the protected set.", "Fold the log archive and living records into the same catalog."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.9: the full network, its records, and the protected log archive all exist.\n\nProvision a protected backup volume on each server, written only by its backup job, an offline backup cabinet with removable media and a custody register, and an isolated recovery network segment (or host) for the restore rehearsal.\n\nHave the custody records handy: the Directory Services Restore Mode (DSRM) passwords from course 1.1 and the CA key protection decisions from course 1.4 — recovery is where those promises come due."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The model's final chapter: a network that cannot come back was never really built. This course backs up what matters — directory, certificate authority, policy, boundary, and the log record — proves one real restore in isolation, and files the tested-recovery evidence the authorization path expects."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Back up the issuing CA — database and private key — with certutil to SBD-SUBCA01's protected backup volume (E:\\Backups in this sample), never to C:\\Evidence: the private key is a secret, and secrets never go into evidence. Protect the key material to the same standard as the course 1.4 ceremony artifacts and record custody; only the backup's hashes and the custody record go into the evidence folder. The root's protected backup already exists from that ceremony and is verified, not duplicated, here."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Refresh the Group Policy backup (the course 1.3 Backup-GPO set, now the whole policy estate) and the router configuration export into the protected target, each hashed into the catalog."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Confirm the log archive's protection already satisfies this course — SBD-AUDIT01's hashed archives are themselves the record — and add the archive catalog to the recovery set so the evidence survives the systems it describes."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Export the living records — register, inventory, exception record, and this course's own sheets — into the set: a recovered network without its records would pass no assessment and teach the wrong lesson."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-SUBCA01: back up to the protected backup volume, never to the evidence folder.\ncertutil -backup E:\\Backups\\SBD-SUBCA01\\ca-backup   # prompts for the password that protects the exported key\n# Only the hashes and the custody record go into evidence.\nGet-ChildItem E:\\Backups\\SBD-SUBCA01\\ca-backup -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv C:\\Evidence\\recovery\\ca-backup-hashes.csv -NoTypeInformation\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\recovery\\gpo-backup\\* | Select-Object -First 6"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The CA backup sits on SBD-SUBCA01's protected backup volume with key custody recorded and only its hashes in evidence, the policy and router exports are current and hashed, and the catalog covers logs and living records alongside the systems."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the CA backup's hash list and custody record (never the backup itself), the refreshed policy and router exports with hashes, and the complete recovery catalog."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Wbadmin (Windows Server Backup)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wbadmin\n\nMicrosoft · Certutil reference (CA backup and restore)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/certutil")], question: "Why does the CA key backup get ceremony-grade handling?", answers: ["A copied issuing key is the power to mint trusted certificates — the backup is as sensitive as the CA itself", "Keys are large files", "certutil requires it"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Backups inherit the sensitivity of what they contain. The course 1.4 custody discipline applies to every copy of key material, forever.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why do the register, inventory, and exception record join the backup set?", ["They are the largest files", "A restored network without its records has lost its assessable story — the evidence must survive what it evidences", "Backups require ten items minimum"], 1, "This path's real product is a defensible record. Recovery that saves the machines and loses the story saves the wrong half."), QuizQuestion("What does this lesson do about the offline root's backup?", ["Copies it onto the network for convenience", "Ignores it", "Verifies the ceremony-era backup's custody — it already exists, offline, and stays that way"], 2, "The root's protection is its isolation. This course checks the promise rather than breaking it with a networked copy."), QuizQuestion("Where does the issuing CA's backup go?", ["Into C:\\Evidence, so assessors can inspect it", "Onto SBD-SUBCA01's protected backup volume, with only its hashes and the custody record in evidence", "Onto pki.sbd.test, beside the CRLs"], 1, "The backup holds the CA's private key, and secrets never go into evidence — the DNS course's rule. Evidence records that the backup exists and is intact: its hashes and its custody, not its contents.")], recap: ["CA database and key: backed up to the protected volume, custody recorded, only hashes in evidence.", "Policy, boundary, logs, and records all live in one hashed catalog.", "The evidence now survives the systems it describes."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 moves the copies to the offline cabinet, by hand and on record.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery#trust-state", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:prove-recovery:offline-copies", title: "Carry the copies to the offline cabinet", minutes: 20, summary: "A backup that sits on the server it protects fails with it. Move a copy of each backup to the offline cabinet by hand, under the same custody rules as any media crossing a boundary, and keep the custody record that proves it.", objectives: ["Define which custodian carries which backup, by tier.", "Transfer the copies to offline media with hashes and custody records.", "Prove a copy can be found, verified, and returned for a restore."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.9: the full network, its records, and the protected log archive all exist.\n\nProvision a protected backup volume on each server, written only by its backup job, an offline backup cabinet with removable media and a custody register, and an isolated recovery network segment (or host) for the restore rehearsal.\n\nHave the custody records handy: the Directory Services Restore Mode (DSRM) passwords from course 1.1 and the CA key protection decisions from course 1.4 — recovery is where those promises come due."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The model's final chapter: a network that cannot come back was never really built. This course backs up what matters — directory, certificate authority, policy, boundary, and the log record — proves one real restore in isolation, and files the tested-recovery evidence the authorization path expects."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Assign custodians by tier: a Tier 0 custodian handles DC system state and the issuing CA's database and key backup; the audit custodian handles the log archive from SBD-AUDIT01; a server custodian handles member-server backups. No one carries a tier they do not hold."), LessonSection("Step 2", "For each copy, write the media ID, hash the backup on its protected volume, copy it to encrypted removable media, and verify the hash on the media before it leaves the room."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Carry the media to the offline backup cabinet and record the handoff in the custody register: who, what, when, the hashes, and the storage slot. This is the course 1.3 media procedure used for backups."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Pull one copy back as a test: find it from the register, verify its hash, and confirm it is readable. Lesson 5's rehearsal restores from a copy that has made this round trip."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Hash on the protected volume, then on the media.\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 E:\\Backups\\SBD-DC02\\*.vhdx\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 F:\\Media-0012\\SBD-DC02\\*.vhdx"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Every item in the recovery set has an offline copy with matching hashes on the volume and the media, a custody entry with a named custodian of the right tier, and one copy has been retrieved and verified."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the custodian assignments, the hash pairs, the custody register entries, and the retrieval test record."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Wbadmin (Windows Server Backup)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wbadmin\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (CP family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Who carries the domain controllers' system-state backup to the cabinet?", answers: ["Any server operator who is free", "A Tier 0 custodian", "The help desk"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "A DC backup holds the directory's secrets. Whoever can handle it can rebuild or read the domain, so it stays with Tier 0.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why is there no network path to the offline backup cabinet?", ["So ransomware or an intruder on the network cannot reach or encrypt the copies", "Because backups are too large to send", "Because NIST forbids network backups"], 0, "An offline copy survives an attack that takes the whole network. The custody record replaces the network path as the proof of what happened."), QuizQuestion("The hash on the media does not match the hash on the volume. What do you do?", ["Store it anyway and note it", "Delete the original", "Do not store it; recopy and investigate, because the copy is not trustworthy"], 2, "A mismatch means the copy is not the backup you verified. Storing it would put an untrusted copy where you will look on the worst day.")], recap: ["Custodians carry only the backups their tier allows.", "Every copy has matching hashes and a custody entry.", "A copy has been retrieved and verified before anyone needs it."], nextStep: "Lesson 5 stops trusting the backups and tests one.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery#offline-copies", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:prove-recovery:rehearse", title: "Rehearse a real restore, in isolation", minutes: 35, summary: "One restore, actually performed: a domain controller's system state brought back on the isolated segment, timed against the objective, verified against the directory — plus the small restore everyone will someday need, from the Recycle Bin.", objectives: ["Restore a DC's system state in Directory Services Restore Mode on the isolated recovery segment.", "Measure the restore against the time objective and verify directory health.", "Perform and record an object-level Recycle Bin restore."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.9: the full network, its records, and the protected log archive all exist.\n\nProvision a protected backup volume on each server, written only by its backup job, an offline backup cabinet with removable media and a custody register, and an isolated recovery network segment (or host) for the restore rehearsal.\n\nHave the custody records handy: the Directory Services Restore Mode (DSRM) passwords from course 1.1 and the CA key protection decisions from course 1.4 — recovery is where those promises come due."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The model's final chapter: a network that cannot come back was never really built. This course backs up what matters — directory, certificate authority, policy, boundary, and the log record — proves one real restore in isolation, and files the tested-recovery evidence the authorization path expects."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Stage the rehearsal: the isolated recovery segment has no path to the production VLANs — a restored controller believing it is authoritative must never meet the live directory; that isolation rule is written before the restore starts."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Restore SBD-DC02 from the offline copy retrieved in lesson 4, timing the work from decision to directory-services-running against the lesson-1 objective. A system state restore needs the same server instance — use a copy of SBD-DC02's virtual machine attached only to the isolated segment — started in DSRM: bcdedit /set safeboot dsrepair, restart, and sign in with the DSRM password. Restoring to different hardware needs a full server (bare-metal) recovery from the critical-volumes backup instead."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Remove the DSRM boot setting (bcdedit /deletevalue safeboot) and restart normally, then verify the restored directory the course 1.1 way — services, shares, a directory query. Record what the rehearsal taught: steps that surprised you, the measured time, and the decision-tree updates; then tear the rehearsal down completely."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Back in production, run the everyday restore: delete a disposable test object, bring it back from the Recycle Bin with attributes intact, and file both rehearsals — the big one and the small one — as the tested-recovery evidence."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On the isolated copy of SBD-DC02: start in DSRM, then sign in with the DSRM password.\nbcdedit /set safeboot dsrepair\nshutdown -t 0 -r\nwbadmin get versions -backupTarget:<media-volume> -machine:SBD-DC02\nwbadmin start systemstaterecovery -version:<version-id> -backupTarget:<media-volume> -machine:SBD-DC02\nbcdedit /deletevalue safeboot   # then restart normally\nGet-Service NTDS,DNS,Netlogon   # after restart, on the restored controller\nRestore-ADObject -Identity <deleted-object-guid>   # production Recycle Bin rehearsal"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The isolated restore ran in DSRM and reached a healthy directory within (or honestly against) the time objective, the DSRM boot setting is removed, the teardown is confirmed, and the production object restore returned the test object intact."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the timed rehearsal log with its verification output, the lessons-learned and decision-tree updates, the teardown confirmation, and the object-restore record."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Active Directory forest recovery guide\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/forest-recovery-guide/ad-forest-recovery-guide\n\nMicrosoft · wbadmin start systemstaterecovery\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wbadmin-start-systemstaterecovery\n\nMicrosoft · Restore AD DS from backup (nonauthoritative restore)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-server-2008-r2-and-2008/cc794755(v=ws.10)\n\nMicrosoft · Restart the domain controller in Directory Services Restore Mode locally\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-server-2008-r2-and-2008/cc816897(v=ws.10)\n\nMicrosoft · AD forest recovery: perform a full server recovery\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/forest-recovery-guide/ad-forest-recovery-perform-full-server-recovery")], question: "Why must the rehearsal segment be isolated from production?", answers: ["To make the restore faster", "A restored controller carries old directory state — meeting the live forest would inject the past into the present", "Isolation is optional for labs"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The rehearsal's first rule protects production from the rehearsal. Old authoritative state on a live network is its own disaster.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("The restore took longer than the objective. What now?", ["Hide the timing", "Declare the objective met anyway", "Record the honest number and either fix the process or revise the objective — both are legitimate; silence is not"], 2, "The rehearsal exists to replace belief with measurement. Either outcome — better process or truer objective — improves the real bad day."), QuizQuestion("Why rehearse the Recycle Bin restore too?", ["The most likely recovery event deserves the same tested-and-recorded treatment as the rare one", "It is required before system state restores", "Deleted objects expire daily"], 0, "Recovery evidence should match recovery reality: object-level loss is the common case, so its tool gets proven, not presumed."), QuizQuestion("Your recovery host is new hardware. Which restore applies?", ["A system state restore, exactly as on the original server", "A full server (bare-metal) recovery from the critical-volumes backup — system state restores are for the same server instance", "Copying NTDS.dit across by hand"], 1, "Microsoft's forest recovery guidance calls for a full server recovery when you restore to different hardware or a different operating system instance. A system state restore runs on the original instance, in DSRM.")], recap: ["One real restore, isolated, timed, verified, and torn down.", "The measured number replaced the hoped-for one.", "The everyday restore is proven alongside the dramatic one."], nextStep: "The final lesson files the recovery record and closes the Build path.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery#rehearse", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:prove-recovery:record", title: "File the recovery record and close the path", minutes: 20, summary: "The Build path ends the way it worked: owners, schedules, and evidence. The recovery record joins the living documents, the final scans snapshot the finished network, and the Body of Evidence is ready for the authorization courses to assemble.", objectives: ["Assemble the tested-recovery record with owners and review dates.", "Run the closing scan snapshot across the finished network.", "Hand the complete evidence set to the authorization path."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.9: the full network, its records, and the protected log archive all exist.\n\nProvision a protected backup volume on each server, written only by its backup job, an offline backup cabinet with removable media and a custody register, and an isolated recovery network segment (or host) for the restore rehearsal.\n\nHave the custody records handy: the Directory Services Restore Mode (DSRM) passwords from course 1.1 and the CA key protection decisions from course 1.4 — recovery is where those promises come due."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The model's final chapter: a network that cannot come back was never really built. This course backs up what matters — directory, certificate authority, policy, boundary, and the log record — proves one real restore in isolation, and files the tested-recovery evidence the authorization path expects."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Assemble the recovery record: the requirements and objectives, the backup catalog, both rehearsal results with the measured times, the decision tree, and a named owner with a scheduled next rehearsal — recovery is a practice with a calendar, not a binder."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Run the closing snapshot: the standing credentialed scan across every VLAN plus current Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) results per the cadence, triaged as always — the dated 'as-authorized' picture path 2 will reference."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Reconcile the three living records one final time — inventory against directory and leases, register against behavior, exception record against open findings — so the authorization path inherits agreement, not archaeology."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Write the Build path's closing note: what exists, where every record lives, and what is intentionally open — then start course 2.1, where this network stops being a project and becomes a system seeking authorization."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ChildItem C:\\Evidence -Directory | Select-Object Name\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\recovery -Recurse | Measure-Object\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\recovery\\recovery-record.pdf -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The recovery record is complete with an owner and next-rehearsal date, the closing snapshot is filed with triage, and all three living records reconcile cleanly into the handoff note."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Finish the checklist: the recovery record, the closing scan snapshot, the three reconciliations, and the Build path's closing note."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Active Directory forest recovery guide\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/forest-recovery-guide/ad-forest-recovery-guide\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (CP family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "What makes recovery 'a practice with a calendar'?", answers: ["A named owner and a scheduled next rehearsal — the test repeats as the network changes", "Laminating the decision tree", "Buying more storage"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "One rehearsal proves today's backups. The schedule is what keeps tomorrow's honest.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why take a full scan snapshot at path's end?", ["Scanners need exercise", "It is the dated security picture the authorization decision will actually be about", "To fill the evidence folder"], 1, "Path 2's assessors and the authorizing official evaluate a system at a moment. The snapshot is that moment, captured."), QuizQuestion("What does path 2 inherit from ten build courses?", ["Just the virtual machines", "A to-do list", "A reconciled, evidenced system: inventory, register, exceptions, scans, and tested recovery — the Body of Evidence, already real"], 2, "The authorization path was never going to invent evidence; it organizes what the Build path produced on purpose, course by course.")], recap: ["Recovery has an owner, a date, and a measured track record.", "The closing snapshot freezes the as-authorized picture.", "The Body of Evidence exists — path 2 assembles, it doesn't excavate."], nextStep: "That completes the Build the Network path. Continue with course 2.1 to carry this network — and its evidence — through the Risk Management Framework to an authorization decision.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery#record", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "rmf-boe-ato", number: "2.1", title: "Risk Management Framework", subtitle: "Scope, categorization, and control selection", category: "Network authorization", description: "Apply the Risk Management Framework (RMF) to the system built in Inside the Network. Establish responsibilities and boundaries, justify mission impacts, and select a defensible control set before developing its Body of Evidence.", icon: "risk", published: true, studyTime: "4 modules · 12 topics · 6 study hours", topics: 12,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-guide", title: "Interactive authorization course", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Lessons, evidence examples, and the capstone.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-word", title: "Full training catalog · Word", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Edition 1.3 · 12 modules", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/Secure_By_Design_RMF_Training_Catalog.docx", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:01", title: "01 · RMF and authorization fundamentals", minutes: 60, summary: "Explain the relationship between the RMF lifecycle, the Body of Evidence, the authorization package, and the authorization decision.", sections: [LessonSection("Start with the mission, then ask about risk.", "The Risk Management Framework (RMF) connects a system’s purpose to the safeguards, evidence, and decisions needed to operate it. Begin with what the organization needs the system to do. Then consider what could happen if information is exposed, changed incorrectly, or unavailable.\n\nAn Authorization to Operate (ATO) records a management decision about risk. Installing security tools or finishing a checklist does not make that decision. The authorization package gives the responsible official information to evaluate it."), LessonSection("Everyone contributes. The responsibilities differ.", "The system owner is accountable for the system and its package. An Information System Security Manager (ISSM) oversees the security program; an Information System Security Officer (ISSO) supports day-to-day implementation and documentation. Engineers operate safeguards and produce records.\n\nA control assessor evaluates the evidence against the assessment objectives. Common control providers document inherited protections. The Authorizing Official (AO) makes the authorization and risk-acceptance decision within their authority. Local appointments determine the precise division of work."), LessonSection("Follow the claim all the way to the decision.", "The Body of Evidence contains the records supporting how the system is secured and operated. The authorization package brings the decision-relevant material together: the plans, assessment reports, outstanding actions, executive summary, and supporting references required by the authority.\n\nA reviewer should be able to move from a requirement to its implementation, to evidence, to an assessment result, and finally to the effect on risk. Missing links are questions to resolve, not paperwork to hide."), LessonSection("From a claim to a decision", "Claim: “Privileged account changes are logged and reviewed.”\n\nEvidence: The selected-event configuration, a sample event, and the analyst’s review record.\n\nAssessment: The assessor tests the event and checks whether the expected review occurred.\n\nDecision: The AO considers the supported result and any remaining gap in the system’s risk context."), LessonSection("Build", "Create a role map for the system owner, ISSM, ISSO, engineers, control assessor, common control provider, and Authorizing Official (AO). Identify applicable privacy responsibilities."), LessonSection("Prove", "Given a missing test result and an unresolved risk, identify who supplies evidence, who assesses it, and who has authority to accept the risk.")], question: "The team installed a SIEM and completed its checklist. What comes next?", answers: ["Treat the system as authorized.", "Support implementation claims with assessment evidence and the required risk review.", "Replace the SSP with screenshots of the SIEM."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Security tools support control implementation. The assessor evaluates the evidence, and the AO makes the authorization decision.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#catalog/01", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:02", title: "02 · Prepare and define the system", minutes: 90, summary: "Establish what is being authorized, why it exists, and which organizational and system preparation decisions must be made before documentation expands.", sections: [LessonSection("Describe the work the system supports.", "Identify the mission, stakeholders, information owners, and dependencies before choosing documents. Record who needs the system, what work it enables, and what service interruption or misuse would affect.\n\nPreparation also happens at the organizational level: risk tolerance, common controls, resources, roles, and oversight influence each system’s approach. Prepare is explicit in NIST SP 800-37 Revision 2."), LessonSection("Draw the boundary, then test the assumptions.", "An authorization boundary identifies the system being evaluated. It is more than a subnet diagram. Consider management interfaces, hosting infrastructure, shared services, information flows, and dependencies.\n\nA component outside the boundary can still affect risk. Document its relationship and the responsible party. Do not use a boundary choice to make a difficult dependency disappear."), LessonSection("Identify the governing direction.", "Record the authority, relevant policies, adopted control versions, required reviewers, and expected deliverables. Clarify which protections are system-specific, inherited, or shared.\n\nAn inherited-control claim needs an identified provider and applicable evidence. In the Inside the Network practice system, transfers across the air gap remain manual and human-controlled."), LessonSection("Resolve a shared-service question", "Question: Is the virtualization host covered by this package?\n\nRecord: Identify the host, its administrator, and the systems it supports.\n\nResolve: Document whether it is inside the boundary or provided through a documented external/common service.\n\nProve: Keep the architecture, inventory, and responsibility record consistent."), LessonSection("Build", "Draft a mission statement, boundary diagram, asset list, data flow narrative, stakeholder register, and assumptions log. Record the governing authority, approved standards, overlays, and review expectations. Check each information flow in your narrative against the connection map in Inside the Network, which shows every permitted, pending, and denied rule for a system."), LessonSection("Prove", "Explain whether a shared service, hypervisor, management path, or offline update transfer is inside the boundary, external, or inherited, and identify supporting responsibility evidence.")], question: "A service sits outside the boundary but provides identity for the system. What should the package do?", answers: ["Ignore it because it is external.", "Assume all of its controls are inherited.", "Document the dependency, responsibilities, and applicable assurance evidence."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Being outside a boundary does not remove a dependency or establish inheritance.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#catalog/02", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:03", title: "03 · Categorize information and impacts", minutes: 90, summary: "Justify confidentiality, integrity, and availability impacts using information types and mission consequences. Treat privacy applicability as an explicit decision.", sections: [LessonSection("Evaluate the consequences for each objective.", "Confidentiality concerns inappropriate disclosure. Integrity concerns unauthorized or improper change. Availability concerns reliable access when needed. Categorization starts with information types and the consequences for the mission.\n\nDifferent objectives can have different impacts. Record the reasoning and applicable methodology; an isolated network alone does not decide the result."), LessonSection("Write a rationale another person can review.", "Explain the information processed, the people or mission affected, the potential consequences, and the justification for each impact determination. Capture assumptions and the required approval.\n\nIf a system’s mission changes, revisit the rationale. Repeating an inherited label without understanding its basis does not make the categorization defensible."), LessonSection("Apply the right categorization authority.", "Federal and national security system contexts do not use identical selection rules. Determine which requirements apply before choosing a baseline or overlay. Consider privacy applicability explicitly.\n\nThe optional Secure By Design exercise uses Moderate confidentiality, Low integrity, and Low availability as its stated planning input. That example must not be imposed on another system or treated as an authorization result."), LessonSection("A dependency changes the discussion", "Initial rationale: An internal service is needed for routine business support.\n\nNew information: A mission process now depends on timely access to it.\n\nReview: Reevaluate availability consequences under the applicable categorization method.\n\nUpdate: Record the rationale and route it for the required review."), LessonSection("Build", "Prepare a categorization worksheet with information types, impact rationale, mission dependencies, approval routing, and privacy considerations. Document the methodology required by the system’s authority."), LessonSection("Prove", "Defend a changed availability impact after a new mission dependency. Explain why an air gap alone does not establish a low impact level or eliminate risk.")], question: "Does an air gap determine the system’s impact level?", answers: ["No. Mission consequences and the applicable categorization method determine the rationale.", "Yes. It always makes confidentiality Low.", "Yes. It eliminates integrity risk."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Isolation is a design characteristic. It does not replace the mission and information impact analysis.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#catalog/03", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:04", title: "04 · Select and tailor controls", minutes: 120, summary: "Translate the approved categorization and applicable requirements into a justified, assignable control set. Distinguish the control catalog from a selected baseline.", sections: [LessonSection("A catalog is not the same as a selected control set.", "NIST SP 800-53 describes security and privacy controls. A baseline provides a starting set under an applicable selection method. Overlays and authority-specific requirements can change that starting point.\n\nSP 800-53B provides federal baselines; a national security or SAP selection also requires the applicable CNSS and program direction. Keep these contexts distinct."), LessonSection("Make tailoring decisions visible.", "Record why a control is added, adjusted, or considered not applicable and who must approve that decision. Resolve organization-defined parameters such as roles, frequencies, and time periods using the governing direction.\n\nDo not mark a requirement not applicable merely because it is difficult to implement. A reviewer needs both the rationale and its authorized disposition."), LessonSection("Allocate work before asking for evidence.", "Identify the owner of each implementation. Distinguish system-specific controls, common controls supplied by a provider, and hybrid controls with shared responsibility.\n\nA Security Control Traceability Matrix (SCTM) can connect the selected requirement, parameter values, ownership, implementation, evidence, assessment result, and associated risk. Its exact format follows local requirements."), LessonSection("An inheritance claim needs support", "Claim: “Account management is inherited.”\n\nQuestion: Which provider performs which part of the requirement?\n\nEvidence: Provider documentation, applicable assessment information, and local responsibilities.\n\nRecord: An allocation that separates inherited activities from the system team’s work."), LessonSection("Build", "Start a Security Control Traceability Matrix (SCTM). Record control revision, applicability, tailoring rationale, organization-defined parameters, owner, and system-specific, common, or hybrid allocation."), LessonSection("Prove", "Resolve a proposed not-applicable control and an inherited-control claim using evidence and the required approval path. Pin the selected control and assessment-procedure versions.")], question: "A team calls a difficult control not applicable. What is missing?", answers: ["A tool screenshot.", "A justified applicability decision and the required approval.", "A shorter control identifier."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Tailoring must follow the governing process and preserve the rationale.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#catalog/04", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "rmf-body-of-evidence", number: "2.2", title: "Body of Evidence", subtitle: "Document, demonstrate, and assess the network", category: "Network authorization", description: "Develop the Body of Evidence for the same Inside the Network build. Write the System Security Plan, connect implementation to proof, organize evidence, and assess findings and residual risk for authorization review.", icon: "risk", published: true, studyTime: "5 modules · 15 topics · 9 study hours", topics: 15,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-guide", title: "Interactive authorization course", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Lessons, evidence examples, and the capstone.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-word", title: "Full training catalog · Word", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Edition 1.3 · 12 modules", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/Secure_By_Design_RMF_Training_Catalog.docx", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-evidence", title: "Body of Evidence examples and starters", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Ten examples with editable Markdown and CSV starters.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#evidence", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:05", title: "05 · Write the system security plan", minutes: 120, summary: "Write a System Security Plan (SSP) that describes the actual system and explains how selected controls are implemented.", sections: [LessonSection("Describe the system that actually exists.", "The System Security Plan (SSP) identifies the system, its environment, responsible parties, dependencies, and how selected controls are implemented. Keep it consistent with the inventory and architecture.\n\nDescribe implemented safeguards and clearly identify planned work. A future improvement should not appear as if it already operates."), LessonSection("Make the implementation understandable.", "Explain who performs an activity, what they do, where it applies, when it happens, and which records support the claim. Use the exact selected requirements and parameters as the basis.\n\n“We comply with policy” leaves essential questions unanswered. A useful statement describes the actual process and links to evidence without claiming more than the evidence supports."), LessonSection("Connect the supporting plans.", "Configuration, access, incident response, contingency, media, privacy, and other applicable plans describe supporting processes. Connect them to the SSP rather than copying inconsistent versions of the same procedure into several places.\n\nRecord the owner, version, and review status of each reference. A plan is evidence of intended process; records and tests help show that the process is carried out."), LessonSection("Improve a vague implementation statement", "Before: “Audit logs are reviewed according to policy.”\n\nBetter: Identify the assigned reviewer, covered event sources, approved review schedule, exception process, and review record.\n\nCheck: Does the scope match the selected requirement and actual source inventory?\n\nLink: Point to the configuration, sample events, and completed review records."), LessonSection("Build", "Draft SSP sections for the boundary, system components, roles, and selected controls. Write statements covering who performs the activity, what happens, where it occurs, when it happens, and the supporting evidence."), LessonSection("Prove", "Replace a vague “compliant with policy” statement with a specific implementation narrative. Reconcile it with the diagrams, inventory, and inheritance record.")], question: "Which SSP statement is most useful?", answers: ["“The organization is fully compliant.”", "“A security product is installed.”", "A specific description of the activity, owner, scope, frequency, and evidence."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "A defensible implementation statement gives reviewers something concrete to examine, interview about, and test.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#catalog/05", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:06", title: "06 · Connect implementation to proof", minutes: 120, summary: "Collect technical and operational evidence that addresses the selected requirement and can support an assessment.", sections: [LessonSection("Capture the configuration and its context.", "A configuration export or hardening checklist is useful when the reviewer can identify the system, setting, scope, version, and collection time. Retain the review or validation record as appropriate.\n\nA passing automated check has a defined scope. Manual checks, applicability decisions, and technical limitations still need attention."), LessonSection("Operational records show whether a process happens.", "Logs, scan reports, incident tickets, access reviews, and backup test records support different claims. Connect each item to the question it can answer.\n\nThe optional examples use Splunk, Tenable, and Trellix records. Equivalent records can support the exercise. Owning a named product does not itself establish that the selected control is effective."), LessonSection("Coverage is part of evidence quality.", "Check whether all relevant systems were included, credentials worked, data was current, and the collection method was appropriate. Preserve collection failures rather than silently excluding them.\n\nExplain what the evidence cannot show. A vulnerability scan does not demonstrate every control, and a backup job success message does not by itself prove recovery."), LessonSection("A clean scan with a coverage problem", "Report: The scan reports no critical findings.\n\nInspection: Authenticated checks failed for two in-scope hosts.\n\nConclusion: The report does not support a complete credentialed assessment of those hosts.\n\nFollow-up: Correct access, rerun the intended checks, and retain the original coverage limitation."), LessonSection("Build", "Collect configuration exports, a reviewed hardening checklist, scan coverage, log review records, and a recovery-test result. Use Splunk, Tenable, and Trellix examples or equivalent supplied training records."), LessonSection("Prove", "Explain what each item proves and leaves untested. Identify a failed authenticated scan, an unreviewed alert, and a configuration screenshot that lacks system or time context.")], question: "A scan reports no findings, but credentials failed. What can you conclude?", answers: ["All required checks passed.", "The available result is limited; credentialed coverage must be resolved.", "No evidence needs to be retained."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The absence of reported findings is not proof that intended checks ran successfully.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#catalog/06", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:07", title: "07 · Organize the Body of Evidence", minutes: 90, summary: "Make evidence easy to retrieve, trace, and maintain across system changes and assessment cycles.", sections: [LessonSection("Give each record a stable identity.", "An evidence index lets a reviewer locate a record without relying on a folder name or someone’s memory. Record its identifier, title, scope, owner, collection date, version, and location.\n\nUse a naming convention that is easy to maintain. Protect and retain evidence according to the applicable requirements."), LessonSection("Trace objectives, not just filenames.", "Link evidence to the relevant requirement and assessment objective. Keep the implementation statement and assessment conclusion connected to the same scope.\n\nOne item may support several objectives, and one objective may need several items. Record the limits of each relationship."), LessonSection("Keep current and historical evidence distinct.", "When a system changes, identify which claims and records are affected. Keep version history so a reviewer can understand what was assessed at a particular time.\n\nA current filename on an old screenshot does not make the evidence current. Record collection and review dates separately when they mean different things."), LessonSection("A broken link in the package", "Index: EV-003 is listed as proof of current privileged event coverage.\n\nRecord: The export predates a new management host.\n\nGap: The record cannot support the updated coverage claim.\n\nAction: Collect the missing evidence and reconcile the SSP, source inventory, and assessment scope."), LessonSection("Build", "Create a master evidence index and link each selected control objective to the implementation, supporting record, owner, collection date, system scope, document version, and review status."), LessonSection("Prove", "Find stale evidence, a missing record, and a mismatch between the SSP and an exported configuration. Record the gap and its effect on the assessment.")], question: "A screenshot predates a major configuration change. What is the best next step?", answers: ["Rename it with today’s date.", "Leave it because the control number matches.", "Review its applicability and obtain current evidence for the changed scope."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Evidence must be relevant to the implementation and time period being assessed.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#catalog/07", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:08", title: "08 · Plan and perform assessment", minutes: 120, summary: "Turn implementation claims into assessable objectives and distinguish a planned test from an observed result.", sections: [LessonSection("Agree on what will be assessed.", "The assessment plan describes the objectives, scope, methods, objects, responsibilities, schedule, and practical constraints. Address the required assessor independence and approval path.\n\nSelect methods and coverage appropriate to the requirement and risk. Document sampling decisions instead of leaving them implicit."), LessonSection("Use examine, interview, and test deliberately.", "Examine records and configurations. Interview people responsible for the process. Test the capability or behavior when appropriate. These methods answer different questions and can complement one another.\n\nCapture the expected result, actual observation, evidence references, and limitations. Follow the adopted SP 800-53A procedures and authority-specific instructions."), LessonSection("Report the result the evidence supports.", "The Security Assessment Report (SAR) communicates what was assessed and the resulting findings. Connect each conclusion to the relevant objective, observed result, and supporting evidence.\n\nUnsupported objectives remain unresolved. Document remediation and retests without erasing the original finding or disguising a limitation as a successful result."), LessonSection("Test a privileged account-change event", "Plan: Specify the permitted event, relevant sources, expected record, and review process.\n\nObserve: Generate the event in the lab and inspect collection and review records.\n\nEvaluate: Compare observations to each selected objective.\n\nReport: Preserve the result, scope, evidence references, and any coverage gap."), LessonSection("Build", "Draft a Security Assessment Plan with objectives, methods, objects, scope, sampling rationale, responsibilities, and schedule. Execute sample procedures and prepare a Security Assessment Report (SAR) excerpt. Include negative tests: run the six attempts in the Inside the Network attack drill, record each expected and observed result in the Operational verification column of the network traffic matrix, and cite the matrix in your SAR excerpt."), LessonSection("Prove", "Link each conclusion to the assessment objective, evidence, actual observation, and limitation. Record unsupported objectives rather than treating a lack of evidence as a successful test.")], question: "A required assessment objective has no supporting evidence. Should it be marked satisfied?", answers: ["No. Record the gap and resolve it through the assessment process.", "Yes, if the owner expects it to work.", "Yes, if another control passed."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Assessment conclusions require support for the relevant objective.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#catalog/08", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:09", title: "09 · Manage findings and residual risk", minutes: 90, summary: "Translate weaknesses into mission-relevant risk and an accountable response. Explain the difference between technical severity and contextual risk.", sections: [LessonSection("Translate the weakness into mission risk.", "A finding identifies a weakness or unmet objective. Risk analysis considers the threat, exposure, consequences, existing safeguards, and remaining uncertainty in the system’s context.\n\nTechnical severity helps inform prioritization, but it is not the complete risk determination. Explain the mission impact and the reasoning behind the proposed response."), LessonSection("Write an action that can be managed.", "A Plan of Action and Milestones (POA&M) records the weakness, affected scope, responsible owner, planned actions, dates, resources, dependencies, and closure criteria.\n\n“Fix soon” is not a milestone. A useful milestone describes a verifiable action and a target date, with a clearly accountable owner."), LessonSection("Verify the correction before closing the record.", "Link the corrective action to the original finding. Retest the relevant condition and retain the result. Track delays and changed assumptions through the required review process.\n\nA POA&M is a management record; it does not itself accept risk or grant permission to operate. Those decisions follow the responsible authority’s process."), LessonSection("Make a logging finding actionable", "Weakness: SBD-MGMT01, a management host, is missing from verified audit collection.\n\nResponse: Restore approved collection and update source coverage records.\n\nMilestones: Confirm configuration, test the selected event, and verify the assigned review process.\n\nClosure: Retain the successful retest and reconcile the SSP, evidence index, and SAR disposition."), LessonSection("Build", "Create a risk analysis record and Plan of Action and Milestones (POA&M) entry with the weakness, affected assets and controls, owner, planned milestones, target dates, resources, status, and closure criteria."), LessonSection("Prove", "Prioritize two findings with different exposure and mission impact. Preserve the original result, document remediation, and require verification before claiming closure.")], question: "Does adding a finding to a POA&M mean its risk has been accepted?", answers: ["Yes, automatically.", "Only if the spreadsheet has a due date.", "No. Risk acceptance follows the authorized decision process."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Tracking a weakness and accepting its risk are different actions.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#catalog/09", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "rmf-authorization-to-operate", number: "2.3", title: "Authorization to Operate", subtitle: "Assemble, brief, and maintain the authorization package", category: "Network authorization", description: "Turn the network’s Body of Evidence into a reviewable Authorization to Operate (ATO) package. Assemble the submission, brief the Authorizing Official on supported conclusions and remaining risk, and maintain evidence after the decision. Complete the shared course with the package capstone.", icon: "risk", published: true, studyTime: "3 modules · 9 topics · 9 study hours", topics: 9,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-guide", title: "Interactive authorization course", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Lessons, evidence examples, and the capstone.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-word", title: "Full training catalog · Word", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Edition 1.3 · 12 modules", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/Secure_By_Design_RMF_Training_Catalog.docx", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-capstone", title: "Authorization package capstone", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Assemble, defend, and maintain the practice package.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#capstone", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-evidence-manifest", title: "Package manifest", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Organize the evidence and submission records", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#evidence/manifest", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:10", title: "10 · Assemble the authorization package", minutes: 120, summary: "Build a reviewable package whose claims, assessment results, and open risks agree across documents.", sections: [LessonSection("Assemble the decision-focused record.", "Bring together the executive summary, security and applicable privacy plans, assessment reports, POA&M, and required supporting material. Use a manifest to identify the exact versions under review.\n\nThe AO determines additional supporting information needed for the decision. Local formats differ, but the package must remain coherent and reviewable."), LessonSection("Check the relationships between documents.", "Compare the boundary, inventory, selected controls, implementation statements, findings, and open actions. Resolve inconsistent identifiers, status, dates, and scope.\n\nA package may be complete as a collection of files and still be unreliable. Readiness requires consistent claims supported by evidence."), LessonSection("Use the approved submission workflow.", "Follow the responsible authority’s system of record, required reviews, roles, signatures, and submission steps. In eMASS or another tool, the records still need the same substance.\n\nThis catalog uses a generic record workflow. Actual screens and submission permissions depend on the environment; no live government submission is part of the training."), LessonSection("A package consistency review", "SSP: Describes 22 named systems and 50 Windows 11 workstations.\n\nAssessment: Documents testing for 20 of the named systems and the workstations.\n\nReview: Determine whether the mismatch is a boundary error, an assessment limitation (the offline root CA has no network path to scan), or a recent change.\n\nResolve: Reconcile the records and explain the effect before requesting a decision."), LessonSection("Build", "Assemble an executive summary, security and applicable privacy planning material, assessment reports, POA&M, and required supporting references. Add a versioned manifest and review record."), LessonSection("Prove", "Reconcile control identifiers, asset counts, findings, dates, and status across the SSP, SCTM, SAR, and POA&M. Demonstrate a generic eMASS-style record workflow using training data.")], question: "The SSP and SAR list different assets. What should happen before submission?", answers: ["Submit both without explanation.", "Resolve or explicitly document the scope difference and its assessment impact.", "Delete the inventory from the package."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Reviewers need a coherent picture of the system and the scope actually assessed.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#catalog/10", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:11", title: "11 · Present the authorization decision", minutes: 90, summary: "Communicate mission need, the assessed security posture, unresolved risk, and proposed risk responses to the decision maker.", sections: [LessonSection("Brief what matters for the decision.", "Explain the system’s mission, assessed posture, significant unresolved risks, proposed responses, and any constraints. Keep the detailed evidence accessible behind the summary.\n\nA useful briefing distinguishes verified results from open questions. Avoid overwhelming the decision maker with raw tool output without explaining its significance."), LessonSection("Answer questions with an evidence trail.", "Be ready to explain who owns a risk, what evidence supports a conclusion, what remains unresolved, and what a proposed corrective action would change.\n\nA request for more evidence may lead to clarification, additional assessment, or remediation. Treat the review as part of the risk decision process."), LessonSection("Record the decision and its conditions.", "The AO issues the decision within their authority. Capture its scope, conditions, monitoring expectations, and review triggers in the required records.\n\nA favorable decision does not freeze the system or end monitoring. The team must understand the operating conditions and carry them into continuing work."), LessonSection("Brief an unresolved finding", "Mission: Describe what the system enables and why the capability is needed.\n\nRisk: Explain the effect of an unverified source of privileged activity.\n\nResponse: Present the planned corrective action, owner, verification approach, and remaining uncertainty.\n\nDecision: Support the AO’s evaluation; do not substitute a course score for authorization."), LessonSection("Build", "Prepare a five minute briefing and a fictional decision exercise. Capture the system scope, major risks, recommended actions, conditions, monitoring expectations, and next review triggers."), LessonSection("Prove", "Defend one unresolved finding and answer a request for additional evidence. Explain why a complete package or a passing score does not automatically produce an ATO.")], question: "Who makes the authorization decision?", answers: ["The product vendor.", "The course instructor.", "The responsible Authorizing Official."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The assessment informs the decision; the AO has the authorization responsibility.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#catalog/11", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:12", title: "12 · Monitor and maintain authorization", minutes: 90, summary: "Keep the authorization evidence useful as the system, threats, personnel, and dependencies change.", sections: [LessonSection("Choose measures that support continuing decisions.", "A monitoring strategy defines what is reviewed, who reviews it, how often, what evidence is retained, and which conditions require escalation. Align frequencies with the applicable direction and risk.\n\nMonitor implementation and effectiveness, not only tool availability. Track changes in threats, dependencies, and system use."), LessonSection("Evaluate the effect of a change.", "A new component, mission dependency, control-provider change, or incident may affect the prior assessment and authorization basis. Identify the affected controls and records.\n\nFollow the authority’s process to determine the needed reassessment, notifications, or authorization action. Not every change has the same consequence."), LessonSection("Keep the package synchronized.", "Update the inventory, architecture, SSP, traceability records, assessment results, and POA&M as appropriate. Preserve the relevant history and review approvals.\n\nContinuous monitoring keeps risk information useful. It does not mean every control is tested constantly or that a particular authorization remains valid regardless of change."), LessonSection("A new server enters the boundary", "Change: Document the service, owner, information flow, and approved configuration.\n\nImpact: Identify the affected inventory, control implementations, logging, scanning, backup, and dependencies.\n\nValidate: Collect relevant evidence and complete the required review.\n\nMaintain: Update package records and engage the designated authority when required."), LessonSection("Build", "Create a monitoring schedule with owners, evidence sources, review frequencies, escalation criteria, and update triggers. Add a change-impact record and an updated package version."), LessonSection("Prove", "Process a new server, a missed security update, and a changed common control. Determine what must be revalidated, what artifacts change, and when the AO or designated authority must be engaged.")], question: "A new service changes a documented information flow. What should the team do?", answers: ["Wait until the next major rewrite.", "Evaluate the change, update affected evidence, and follow the required review process.", "Assume the existing ATO covers every future service."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Changes must be evaluated against the assessed and authorized scope.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#catalog/12", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ])
];
